\subsection{Local measurement of irreversibility: the role of topological defects}\label{sec:epr_defects}

In the turbulent state, numerical measurements of the local IEPR $\dot{\sigma}$ [Eq.~\eqref{loc_epr}] show that it is typically larger at interfaces than in the bulks, and also higher in regions of large vorticity [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr}(a)]. Remarkably, the inhomogeneities in $\dot{\sigma}$ can be rationalized in terms of patterns and symmetries of topological defects of the nematic field $Q$ [Eq.~\eqref{eq:Q}]. In the next paragraphs, we show that our analytical predictions reproduce the IEPR profiles observed in the simulations.


For isolated defects, the symmetries of the local nematic orientation (two-fold and six-fold symmetries for $+\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\frac{1}{2}$ defects, respectively) clearly affect the profile of $\dot{\sigma}$ [Figs.~\ref{fig:epr}(b),(d)]. For a pair of $+\frac{1}{2}$ defects, $\dot\sigma$ is locally maximum in between the defects [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr}(f)]; for a pair of $+\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\frac{1}{2}$ defects, $\dot\sigma$ is locally higher close to the $+\frac{1}{2}$ defect [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr}(h)]. These observations suggest a route to predict the profile $\dot\sigma$ from the symmetries and relative orientations of defect pairs.


