Finally, we consider a symmetric configuration of a $(+\frac 1 2,-\frac 1 2)$ pair [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr_creation}(a)], and examine the effect of parametrically varying the separation distance $d$ at a fixed orientation $\Phi=0$. For each value of $d$, we introduce the integrated IEPR as
\begin{equation}\label{int_epr}
    \dot{\cal S}_R = \int_{D_R}\dot{\sigma}(\vb*{r})\, \mathrm{d}^2 \vb*{r} ,
\end{equation}
where $D_R$ is the disc of radius $R$ centered at the mid-point between defect cores. We observe that $\dot{\cal S}_R$ increases monotonically with $d$: it vanishes at $d=0$, and approaches the sum of contributions from isolated defects as $d$ increases, as expected [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr_creation}(b)]. In the turbulent state, the fluid is continuously stirred by the creation and annihilation of $(+\frac 1 2,-\frac 1 2)$~\cite{Giomi_2013}. It is tempting to interpret our symmetric configurations [Fig.~\ref{fig:epr_creation}(a)] as a time-series of snapshots during such a creation (namely, for increasing $d$) and annihilation (namely, for decreasing $d$). Our results suggest that the IEPR increases/decreases monotonically when two defects are brought further/closer infinitely slowly.


