%%%%% Results %%%%%%
Figure~\ref{fig:Fig_TS} shows representative $\mu$SR time spectra measured on the Pristine and NV sample at 290 and 20~K.
Zeeman energy applied by the longitudinal field decouples HF interactions, which results in the recovery of muon spin polarization.
To measure this decoupling behavior, the time spectra are integrated and averaged, and plotted as a function of LF.
Namely, the quantity,
$\lbrack \sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{N} A(t_i) \rbrack /N$,
has been calculated for each field, where, in the numerator, the normalized muon asymmetry $A$ measured at time $t_i$ are summed over all data bins from $i$ = 1 to $N$.
These so-called ``repolarization curves'' are shown in FIG.~\ref{fig:Fig_int}.
Since most of the muon spin polarization is lost at 0~G ($\sim$0.1), it is clear that there is fast dynamics in the system, which depolarizes Mu in their triplet state (with its total spin $m_F$ = +1).
This fast depolarization appears to be too fast to observe at pulsed sources.
The signals are then quickly recovered by the field up to 400~G, followed by the slower repolarization that continues to 4000~G.
Based on the previous studies~\cite{Cox_Rev, Connell}, Mu$_\text{BC}^0$ and Mu$_\text{T}^0$ can be associated with the repolarization in the low and high field respectively.
Clearly, the repolarization behavior in the high-field range is dependent on the sample and temperature, presumably reflecting changes in how Mu$_\text{T}^0$ interacts with the host system.
However, in order to fully understand physics behind the curves, we need to simulate the $\mu$SR time spectra with a model.

We start with discussing data from the Pristine sample, where a single point defect, N$_\text{s}^0$, is expected to dominate the interaction with Mu.
To build a model, we simply assumed that
Mu$_\text{T}^0$ and Mu$_\text{BC}^0$ are the only active Mu states in the system, and
the rest is diamagnetic giving a constant background.
Indeed, part of the diamagnetic component must come from muons that missed the sample and were implanted in the silver materials.
This model is shown in FIG.~\ref{fig:Fig_Mu_diagrams}(a).
Here, there are site exchange interactions, where Mu$_\text{T}^0$ can be spontaneously converted to Mu$_\text{BC}^0$ and vice versa.
Each transition process can be characterized with an exchange rate $\Lambda$.
In addition, Mu$_\text{T}^0$ and Mu$_\text{BC}^0$ can exchange their electron's spin with the bath with rates, $\Lambda_\text{T}^0$ and $\Lambda_\text{BC}^0$, respectively.
The diamagnetic component is part of the simulation but assumed only to give a time-independent background.

%!!20250703: somewhere in this paragraph, mention how the orientation averaging been taken account!!%
The simulation was carried out using QUANTUM, a Python program to solve the time evolution of the muon spin using the density matrix method~\cite{Lord1, Lord2}.
The program generates a full Hamiltonian for a given system and diagonalizes it to obtain eigenvectors and eigenstates.
The density matrix evolves with time, which can then be used to calculate the muon spin evolution~\cite{Chow_Rev}.
For a system with spin flips and state changes, the program calculates a matrix describing the evolution of the density matrix.
State transitions are assumed to take place randomly with a uniform rate.
A representative source code and details of the curve fitting procedure are provided in the Supplemental Material~\cite{SM}.
In the code, $f(\text{Mu}_\text{T}^0)$ and $f(\text{Mu}_\text{BC}^0)$ are independent variables, but the diamagnetic component is given by $1 - f(\text{Mu}_\text{T}^0) - f(\text{Mu}_\text{BC}^0)$.
In Mu$_{\text{T}}^0$ and Mu$_{\text{BC}}^0$, their muon spin interacts with the bound electron via the HF constants.
In the QUANTUM software, A$_{\parallel}$ and A$_{\perp}$ for Mu$_{\text{BC}}^0$ are decomposed into
an isotropic ($\text{A}_{\text{iso}}$) and dipolar (D) term, given by:
$\text{A}_{\text{iso}} = (\text{A}_{\parallel} + 2\text{A}_{\perp})/3$ and 
$\text{D} = 2(\text{A}_{\parallel} - \text{A}_{\perp})/3$.
In diamond, Mu interaction with nearby nuclear spins is negligibly small because there is no nuclear spin in $^{12}$C nuclei, which take up $\sim$99~\% of naturally occurring isotopes.
Hence, system's interaction with the bath takes place only via the electron spin relaxation and state transitions.
Note that the diffusive motion of Mu$_{\text{T}}^0$ is not part of the model;
however, the electronic spin relaxation and state transition rates can carry contribution from the hopping motion (see below).
The modeling takes account of the Zeeman interaction as a fixed parameter, which was set by the instrument's LF magnets.
Spin repolarization by the Zeeman effect plays a key role in differentiating the HF interactions.
After a calculation, the program returns a normalized time domain signal, which can be compared with the measured $\mu$SR spectrum after normalization with the instrument's full muon asymmetry.
Global curve fitting on {\it all} of $\mu$SR time spectra in a LF data set enables us to narrow the model down to a single candidate.

The good-quality fitting, as shown in FIG.~\ref{fig:Fig_TS}(a) and (b), supports the model in FIG.~\ref{fig:Fig_Mu_diagrams}(a) as a good candidate for the Pristine sample.
This agreement can also be seen in calculated repolarization curves as shown in FIG.~\ref{fig:Fig_int}(c), where the simulated time domain signal have been integrated and averaged.
Here, the data points for 290 and 20~K are broadly similar.
This observation is reflected in the obtained fit parameters, as summarized in TABLE~\ref{table:fit_results},
where we can draw a few physical insights.
%
First, prompt Mu yields, $f(\text{Mu}_\text{T}^0)$ $\approx$ 60~\% and $f(\text{Mu}_\text{BC}^0)$ $\approx$ 30~\%, were obtained at both temperatures.
This result agrees with the reported values~\cite{Connell}.
The negligibly small difference between 290 and 20~K may suggest that the Mu yields stay constant in this temperature range.
It is no wonder that these fractions stayed roughly the same in the NV sample because the process of NV generation cannot change bulk properties of the host crystal.

