\section{Introduction}


\subsection{Overview}
After Margulis' celebrated superrigidity theorems on lattices of higher rank followed up with Zimmer's superrigidity for cocycles (\cite{margulis1991discrete},\cite{Zimmer}), the phenomenon was extended to other frameworks by considering the target group to be the isometry group of a space having nonpositive curvature features (see, for instance, \cite{Monod},\cite{Monod-Shalom},\cite{Shalom},\cite{BaderFurman}). In this paper, we consider the superrigidity of cocycles for a product of locally compact second countable groups, and the target group is the isometry group of a complete separable irreducible median space of finite rank. The argument when applied to the case of action of irreducible lattices in a product of locally compact second countable groups on such median spaces gives rise to a new proof of known superrigidity in this context. \par
Median spaces (algebras) first appeared in the mid of the 20th in the context of distributive lattices and Boolean algebras. They raised again in the beginning of the 21th in the area of geometric group theory as median spaces give a common framework for the study of real trees and CAT(0) cube complexes, as well as the geometric characterization of Kazhdan's property (T) and Haagerup property. Particular examples of median spaces of finite rank include, for instance, $\mathbb{R}$-trees, zero-skeleton of finite dimensional CAT(0) cube complexes endowed with the combinatorial metric, asymptotic cones over coarse median space of finite rank such as hyperbolic group, Mapping class group, Teichmuller space, etc (see \cite{ChaDH_median}, \cite{ChaD_hyperbolic}, \cite{Fior_tits},\cite{Fior_median_property},\cite{Fior_superrigidity},\cite{Fior_auto}, \cite{Bowd_conv},\cite{Bowd_median-algebras}, \cite{Genevois}, \cite{Messaci} for different results and properties of the median geometry).\par 
\par Our approach towards the superrigidity results is based on the nonpositive curvature features of group actions on median spaces of finite rank. As there is a vast literature on superrigidity of cocycles and homomorphisms, we only include those that are closely related to our setting here.\par

Regarding the superrigidity of homomorphism for irreducible lattices in product groups, we cite the following results.
\begin{itemize}
\item In \cite{Shalom}, this was shown for affine isometric actions on Hilbert spaces using the induction of action approach and a splitting result for the first reduced cohomology class. 
 \item In \cite{Monod}, it was shown in the case of isometry groups of CAT(0) spaces, by proving a splitting result for the induced action of $G$ on the space of $L^1$-integrable sections from $G/\Gamma$ into the CAT(0) space. 
 \item In \cite{Gelander-Karlsson-Margulis}, the same result was shown for the $L^1$-integrable lattices and when the target group is the isometry group of a Busemann negatively curved space. The proof also relies on a splitting result of the induced $G$-action but the argument uses on a harmonic approach and a generalized Eells–Sampson's type existence theorem.
 \item In \cite{ChaIF}, it was proven for automorphism groups of CAT(0) cube complexes and the argument relies on the boundary theory and non vanishing of a bounded cohomology class that encode the action in the same vein as \cite{Monod-Shalom}, which however does not require the lattice to be square integrable.
 \item In \cite{Fior_superrigidity}, it was shown for the isometry group of median space of finite rank by characterizing such action by a reduced cohomology class and exploiting Shalom's splitting result to prove the superrigidity result.
\end{itemize}
The approach considered in \cite{Shalom}, \cite{Monod},\cite{Gelander-Karlsson-Margulis} and \cite{Fior_superrigidity} consists of considering the induction of the action (explicitly or implicitly). Note that for such an induced $G$-action to be defined, the lattice needs to be $L^1$- integrable. This is in contrast with the dynamical approach as in \cite{Bader-Furman-Weyl_group}, \cite{BCFS}, \cite{BaderFurman} where the $L^1$-integrability of lattice assumption is no longer needed.\par 
However, relative few are known on superrigidity of cocycles. In \cite{Monod-Shalom}, the authors proved the superrigidity of cocycles for a product of locally compact second countable groups and the target group being the isometry group of a locally compact negatively curved metric space. In their paper, the superrigidity result is based on a bounded cohomology argument and a splitting result \cite{Burger-Monod}. In \cite{Bader-Furman-Weyl_group}, the authors provided an approach based on the notion of generalized Weyl groups. In \cite{Guirardel-Horbez-Lecureux}, the latter approach was used to prove the superrigidity of cocycle when the target group is the outer automorphism of a hyperbolic group. %The same approach approach was considered in \cite{BCFS} when the target group is the isometry group of a (not necessarily proper) hyperbolic space.
It is the same approach that we adapt to the median case in the present paper.\par 


\subsection{Statement of the results}

Let $G=G_1\times G_2$ be a product of second countable, locally compact, non compact groups, and let $\Omega$ be a standard probability space on which each $G_i$ acts ergodically by measure-preserving action. The reader is referred to Section \ref{section_strongboundaries_cocycles} for cocycles.
 \begin{thm}[Theorem \ref{theoerem_superrigidity_cocycle}]\label{thm_intro_cocycles}
 Let $X$ be a complete separable irreducible median space of finite rank and $\alpha:G\times \Omega \rightarrow Isom(X)$ a non-elementary cocycle. 
 Then, there exists an $Isom(X)$-invariant median subspace $Y\subseteq X$ such that the cocycle $\alpha$ is cohomologous to a group homomorphism $h:G\rightarrow Isom(Y)$ that factors through the projection onto one factor $G_i$.
 \end{thm}\par 
 We note that the superrigidity of cocycles is new even when the target group is the automorphism group of a finite dimensional CAT(0) cube complex.\par 
 We have two remarks. Firstly, the $Isom(X)$-invariant median subspace $Y$ arising in Theorem \ref{thm_intro_cocycles}, that we refer to as the \textbf{\textit{median core}}, has the property that the restriction of any isometry of $X$ on $Y$ is uniquely determined by its restriction on its boundary. In general, a homomorphism from $G$ to $Isom(Y)$ cannot be lifted to $Isom(X)$ (see Example 4.7 \cite{Fior_superrigidity}). Secondly, The median core $Y$ is in general not convex, hence its existence does not contradict the Roller minimality assumption.\par

 Using the same approach in the particular context of the superrigidity of homomorphism of lattices, this gives rise to a new argument that does not rely on the non vanishing of a bounded cohomology or induced cohomology class. It recovers Fioravanti's superrigidity result for actions on median spaces of finite rank (\cite{Fior_superrigidity}) and extends it to the class of non square-integrable lattices.
 \begin{thm}[Theorem \ref{thm_superrigidity_principal}]\label{thm_intro_homo}
 Let $\Gamma\leq G_1\times G_2$ be an irreducible lattice in a product of locally compact second countable groups. If $\Gamma$ acts Roller minimally and Roller non-elementarily on a complete separable irreducible median space of finite rank, there exists an $Isom(X)$-invariant median subspace $Y$ on which the $\Gamma$-action extends to $G$ and factors through one of the $G_i$'s.
 \end{thm}

 The main step in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm_superrigidity_principal} is the proof of the existence and uniqueness of a boundary map from the $G$-boundary to the regular boundary $\partial_r X$ of the median space $X$, that is the part of the Roller boundary that encodes the tree-like part of the median space.

 \begin{thm}[Theorem \ref{thm_image_in_regular_boundary}]
 Let $G$ be a locally compact second countable group acting Roller non elementarilly and Roller minimally on an irreducible finite rank median space. Then there exists a unique $G$-equivariant measurable map $\phi:B\rightarrow \partial_r X$.
 \end{thm}
For results on boundary maps in the median setting, see \cite{ChaIF},\cite{Fernos}, \cite{Sageev-Nevo} and \cite{Bader-Taller}.
 The regular boundary $\partial_r X$ can be defined as the limit set of a particular set of contracting isometries. These are isometries whose axes of translation skewer through two strongly separated halfspaces, where two halfspaces are said to be strongly separated if there is no halfspace that is transverse to both. The existence of such elements is ensured under the mild assumptions that the median space is irreducible and the isometry group acts Roller non elementarily and Roller minimally (see Definitions \ref{def_Roller_minimal}).

 \par 

 To construct the boundary map, we adapt to the median case the approach given in \cite{ChaIF} which relies on the halfspaces to associate a ``center of mass" to each measure. In their approach, to prove the measurability of some key maps, they relied on the fact that the space of halfspaces of the cube complex is countable. To remedy the lack of discreteness of the median space, we prove the existence of a countable dense subset of halfspaces that is invariant under the whole group of isometries.
\begin{thm}\label{thm_fundamental_family_introduction}[Theorem \ref{thm_countable_medians_subspace}]
Let $X$ be a complete separable irreducible median space of finite rank. If $Isom(X)$ acts Roller non elementarily and Roller minimally, there exists an $Isom(X)$ invariant countable set of halfspaces that separate any pair of points of the median core.

%that is dense in the sense that any pair of distinct points in the Roller compactification of $X$ is separated by a halfspace in the family.
\end{thm}

The theorem above is very practical for dealing with the measurability question regarding maps and subsets in the Roller compactification.

\subsection{Outline of the proof}
The proof of Theorem \ref{thm_intro_cocycles} and Theorem \ref{thm_intro_homo} follows almost the same lines, the paper is organized to first prove Theorem \ref{thm_intro_homo} and then Theorem \ref{thm_intro_cocycles} by pointing out minor changes. It has the advantage that no background on cocycles is needed to understand the main ideas. We now present an outline of the proof.

\paragraph{Superrigidity of homomorphisms (Section \ref{section_superrigidity_of_homomorphisms}).}
Let $\Gamma\leq G_1\times G_2$ be an irreducible lattice in a product of locally compact groups and let $X$ be a complete, irreducible, separable median space of finite rank. Assume that $\Gamma$ acts Roller non elementarily and Roller minimally on $X$. For a fixed $G$-boundary $B$, the first step is to construct a $G$-equivariant map $\phi:B\rightarrow \bar X$ (Theorem \ref{thm_contruction_boundary_map}). To do so, one first starts from the $G$-equivariant map $\Phi:B\rightarrow \PP(\bar X)$, where $\PP(\bar X)$ is the space of probability measures over the Roller compactification $\bar X$. Such $G$-equivariant map is ensured by the amenability of the action $G\curvearrowright B$. For each probability measure $\mu\in \PP(\bar X)$ one associates a center of mass by considering the intersection of all closed halfspaces having measure bigger than $\frac{1}{2}$ (see Section \ref{section_barycenter_map}). Using the ergodicity of the diagonal $G$-action on $B\times B$, one deduces that for almost all $b\in B$, the center of mass of the corresponding measure is a singleton (Proposition \ref{prop_balanced_halfspaces_zero_measure}). Thus, the map $\Phi$ descends to a measurable map $\phi: B\rightarrow \bar X$.\par 
The second step consists of showing that the map $\phi$ has an image essentially in the regular boundary (Theorem \ref{thm_image_in_regular_boundary}). To do so, one uses again the double ergodicty of the action $G\curvearrowright B\times B$ to deduce that for almost all pair $b_1,b_2$ their images under $\phi$ are separated by an arbitrarily large set of strongly separated hyperplanes. The regular boundary has the property that for any triple of pairwise distinct points, their median point lies in $X$.\par 
 Having these data in hand and borrowing an argument from Bader-Furman \cite{BaderFurman}, one shows that any $G$-equivariant map from $B\times B$ into $\partial_r X$ depends on one variable and is mapped through $\phi$ (Theorem \ref{thm_unicity_of_boundary_map_product}). Restricting to the situation where the group is an irreducible lattice in a product of locally compact second countable groups, one has $B=B_1\times B_2$ where $B_i$ is a $G_i$-boundary. In this case, the product $B\times B$ contains a non ``trivial" automorphism that commute with the action (the generalized Weyl group introduced in \cite{Bader-Furman-Weyl_group} is bigger than $\ZZ/2\ZZ$). Combining this with the proven fact that any map from $B\times B$ to $\partial_r X$ depends on one variable, this imposes the boundary map $\phi$ to depend only on one factor $B_i$ (Theorem \ref{thm_factor_boundary_map}). Hence, one obtains a $G_i$-action on $\phi(B)\subseteq \partial_r X$ extending the $\Gamma$-action. The last step is to show that the $G_i$-action extends continuously to the median closure of $\phi(b)$ inside $X$, using the median ternary operation and the density of the projection of $\Gamma$ in $G_i$ (Theorem \ref{thm_superrigidity_principal}).

 \paragraph{Superrigidity of cocycles (Section \ref{section_superrigidity_of_cocycles}).}

 Let $G=G_1\times G_2$ be a product of second countable locally compact groups, and let $\Omega$ be a standard probability space on which each $G_i$ acts ergodically with measure-preserving transformations. Let $X$ be a complete separable irreducible median space of finite rank and let $\alpha:G\times \Omega \rightarrow Isom(X) $ be a non elementary cocycle (see Section \ref{section_strongboundaries_cocycles}). The cocycle defines a natural $G$-action on the space of class of measurable sections from $\Omega$ to the median core $Y$. This space has a natural structure of a median algebra, where the median operation is defined pointwise. The goal, inspired by \cite{bader2006superrigidity}, is to prove the existence of a median subalgebra which, when endowed with the $L^1$-metric, is a median space isometric to $Y$ (the median closure of the regular boundary) and which is $G$-invariant with one of the factors $G_i$ acting trivially (say $G_2$). \par 
 This median subspace consists of class of $\alpha_{G_2}=\alpha|_{G_2}$-equivariant sections from $\Omega$ to $Y$. To prove the existence of such $\alpha_{G_2}$-equivariant sections, we start with an $\alpha$-equivariant section $\Phi:\Omega\times B\rightarrow \PP(\bar X)$. Adapting the same arguments used in the superrigidity of homomorphisms to the case of cocycle, we show that the map $\Phi$ descends to an $\alpha$-equivariant map $\phi:\Omega\times B\rightarrow \partial_r X$ that factors through the projection into $\Omega\times B_1$ (Proposition \ref{prop_boundary_map_cocycle}, Proposition \ref{prop_boundary_map_factors}). Almost all fixed $b_1\in B_1$ gives rise to an $\alpha_{G_2}$-equivariant section from $\Omega$ into $\partial_r X$. After composing such sections with the ternary operation, one obtains infinitely many $\alpha_{G_2}$-equivariant sections from $\Omega$ to $Y$, denoted by $\YY$. The final step is then to show that such a 
 subspace is isometric to $Y$ (Theorem \ref{theoerem_superrigidity_cocycle}), using the ergodicity of the $G_i$-actions on $\Omega$.


