\medskip

\noindent \textbf{2.}~ Under the U.S. Military Academy matching process to military branches used in 2006, each cadet (i) ranked the branches and (ii) stated, for every branch, whether they would accept a longer service obligation in exchange for a priority boost \citep{sonmez2013matching}. This elicitation did not allow them to report \emph{cross-branch} trade-offs. Omitting this information let the Academy honour the official order-of-merit list while making it possible to steer more cadets into longer commitments that would have been rejected under full preference elicitation. As \citet{sonmezMinimalistMarketDesign2024} explains, this design kept such priority violations hidden:

\begin{quote}
“Several years later, in 2019, I finally learned why the Army initially did not pursue a potential reform of the USMA-2006 mechanism. (...) Any failure of the no-justified-envy axiom rooted in this first issue was also ‘invisible’ to the Army. When a cadet receives his first-choice branch at the increased price but prefers his second choice at the base price, this information was simply unavailable under the strategy space of the USMA-2006 mechanism.”
\end{quote}

\medskip

\noindent \textbf{3.}~In the Chinese college admissions system, applicants submit a structured rank-order list in which majors are nested under colleges, effectively enforcing a lexicographic hierarchy: once a college is deemed higher-ranked, every major it offers is treated as strictly preferred to any program at a lower-ranked college. Although this structure is known to generate numerous cases of justified envy in practice, none of these can be challenged under the restricted message space \citep[see][]{huchinesecolleges}.\footnote{As of January 2025, 23 out of 31 provinces in China retain the nested rank-order procedure \citep{huchinesecolleges}, including Shanghai, Beijing, Tianjin, Hainan, Jiangsu, Fujian, Hubei, Hunan, Guangdong, Heilongjiang, Gansu, Jilin, Anhui, Jiangxi, Guangxi, Shanxi, Henan, Shaanxi, Ningxia, Sichuan, Yunnan, Tibet, and Xinjiang.  We provide screenshots from Fujian and Shanghai's official college-major list sample form in Appendix B.}

\medskip

As the preceding cases show, limiting what participants may report can hide genuine priority breaches. We say a mechanism is \textbf{visibly fair} when, given the elicited (partial) preferences, no outcome appears to violate priority. For example, if seats are partitioned into zones and agents may only rank seats \emph{within} each zone, allocation by global priority inside every zone looks perfectly fair—even though a cross-zone comparison (never elicited) might reveal a lower-ranked agent holding a seat a higher-ranked agent prefers. By restricting the scope of reported preferences, designers can pursue goals such as geographic diversity while keeping any latent violations invisible.

For example, if positions are partitioned into zones and agents may only rank positions \emph{within} each zone, an allocation by priority inside every zone looks perfectly fair—even though a cross-zone comparison (never elicited) might reveal a lower-ranked agent holding a seat a higher-ranked agent prefers. By restricting the scope of reported preferences, designers can pursue goals such as geographic diversity while keeping any underlying violations of priorities invisible.

Inspired by these observations, \emph{we examine the design problem of assigning participants to seats under a strict priority ordering while maintaining visible fairness}. In contrast to standard models that fix a preference-reporting format, in this framework \textbf{the designer chooses both the message space} (the form of partial preferences agents can report) \textbf{and the outcome rule}. Our analysis provides a framework and results on how to configure these two elements together so as to achieve desired policy objectives.


\subsection*{Summary of Results}\label{subsec:summary-results}

Our analysis delivers three main sets of results.
First, we pin down the precise structure that visible fairness imposes on allocation rules.  \Cref{thm:CharacterizationVisiblyFairMechanisms} shows that any visibly–fair mechanism must operate as an \emph{$m$-queue allocation}: officers are processed in strict priority order, and each is assigned a state that is undominated within the partial ranking she is allowed to report.  When the message space is zonal, this characterization specializes in \Cref{thm:CharacterizationIncontestableMechanisms2,thm:CharacterizationIncontestableMechanisms3}: the only visibly–fair rules are partitioned (and, with cross-zone rankings, ranked–partitioned) priority mechanisms. While visible fairness implies serial dictatorship (and therefore strategy-proofness) when using direct mechanisms (\Cref{coro:Truthful1}), that is not the case for general message spaces. We we show in \Cref{thm:Truthful1} that strategy-proofness is obtained exactly when the mechanism also satisfies \emph{expressiveness} and (weak) \emph{availability}, two properties that rule out profitable deviations when the mapping from message profiles to outcomes are more general.

Second, we introduce a flexible way to encode distributional goals through \emph{modular upper-bounds}.  A quota system is modular when every bound caps a single type within an arbitrary subset of states (\Cref{def:ModularUpperBounds}).  Modular bounds induce a natural partition of states—formally captured in \Cref{def:modularInducedMessageSpaces}—that yields a message space where all states subject to the same collection of caps fall in the same ``zone.''  Building on this structure, the \emph{Modular Priority Mechanism} (\Cref{def:modularPriorityMechanism}) processes officers by priority while dynamically blocking zones whose relevant caps have just filled.  \Cref{modulartheorem} proves that this mechanism simultaneously respects every modular bound, remains visibly fair, and is strategy-proof.  

Yet, as \Cref{ex:motivation_formal} and the impossibility result in \Cref{thm:NoMechanismAllThree} show, no \emph{static} (one-shot) rule can simultaneously (i) satisfy visible fairness, (ii) respect modular upper-bounds, and (iii) not be Pareto-improved by quota-feasible allocations.
The root of the conflict is informational.
To guarantee that \emph{every} admissible report keeps the quotas intact, the designer must prune the message space in advance, excluding comparisons whose truthful revelation could otherwise force a violation.
This secures fairness and feasibility but withholds preference information that would uncover (and execute) mutually beneficial swaps still compatible with the same caps.

Finally, we show that this tension can be resolved using a dynamic mechanism.  The \emph{Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism} (\Cref{def:DynamicModularPriorityMech}) re-elicits each officer’s preferences after observing earlier assignments, restricting her menu only by the quotas that are now binding.  This simple refinement recovers constrained Pareto efficiency, while being strategy-proof (\Cref{dynamicModPriorityMechVisiblyFairModularAndSP}).  


\subsection*{Related Literature}

A foundational theory for mechanisms with restricted message spaces comes from \cite{greenPartiallyVerifiableInformation1986},  who study settings where agents are restricted to a limited message space that depends on their true state. Their key insight is that by constraining the information an agent can reveal, the set of implementable outcomes can be expanded beyond what is possible in standard direct mechanisms. We deliberately design message spaces to elicit only partial preferences and make certain comparisons impossible, enabling the achievement of distributional goals that would otherwise conflict with fairness claims. 

Beyond the already discussed cases of the Indian Administrative Service (IAS) cadre allocation \citep{thakur2021matching}, the U.S.\ Military Academy’s cadet-branch matching \citep{sonmez2013matching,sonmez2013bidding}, and Chinese college admissions \citep{huchinesecolleges}, many real-world mechanisms also limit the extent of preference reporting. In school choice, for example, some systems cap the number of schools an applicant may rank \citep{haeringer2009constrained, calsamigliaConstrainedSchoolChoice2010}, while others allow students to “bundle” schools into groups without inter-group comparisons \citep{huang2025bundled}. 

Dynamic procedures also restrict preferences and by doing so, might reduce complexity \citep{pyciaTheorySimplicityGames2023}. \cite{boIterativeDeferredAcceptance2022} propose Iterative Deferred Acceptance by letting agents choose from menus, obtaining stable results without demanding complete rankings, and \cite{boPickanObjectMechanisms2024,mackenzieMenuMechanisms2022} extend this idea to mechanisms that sequentially offer feasible outcomes—enhancing privacy and performing well in controlled experiments. Even small constraints, like limiting the length of rank-ordered lists, can disrupt classical incentive properties: \cite{haeringerConstrainedSchoolChoice2009} show how capping the number of ranked schools compromises the usual strategy-proofness of the Deferred Acceptance procedure. Meanwhile, \cite{Caspari2024} propose precise conditions for stability and incentives with non-standard preference formats. Collectively, these studies highlight how restricting preference articulation can open new design possibilities while preserving key desiderata—an insight we leverage in defining and deploying \emph{visible fairness}.

\cite{decerfIncontestableAssignments2024} is perhaps the most conceptually related work to ours. Their notion of \emph{incontestable assignments} describes an environment where students cannot fully observe others’ preferences or placements, leaving them unable to identify certain envy issues. This parallels our concept of \emph{visible fairness}, in which certain violations become undetectable. The key difference, however, is that \cite{decerfIncontestableAssignments2024} derive their informational constraints from the students’ limited ability to view the full outcome, whereas in our framework, these constraints are intentionally \emph{designed} by the mechanism planner. Specifically, in our setup the designer restricts what agents can report so as to preclude distributional tensions that might otherwise manifest as visible grievances. In addition, while \cite{decerfIncontestableAssignments2024} accommodate a variety of school-specific priorities, our model considers a single, strict priority ranking that orders all participants.

Partial preferences has been used in other contexts as well in market design. 
One strand lets participants declare indifference classes directly: \cite{ERDIL2017268,manjunathStrategyproofExchangeTrichotomous2021,anderssonOrganizingTimeExchanges2021} build mechanisms that treat weak orders—strict ranks punctuated by ties—as the primitives, and then exploit those ties to recover efficiency and strategy‐proofness. A second strand considers problems in which not every pair of outcomes can be compared, evaluating which adaptation of standard properties, such as stability, can nonetheless sustain strategy-proofness under suitable conditions \citep{Caspari2024,kuvalekarMatchingIncompletePreferences2023}. Typically, these frameworks rely on “weak stability,” where agents who are indifferent or indecisive simply cannot block assignments. In contrast, our approach presumes agents \emph{do} have complete preferences but are deliberately constrained from revealing them in full.

The design of matching markets with distributional constraints through quota systems has emerged as a critical area of research in market design, balancing equity objectives with efficiency and stability considerations \citep{echeniqueHowControlControlled2015, abdulkadiroglu2023market}.\footnote{Practical implementations in education markets reveal both the potential and complexity of quota systems. \cite{combe2022design} quantified these trade-offs through France’s teacher assignment reforms, where experience-based distribution constraints reduced novice teacher concentrations in disadvantaged schools by 18\% without significant efficiency losses. \cite{combe2022market} extended this through a reassignment algorithm that prioritized understaffed schools, demonstrating how temporal flexibility in constraints (allowing multi-year adjustment periods) could mitigate short-term displacement costs. } \cite{kamadaEfficientMatchingDistributional2015} introduced the idea of matching with distributional constraints, showing that conventional stable matching algorithms can break down under strict regional quotas. To address these deficiencies, they introduced new mechanisms that ensure such constraints are respected while preserving or improving upon stability, efficiency, and incentive alignment. Subsequent research refines and generalizes these insights: for instance, \cite{kamadaStabilityStrategyproofnessMatching2018} identify structural conditions enabling strategy-proof and stable mechanisms under distributional constraints, and their more recent work \citep{kamadaFairMatchingConstraints2024} extends stability ideas to increasingly nuanced affirmative action policies.\footnote{Additional contributions include \cite{azizMatchingDiversityConstraints2020}, who introduce the principle of “cutoff stability” for diversity-constrained matching, and \cite{kojimaJobMatchingConstraints2020}, who identify conditions ensuring that distributional constraints do not undermine substitutability in job-matching markets.} 

At the core of this field lies the tension between rigid distributional quotas and the flexible preferences of market participants. \cite{fragiadakis2017improving} demonstrated this through military cadet matching, where static reservation systems created inefficiencies by locking seats for specific groups prematurely. Their dynamic quota mechanism represented a paradigm shift, adjusting reservation targets based on revealed preferences while maintaining strategy-proofness. This approach inspired subsequent innovations like the Adaptive Deferred Acceptance (ADA) mechanism by \cite{gotoDesigningMatchingMechanisms2017}, which introduced hereditary constraints—rules where satisfying a constraint automatically satisfies all its subsets. The ADA mechanism’s success in Japanese medical residency matching showed that carefully designed constraints need not sacrifice core market principles like nonwastefulness and strategy-proofness. 

We add to this literature by demonstrating how appropriately crafted message spaces—which limit how agents can express their preferences—allow mechanism designers to achieve distributional objectives while preserving fairness and incentive properties.

A significant direction for future research is to design message spaces that can accommodate floor constraints, where balancing stability and efficiency remains a central theoretical challenge. \cite{tomoeda2018finding} established mathematical conditions where type-specific minimum quotas could coexist with stable matchings, requiring that institutions’ preferences satisfy substitutability across types—a condition frequently violated in practice. \cite{ehlers2014school} proposed softening this tension through probabilistic constraints, where schools could satisfy diversity targets over multiple matching cycles rather than single instances. This ``soft bounds" approach proved particularly valuable in school choice contexts, where rigid racial or socioeconomic quotas often face legal challenges.  \cite{akin2021matching} introduced a stability concept accommodating strict floor constraints, along with a mechanism that is strategy-proof for doctors and results in a stable matching. 







\bigskip

\noindent \textbf{Structure of the paper.}
In Section 2, we introduce the model and definitions, covering partial preferences, feasible allocations, and our message-space framework. Section 3 then characterizes visibly fair mechanisms, identifying them as queue-allocation variants and examining applications such as zonal message spaces. Section 4 turns to incentives, specifying exactly when these mechanisms are strategy-proof via conditions of expressiveness and availability. Sections 5 and 6 present our central results on distributional objectives, including modular upper-bounds and the Modular Priority Mechanism, alongside an analysis of efficiency that leads to our Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism. Section 7 concludes. Omitted proofs are relegated to Appendix A. 



\section{Model and Definitions}
A problem consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item a finite set of \textbf{officers} $I=\{i_1,i_2,\ldots\}$,
    \item a finite set of \textbf{states} $S=\{s_1,s_2,\ldots\}$,
    \item a \textbf{capacity} for each state $(q_s)_{s\in S}$, such that $\sum_{s\in S} q_s\geq |I|$, 
    \item a strict and complete \textbf{preference} for each officer $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$ over states $S$, and
    \item a \textbf{priority} ranking $\pi$ of officers $I$, where officer $i$ is ranked higher than officer $j$ if $\pi(i) < \pi(j)$.
\end{enumerate}

For a given problem, the goal is to produce an allocation of officers to states.  
Formally, an \textbf{allocation} $a=(a_i)_{i\in I}$ is a list specifying a state $a_i \in S$ for each officer $i\in I$.  
An allocation is \textbf{feasible} if, for each $s\in S$, we have $|\{ i\in I: a_i=s\}|\leq q_s$. We denote the set of all feasible allocations by $A$.

While the allocation decision is typically based on officers' reported preferences and assigned priorities, in our setup, officers do not necessarily communicate their full preferences directly but instead provide partial preference information from a menu of partial preferences available. 


More specifically, for each officer $i \in I$, let $M_i$ denote the \emph{message space} for $i$. For every message $m_i\in M_i$, let $\succ_{m_i}$ denote the strict partial
order it reports:
\[
  s\succ_{m_i} s'
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
  (s,s')\in m_i.
\]
Throughout, “$\succ_{m_i}$” will be read as “preferred under the message $m_i$.” Each message $m_i\in M_i$ consists of a \emph{strict partial order} over the set of states~$S$; that is, $m_i\subseteq S\times S$ is\footnote{A binary relation $R$ on $S$ is a \emph{strict partial order} if it is \emph{irreflexive} ($\lnot(sRs)$ for every $s$) and \emph{transitive} ($sRs'\land s'Rs''\Rightarrow sRs''$).} irreflexive and transitive. We say that states $s$ and $s'$ are \textbf{comparable} under $M_i$ if, for every $m_i\in M_i$, either $s \succ_{m_i} s'$ or $s' \succ_{m_i} s$. Moreover, for any $m_i\in M_i$, we denote by  $\succsim_{m_i}$ the associated weak preference---that is, $s\succsim_{m_i}s'\iff s\succ_{m_i}s'\text{ or }s=s'$.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:richness}
A message space $M_i$ satisfies \textbf{richness} if, whenever there exists a message $m_i \in M_i$ and two states $s, s' \in S$ such that $s\,\succ_{m_i}\,s'$, there also exists a message $m_i' \in M_i$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item for every pair $(s_1,s_2) \in S\times S$ with $\{s_1,s_2\} \neq \{s,s'\}$, $s_1\,\succ_{m_i}\,s_2$ if and only if $s_1\,\succ_{m_i'}\,s_2$, and
    \item $s'\,\succ_{m_i'}\,s$.
\end{itemize}
\end{defi}

In words, whenever the designer allows an officer to send a message that ranks
$s$ above $s'$, there also exists a message $m_i' \in M_i$ that preserves every other pairwise comparison of $m_i$, but reverses the comparison of $s$ and $s'$.

Henceforth, we assume each message space $M_i$ satisfies richness, unless noted otherwise. This property guarantees that the preference component of the message space is \emph{balanced}: it never forces the officer to reveal a comparison in one direction without permitting the symmetric comparison in the opposite direction. Whenever the message space is clear from context we continue to write simply $M=(M_i)_{i\in I}$ and $m=(m_i)_{i\in I}$. 

Having defined both allocations and messages, we now formally define a mechanism.  
A \textbf{M-mechanism} is a function from message profiles to allocations, $\psi: M \to A$.  
We will use the shorthand \textbf{mechanism} when the space of message profiles is clear from the context.


%Let $\psi(m)_i$ denote the allocation of individual $i$ under mechanism $\psi$ for message profile $m$.  

%In particular, the current version of the IndianCS mechanism requires officers to submit both  partial preference information as well as additional information (ranking over zones) which will be used to determine the resulting allocation. 


\section{Visibly Fair Mechanisms}




\subsection{Visible Fairness}

The key notions that we introduce in this paper is that of visible fairness.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:visibleFairness}
An allocation $a$ is \textbf{visibly unfair under $m$} if for some $i\in I$ either

\begin{itemize}
    \item[i)]there is a $j\in I$ such that $a_i\not=a_j$, $\pi(i) <  \pi(j)$, and $a_j \succ_{m_i} a_i$, or
    \item[ii)]there is a $s\in S$ such that $a_i\not=s$, $|\{ i\in I: a_i=s\}|< q_s$, and $s \succ_{m_i} a_i$.
\end{itemize}
A mechanism $\psi$ is \textbf{visibly fair} if there does not exist $m\in M$ such that $\psi(m)$ is visibly unfair under $m$. 
\end{defi}

At first sight, visible (un)fairness appears to be a combination of standard non-wastefulness and elimination of justified envy. The distinction, however, lies on the fact that $m_i$, in general is incomplete, and therefore some existing wastefulness or justified envy is ``invisible'' due to the limits to the expression of the associated prefrences imposed by the message space.


Define the set $G(X, m_i)$ of all $m_i$-\textbf{maximal elements} of $X\subseteq S$ by

$$ G(X, m_i) = \{s \in X : \neg(s' \succ_{m_i} s) \text{  for all  } s' \in X\}. $$



% HOW ABOUT: $ G(X, m_i) = \{s \in X : \nexists \; s'\in X  \text{ s.t. } s' \succ_{m_i} s\}.$ 

We next introduce a new family of mechanisms.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:mQueueMechanisms}
    A mechanism $\psi^Q$ is a \textbf{$m$-queue allocation mechanism} if $\psi^Q(m)$ is the outcome produced by the following procedure:


\begin{itemize}
\item[] \textbf{Step $0$}: Set $S^1= S$.
%    \item[] \textbf{Step 1}: $a_1= G(S, m_i) \cap \mathcal{C}_i(m,S) = s^1$. If the number of officers assigned to $s^1$ reaches $q_{s^1}$, then $S^2\equiv S\backslash\{s^1\}$. Otherwise, let $S^2\equiv S$.
   \item[] \textbf{Step $k$ ($1\leq k\leq n$)}:  $a_k = s^k \in G(S^k, m_k)$. If the number of officers assigned to $s^k$ reaches $q_{s^k}$, that is $\left|\{ i\in I:  i\leq k \text{ and } a_i=s^k\}\right|= q_{s^k}$, then $S^{k+1}\equiv S^{k}\backslash\{s^k\}$. Otherwise, $S^{k+1}= S^{k}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{defi}



The $m$-queue allocation mechanism is the natural partial-order analogue of serial dictatorship. Starting from the highest-ranked officer, these are matched to undominated states, given previously assigned ones. The difference here being, of course, that there might be multiple undominated states, and therefore some selection criterion between them must also be defined.

\begin{thm}
\label{thm:CharacterizationVisiblyFairMechanisms}
$\psi$ is visibly fair if and only if it is a $m$-queue allocation mechanism. 
\end{thm}



When considering complete message spaces, Theorem \ref{thm:CharacterizationVisiblyFairMechanisms} gives us the following corollary:

\begin{coro}
\label{cor:SDUniqueVisiblyFairDM}
    If for every $M_i$ and every $s,s'\in S$, states $s$ and $s'$ are comparable, then Serial Dictatorship is the unique visibly fair $M$-mechanism. 
\end{coro}

Theorem \ref{thm:CharacterizationVisiblyFairMechanisms} provides a full characterization of visibly fair mechanisms: any such mechanism must be an \(m\)-queue allocation mechanism. While this definition is compact, it captures an extensive class of mechanisms. Designing an \(m\)-queue mechanism involves two layers of choice—first, specifying the message spaces officers can use, and second, given a message space, determining which of the undominated (\(m_i\)-maximal) states each officer is matched to. This latter choice can depend on exogenous policy parameters or on the overall message profile. In contrast, as indicated in the corollary above, when officers are allowed to submit complete preferences over all states, visible fairness alone pins down a unique mechanism—serial dictatorship. The characterization in Theorem \ref{thm:CharacterizationVisiblyFairMechanisms} therefore illustrates how relaxing the message space dramatically expands the set of mechanisms that satisfy visible fairness, enabling greater flexibility in accommodating policy goals beyond those implementable via serial dictatorship.

\subsection{Two Special Message Spaces}

We will now consider two natural families of message spaces, which are used in some of the existing real-life applications of our concepts and that will serve as the base for the implementation of distributional objectives in Section \ref{sec:DistributionalObjectives}.

\subsubsection{Zonal Message Space}

We now introduce a particular family of message spaces that induce a partition of the set of states into “zones.” Formally, suppose a message space \(M_i\) is such that the states \(S\) can be partitioned into disjoint subsets \(Z = \{z_1, \ldots, z_\ell\}\) with \(\bigcup_{j=1}^\ell z_j = S\) and \(z_j \cap z_{j'} = \emptyset\) for \(j \neq j'\).  We call \(M_i\) a \textbf{zonal message space} if, within each subset \(z_j\), any two states are comparable, while any two states in different subsets are never comparable.  Concretely:

\textbf{Within-zone completeness}: For every zone \(z_j\) and every pair of distinct states \((s, s')\) in \(z_j\), there exists some message \(m_i \in M_i\) such that \(s \succ_{m_i} s'\), and also another message \(m_i' \in M_i\) such that \(s' \succ_{m_i'} s\).  
   In other words, within each zone, the message space is rich enough to permit any ranking of states within that zone.

\textbf{Across-zone incomparability}: For every pair of zones \((z_j, z_{j'})\) with \(j \neq j'\), and for every message \(m_i \in M_i\), no two states in \(z_j\) and \(z_{j'}\) are ever ordered under \(\succ_{m_i}\).  
   That is, the message space never allows an officer to rank a state in \(z_j\) relative to a state in \(z_{j'}\).

From an officer’s perspective, submitting a message in a zonal message space amounts to choosing a complete (strict) ordering over states within each zone but leaving no comparison defined across zones. 

\begin{example}
Suppose the states \(S\) are \(\{s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4\}\).  One could design a message space that {effectively} partitions these states into two zones:

\[
z_1 = \{s_1, s_2\}, \quad z_2 = \{s_3, s_4\}.
\]

Officers would be required to provide complete rankings among \(\{s_1, s_2\}\) and among \(\{s_3, s_4\}\), but they would never be allowed to compare \(s_1\) (or \(s_2\)) with \(s_3\) (or \(s_4\)).  Consequently, any message in this space is of the form:  

\begin{itemize}
    \item Within \(z_1\), rank \(s_1\) above \(s_2\) (or vice versa). 
    \item Within \(z_2\), rank \(s_3\) above \(s_4\) (or vice versa).
    \item Across \(z_1\) and \(z_2\), no ordering is possible.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}


Although the zone partition can be entirely endogenously derived from how comparisons are restricted in the message space, a mechanism designer may also choose to impose such a structure normatively, for example to ensure no cross-zone comparisons are made (as in the Indian Civil Services, where certain sets of states are grouped into “zones”).  From a theoretical standpoint, both perspectives are equivalent: a zonal message space is simply one in which states are fully comparable within each zone and never comparable across zones.

The zonal structure will be convenient for discussing distributional objectives in later sections (Section \ref{sec:DistributionalObjectives}). Intuitively, each zone can be treated as an internally ranked “block” of states, allowing certain policy objectives—such as balancing officer assignments across different geographical or administrative regions—to be embedded in how officers are permitted to convey their preferences.

Notice that given any $Z$, $i$, zonal message space $M_i$, and a preference over states $\succ_i$, there is exactly one message $m_i\in M_i$ such that for every pair of states $s,s'$, $s \succ_i s' \iff  s \succ_{m_i} s'$. We will say that $m_i$ \textbf{represents} $\succ_i$ in $M_i$. 

Suppose each officer has a zonal message space. Consider a \textbf{zone selection function} $\mathcal{C}_i: 2^S \times {M}\to Z$, which is a function such that for any $X\subseteq S$, $X \,\cap\, \mathcal{C}_i(X,m)  \not= \emptyset$.\footnote{That is, as long as there are states with spare capacity available, $C_i$ must choose a zone with at least one of them.}

\begin{defi}
\label{def:PartitionPriorityMech}
A mechanism $\psi$ is a \textbf{partitioned priority mechanism} if there exists a zone selection function profile $\left(\mathcal{C}_i\right)_{i\in I}$ such that for any message profile $m$, $\psi(m)$ is the outcome produced by the following procedure:

\begin{itemize}
\item[] \textbf{Step $0$}: Set $S^1= S$.
%    \item[] \textbf{Step 1}: $a_1= G(S, m_i) \cap \mathcal{C}_i(m,S) = s^1$. If the number of officers assigned to $s^1$ reaches $q_{s^1}$, then $S^2\equiv S\backslash\{s^1\}$. Otherwise, let $S^2\equiv S$.
   \item[] \textbf{Step $k$ ($1\leq k\leq n$)}:  $a_k \in G(S^k, m_k) \cap \mathcal{C}_k(S^k,m))= \{s^k\}$.\footnote{It is easy to see that within each zone, there is a unique $m_i$-maximal element, since all states in a zone are comparable.} If the number of officers assigned to $s^k$ reaches $q_{s^k}$, that is $\left|\{ i\in I:  i\leq k \text{ and } a_i=s^k\}\right|= q_{s^k}$, then $S^{k+1}\equiv S^{k}\backslash\{s^k\}$. Otherwise, $S^{k+1}= S^{k}$.
\end{itemize} 
\end{defi}






\begin{thm}
\label{thm:CharacterizationIncontestableMechanisms2}
For a zonal message space $M$, $\psi$ is visibly fair if and only if it is a partitioned priority mechanism.
\end{thm}



\subsubsection{Zonal Message Space with Ranking over the Zones}

We now enrich the idea of a zonal message space by allowing officers to impose a strict ordering \emph{across} the zones.  As before, let $Z = \{z_1,\dots,z_\ell\}$ be a partition of $S$ into disjoint zones.  Let, moreover, $\max(X,m_i)\equiv\bigl\{\,s\in X:\nexists s'\in X,\;s'\succ_{m_i}s\,\bigr\}$ and $\min(X,m_i)\equiv\bigl\{\,s\in X:\nexists s'\in X,\;s\succ_{m_i}s'\bigr\}$. A \textbf{zonal message space with ranking over the zones} is a message space $M_i$ in which:

\textbf{Within each zone}, every pair of states is fully comparable (i.e., for any two states $s, s'\in z$, there exist messages in which $s \succ_{m_i} s'$ and others in which $s' \succ_{m_i} s$), just as in the standard zonal case.

\textbf{Across zones}, each message $m_i\in M_i$ implies a ranking over zones:
  \[
    z_1 \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; z_2 \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; \dots \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; z_\ell ,
  \]
  where  
  \[
    z_i \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; z_{j}
    \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
    \max(z_i,m_i)\;\succ_{m_i}\;\min\!\bigl(z_{j},m_i\bigr).
  \]

No other preferences among states can be expressed in these message spaces.

In words, a these rankings augment zonal message spaces in a minimal sense: it allows for officers to express some information about how they rank zones in the weakest sense: by ranking zone $z$ above $z'$, they are saying that the best state in $z$ is preferred over the worst in $z'$. Notice that if this was not the case, we would have $z$ being ranked above $z'$ while every state in $z'$ is preferred to every state in $z$.\footnote{To see this, suppose that $Z=\{z_1,z_2\}$, $z_1=\{s_1,s_2\}$ and $z_2=\{s_3,s_4\}$. Let $m_i$ be such that $s_1\succ_{m_i}s_2$ and $s_3\succ_{m_i}s_4$. If a ranking $z_1 \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; z_2$ is associated with $s_1\succ_{m_i}s_4$, we have this additional comparison between states and nothing else. But if it was associated with $s_4\succ_{m_i}s_1$, this would imply that $s_3\succ_{m_i}s_4\succ_{m_i}s_1\succ_{m_i}s_2$, that is, a complete preference in which every state in $z_2$ is preferred to every state in $z_1$, which would be at odds with any reasonable interpretation of what $z_1 \;\triangleright_{m_i}\; z_2$ implies for preferences among these states.}

Notice, moreover, that Zonal Message Space with Ranking over the Zones do not satisfy the richness condition we introduced in definition \ref{def:richness}: you can express $\max(z_k,m_i)\;\succ_{m_i}\;\min\!\bigl(z_{j},m_i\bigr)$, but not $\min\!\bigl(z_{j},m_i\bigr)\succ_{m_i}\max(z_k,m_i)\;$.

\begin{example}
Suppose the states \(S\) are \(\{s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4\}\).  One could design a message space that {effectively} partitions these states into two zones:

\[
z_1 = \{s_1, s_2\}, \quad z_2 = \{s_3, s_4\}.
\]

Officers would be required to provide complete rankings among \(\{s_1, s_2\}\) and among \(\{s_3, s_4\}\), and a ranking over zones $\triangleright_{m_i}$. Consequently, any message in this space is of the form:  

\begin{itemize}
    \item Within \(z_1\), rank \(s_1\) above \(s_2\) (or vice versa).
    \item Within \(z_2\), rank \(s_3\) above \(s_4\) (or vice versa).
    \item In addition to these:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item If $s_1\succ_{m_i}s_2$ and $s_3\succ_{m_i}s_4$, either $s_1$ is ranked above $s_4$ ($z_1\triangleright_{m_i}z_2$), or $s_3$ above $s_2$ ($z_2\triangleright_{m_i}z_1$).
            \item If $s_1\succ_{m_i}s_2$ and $s_4\succ_{m_i}s_3$, either $s_1$ is ranked above $s_3$ ($z_1\triangleright_{m_i}z_2$), or $s_4$ above $s_2$ ($z_2\triangleright_{m_i}z_1$).
            \item If $s_2\succ_{m_i}s_1$ and $s_3\succ_{m_i}s_4$, either $s_2$ is ranked above $s_4$ ($z_1\triangleright_{m_i}z_2$), or $s_3$ above $s_1$ ($z_2\triangleright_{m_i}z_1$).
            \item If $s_2\succ_{m_i}s_1$ and $s_4\succ_{m_i}s_3$, either $s_2$ is ranked above $s_3$ ($z_1\triangleright_{m_i}z_2$), or $s_4$ above $s_1$ ($z_2\triangleright_{m_i}z_1$).
        \end{itemize}
    \item No other preferences across states are possible.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}\medskip


Suppose each officer $i$ has a zonal message space with ranking over the zones, as described above.
Define a \textbf{ranked zone selection function} $\mathcal{C}_i: 2^S \times M \,\to\, Z$ to be a mapping that, for each subset of states $X \subseteq S$ and each message profile $m$, selects a zone $\mathcal{C}_i(X,m) \in Z$ such that:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item $X \cap \mathcal{C}_i(X,m) \neq \emptyset$, and
    \item either
   \[
     X \;\cap\; \mathcal{C}_i(X,m) 
     \;\neq\; \bigl\{\min\!\bigl(\mathcal{C}_i(X,m),m_i\bigr)\bigr\},
   \]
   or there is no zone $z \in Z$ with $z \,\triangleright_{m_i}\, \mathcal{C}_i(X,m)$ and 
   \(\max\!\bigl(z,m_i\bigr) \,\in\, X.\)
\end{enumerate}

A ranked zone selection function restricts which zone will be assigned to an officer on the basis of the limits visible fairness imply given the ranking over zones: if a zone $z$ contains only the state deemed as the least preferred in $z$, no zone $z'$ for which $z'\triangleright_{m_i}z$ can have capacity in its most-preferred state. 

\begin{defi}
A mechanism $\psi$ is a \textbf{ranked partitioned priority mechanism} if there exists a ranked zone selection function profile $(\mathcal{C}_i)_{i\in I}$ such that for any message profile $m$, $\psi(m)$ is the outcome produced by the following procedure:


\begin{itemize}
\item[] \textbf{Step $0$}: Set $S^1= S$.
   \item[] \textbf{Step $k$ ($1\leq k\leq n$)}:  $a_k \in G(S^k, m_k) \cap \mathcal{C}_k(S^k,m))= \{s^k\}$.\footnote{Ranked zone selection function makes sure that within the selected zone, $m_k$-maximal element exists. Since all states in a zone are comparable, this element is unique.} If the number of officers assigned to $s^k$ reaches $q_{s^k}$, that is $\left|\{ i\in I:  i\leq k \text{ and } a_i=s^k\}\right|= q_{s^k}$, then $S^{k+1}\equiv S^{k}\backslash\{s^k\}$. Otherwise, $S^{k+1}= S^{k}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{defi}


\begin{thm}
\label{thm:CharacterizationIncontestableMechanisms3}
For zonal message space with ranking over zones, $\psi$ is visibly fair if and only if it is a ranked partitioned priority mechanism.
\end{thm}





\section{Incentives}


To analyze incentives, we first define what it means for an officer to report truthfully. The idea is straightforward: given an officer’s preference over states, their report is truthful if the underlying preference information in the submitted message aligns with their actual preferences.  
For a given officer $i\in I$, let $\mathcal{Q}_i$ be the \textbf{set of all preferences} over $S$. Officer $i$'s message $m_i$ is a \textbf{truthful message} for a preference $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$,  if for all $s,s'\in S$, $$s \succ_{m_i} s' \implies  s \succ_i s'.$$
Note that our richness assumption on the message space $M_i$ ensures  the existence of truthful message for every officer. 
That is, for every preference $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$, there exists at least one message $m_i\in M_i$ that is truthful.


When it comes to incentives, a key desideratum is that an officer who submits a truthful message should never receive a less preferred allocation than if they were to report any other message. 
Formally, a mechanism $\psi$ is \textbf{strategy-proof} if for all $i\in I$, $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$, for any $m_i\in M_i$ that is a truthful message for $\succsim_i$, and any other message $\hat{m}_i\in {M}_i$ we have
$$\psi(m)_i \succsim_i \psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i.$$ 



We next define two conditions that are sufficient for a visibly fair mechanism to be strategy-proof, and weakening one of the two conditions are also necessary for a visibly fair mechanism to be strategy-proof. 

The first condition ensures that an officer can provide preference information about other states he can obtain by deviating from a truthful message.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:expressiveness}
Let $\psi$ be a mechanism and let $m$ be a message profile.  
The interest into state $s \in S \setminus \{\psi(m)\}$ is \textbf{expressed} by officer $i$ under $\psi(m)$ if either $s \succ_{m_i} \psi(m)_i$ or $\psi(m)_i \succ_{m_i} s$.

A mechanism \(\psi\) satisfies \textbf{expressiveness} if for all $i\in I$, $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$, for any $m_i\in M_i$ that is a truthful message for $\succsim_i$, and any other message $\hat{m}_i\in {M}_i$ s.t. \(\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i \neq_i \psi(m)_i\),
we have that interest into state \(\psi(\hat{m}_i,m_{-i})_i\) was \emph{expressed} by officer $i$ under \(\psi(m)\).
\end{defi}


The second condition captures the idea that an officer cannot manipulate the availability of states by deviating from a truthful message. 
%Note that, as there are potentially multiple truthful messages a deviation can either be a different truthful message or an untruthful message.


\begin{defi}
\label{def:availability}
Let \(\psi\) be a mechanism and let \(m\) be a message profile.  
A state \(s \in S \setminus \{\psi(m)\}\) is \textbf{available} to officer \(i\) under \(\psi(m)\) if \\
$   \left|\{\,j\in I : \psi(m)_j = s\} \,\cap\,\{\,j: \pi(j)<\pi(i)\}\right|
   \;<\; q_s.$


A mechanism \(\psi\) satisfies \textbf{availability} if for all $i\in I$, $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$, for any $m_i\in M_i$ that is a truthful message for $\succsim_i$, and any other message $\hat{m}_i\in {M}_i$ s.t.
\(\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i \neq_i \psi(m)_i\),
we have that state \(\psi(\hat{m}_i,m_{-i})_i\) was \emph{available} to officer $i$ under \(\psi(m)\).
\end{defi}

Interestingly, availability is too strong of a condition for ensuring strategy-proofness. Indeed, while availability ensures that an officer cannot manipulate the availability of states by deviating from a truthful message, we only require that an officer cannot manipulate the availability of strictly preferred states relative to his assignment --- this condition, together with expressiveness in necessary for a visibly fair mechanism to be strategy-proof. 



\begin{defi}
\label{def:weakavailability}
A mechanism \(\psi\) satisfies \textbf{weak availability} if for all $i\in I$, $\succsim_i\in \mathcal{Q}_i$, for any $m_i\in M_i$ that is a truthful message for $\succsim_i$, and any other message $\hat{m}_i\in {M}_i$ s.t. \(\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i \succ_i \psi(m)_i\),
we have that state \(\psi(\hat{m}_i,m_{-i})_i\) was \emph{available} to officer $i$ under \(\psi(m)\).
\end{defi}



Now we are ready to formally state our main result on incentives.

\begin{thm}
\label{thm:Truthful1} A visibly fair mechanism is strategy-proof 
if and only if it satisfies expressiveness and weak availability. 
\end{thm}

The following corollary is immediate: 
\begin{coro}
\label{coro:Truthful1}
    A visibly fair mechanism is strategy-proof if it satisfies expressiveness and availability. 
\end{coro}


To give some intuition behind the results we given an example of a strategy-proof mechanism violating availability --- but satisfying weak availability and expressiveness. 

\begin{example}[Strategy-proof mechanism violating availability]
\label{example:Violation availability}
\quad
\begin{itemize}
\item 
Officers $I=\{\,i_1,i_2\}$ with priority $\pi(i_1)<\pi(i_2)$.
\item 
States $S=\{\,s_1,s_2\}$, each with capacity $q_s=1$.

\end{itemize}
Consider a mechanism $\psi$ where $i_1$ can only submit a single message $m_{i_1}$ without any preference information and $i_2$ can either submit message $m_i: s_1 -s_2$ or $m'_i: s_2 -s_1$.
Finally, let $\psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})=(s_2,s_1)$ and $\psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})=(s_1,s_2)$. 


Clearly, the mechanism is strategy-proof as $i_1$ cannot influence the outcome by submitting a different message, and $i_2$ always gets his top choice when submitting preferences truthfully. 
Moreover, the mechanism is visibly fair as $i_1$ gives no preference information and $i_2$ gets his top choice. 

It is easy to verify that availability is violated as, e.g., suppose we have $\succsim_{i_2}: s_1 - s_2$, then submitting message $m_i$ which is truthful for $\succsim_{i_2}$, we have another message $m'_i$ that leads to a different outcome $\psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})_{i_2}=\{s_2\} \neq \psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2}=\{s_1\}$ which is not available under $\psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2}$. 

Note that weak availability is not violated as under $s_1 \succsim_{i_2} s_2$ we have $\psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2} \succ_{i_2} \psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})_{i_2} $, and analogous under $s_2 \succsim_{i_2} s_1$ --- and truthful message $m'_{i_1}$ --- we have $\psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})_{i_2} \succsim_{i_2} \psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2}$. 

Overall this example illustrates how availability too strong a requirement for strategy-proofness. 
\end{example}\medskip





Furthermore, we give a second example of a mechanism satisfying expressiveness but violating weak availability and thus strategy-proofness. 


\begin{example}[Expressive but not weakly available mechanism]
\label{example:Violation availability}
\quad
\begin{itemize}
\item 
Officers $I=\{\,i_1,i_2\}$ with priority $\pi(i_1)<\pi(i_2)$.
\item 
States $S=\{\,s_1,s_2\}$, each with capacity $q_s=1$.

\end{itemize}
Consider a mechanism $\psi$ where $i_1$ can only submit a single message $m_{i_1}$ without any preference information and $i_2$ can either submit message $m_i: s_1 \succ_{m_i} s_2$ or $m'_i: s_2 \succ_{m'_i} s_1$.
Finally, let $\psi(m_{i_1},m_{i_2})=(s_1,s_2)$ and $\psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})=(s_2,s_1)$. 

Clearly, the mechanism is not strategy-proof as $i_2$ always gets his second choice when submitting a truthful message and can get his first choice by simply reporting the opposite message. 
The mechanism is visibly fair as $i_1$ does not give any preference information and $i_2$ only reported preferred state is given to the higher priority officer $i_1$. 

The mechanism also satisfies expressiveness as under any message $i_2$ gives full preference information, while $i_1$ has a single message automatically satisfying expressiveness. 
At the same time the mechanism violates weak availability as e.g. under $\succsim_{i_2}: s_1 - s_2$ and truthful message $m_{i_2}$ we have another message $m'_{i_2}$ s.t. $\psi(m'_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2} \succ_{i_2} \psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})_{i_2} $ and $\psi(m'_{i_1},m_{i_2})_{i_2}$ not being available under $\psi(m_{i_1},m'_{i_2})$.


\end{example}\medskip

Finally, an alternative condition that ensures that a mechanism is strategy proof is as follows: 

\begin{defi}
  A mechanism satisfies \textbf{consistency} if for any message $m_i \in M_i$ and any other message $\hat{m}_i \in M_i$ such that $\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i \neq \psi(m)_i$ we have $\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i\not\in G(\{\psi(m)_i\} \cup \{\psi(\hat{m}_i, m_{-i})_i\} , m_i)$.  
\end{defi}


Indeed, the following result follows almost immediately: 

\begin{thm}
\label{thm:Truthful2} A mechanism is strategy-proof if and only if it satisfies consistency. 
\end{thm}

\subsection{Two special message spaces revisited}

In this section we show that, in its most general form, neither the class of partition priority mechanism nor the ranked partition priority mechanisms are strategy-proof.
In \Cref{example:Violation s-p pp} we show that in a zonal message space partition priority mechanisms can fail availability.
Moreover, in \Cref{example:Violation s-p rpp} we show that in a zonal message space with rankings no visibly fair mechanism, thus including ranked partition priority mechanisms, might satisfy expressiveness. 


\begin{example}[Partitioned‐priority can violate strategy-proofness]
\label{example:Violation s-p pp}
\quad
\begin{itemize}
\item 
Officers $I=\{\,i_1,i_2,i_3\}$ with priority $\pi(i_1)<\pi(i_2)<\pi(i_3)$.
\item 
States $S=\{\,s_1,s_2,s_3\}$, each with capacity $q_s=1$.
\item 
A zonal message space with zones $z_1=\{s_1,s_2\}$ and $z_2=\{s_3\}$.
\item 
Suppose each officer’s true preference is $s_1 \succ_i s_2 \succ_i s_3$. For each $i$, let $m_i$ be the message that ranks $s_1 \succ_{m_i} s_2$, and let $m_i'$ reverse it: $s_2 \succ_{m_i'} s_1.$
\end{itemize}

Consider the following zone selection function:

\[
\begin{aligned}
  &\mathcal{C}_{i_1}(S,\,m) \;=\; z_1, 
    &&\mathcal{C}_{i_1}(S,\,(m_{-i_3},m_{i_3}')) \;=\; z_1,\\
  &\mathcal{C}_{i_2}(\{s_2,s_3\},\,m) \;=\; z_1,
    &&\mathcal{C}_{i_2}(\{s_2,s_3\},\,(m_{-i_3},m_{i_3}')) \;=\; z_2,\\
  &\mathcal{C}_{i_3}(\{s_3\},\,m) \;=\; z_2,
    &&\mathcal{C}_{i_3}(\{s_2\},\,(m_{-i_3},m_{i_3}')) \;=\; z_1.
\end{aligned}
\]
\smallskip

Consider the following successful manipulation for $i_3$: Under the truthful profile $m$, the allocation is $(s_1, s_2, s_3)$; but when $i_3$ flips her internal ranking $(m_{i_3}\!\to\!m_{i_3}')$, the mechanism assigns $(s_1, s_3, s_2)$, giving $i_3$ a better match $s_2 \succ_{i_3} s_3$.  

Weak availability is violated as e.g. $s_2$ is available under $m_{i_3}'$ but not under $m_{i_3}$ which is a truthful message for $\succ_{i_3}$ under which $s_2$ is preferred to $s_3$.

Similarly, expressiveness is violated as e.g. officer $i_3$ does not express any preference information regarding $s_2$ and $s_3$ given message $m_{i_3}$, which is a truthful message for $\succ_{i_3}$, even though $s_2$ is allocated to $i_3$ under message $m_{i_3}'$ and $s_3$ is allocated to $i_3$ under message $m_{i_3}$.

\end{example}\medskip


Finally, we give an example that illustrates the failure of a ranked partition priority mechanism to be strategy-proof due to its violation of expressiveness. 




\begin{example}[Ranked‐partitioned priority can violate Expressiveness]
\label{example:Violation s-p rpp}
\quad
\begin{itemize}
\item 
Officers $I = \{i_1, i_2, i_3\}$ with priority $\pi(i_1)<\pi(i_2)<\pi(i_3)$.
\item 
States $S = \{s_1, s_2, s_3\}$, each with capacity $q_s = 1$.
\item 
A \emph{zonal message space ranking over zones}: $z_1=\{s_1,s_2\}$, $z_2=\{s_3\}$.  
Every officer’s message must fully rank $s_1$ and $s_2$ and also specify whether $z_1\rhd_{m_i}z_2$ or $z_2\rhd_{m_i}z_1$.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
\emph{True preferences for $i_1$:} $s_1 \succ_{i_1} s_2 \succ_{i_1} s_3$.  
This yields a \emph{unique} truthful message $m^\rhd_{i_1}: (s_1 \succ s_2 \rhd_{} s_3)$,\footnote{A message $m^{\rhd}_{i_1}: s_1 \succ_{i_1} s_2 \rhd s_3$ is our abbreviation for the preference message with $s_1\succ_{m_{i_1}} s_2$ and $z_1 \rhd_{m_{i_1}} z_2$.} ensuring $i_1$ obtains $s_1$ in any visibly fair mechanism.

\smallskip
\smallskip
\smallskip

\noindent
\emph{Two preferences for $i_2$:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item First, suppose $i_2$ has $s_3 \succ_{i_2} s_2 \succ_{i_2} s_1$.  
The unique truthful message $m_{i_2}: (s_3\,\rhd\, s_2 \succ s_1)$  forces $i_2$ to end up with $s_3$ under any visibly fair, strategy‐proof mechanism. To see this, suppose that $i_2$ is assigned $s_2$ instead. Then, by submitting $m^{\rhd \prime}_{i_2}: s_3 \rhd s_1 \succ s_2$, any visibly fair mechanism must assign $s_3$ to $i_2$, leading to a successful manipulation. 

\item Next, consider $\succ_{i_2}: \,s_2 \succ s_3 \succ s_1$.   Here, \emph{two} truthful messages are possible.  One message 
$m_{i_2}: (s_3 \rhd s_2 \succ s_1)$ again assigns $s_3$ to $i_2$.  
Another message 
$m^{\rhd \prime \prime}_{i_2}: (s_2 \succ s_1 \rhd s_3)$ assigns $s_2$ to $i_2$.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent

Here, expressiveness is violated as e.g. officer $i_2$ does not express any preference information regarding $s_2$ and $s_3$ given message $m_{i_2}$, which is a truthful message for $\succ_{i_2}: \,s_2 \succ s_3 \succ s_1$, even though $s_2$ is allocated to $i_2$ under message $m^{\rhd \prime \prime}_{i_2}$ and $s_3$ is allocated to $i_2$ under message $m_{i_2}$.
On the other hand, weak availability does not pose a problem in the above example. 

\end{example}\medskip


Given \Cref{thm:Truthful1}, combined with \Cref{example:Violation s-p pp} and \Cref{example:Violation s-p rpp} the following corollary is immediate: 

\begin{coro}
\label{coro: s-p}
Consider the zonal message space with and without rankings, then
both the partitioned priority mechanism and ranked partition priority mechanism might fail to be strategy-proof.  
\end{coro}

%\textit{Note: the example above can probably be extended to represent the last two agents, so that we might have lots of states and zones, but just left with three states and two zones available. This should be enough for the negative result to be ``almost universal''.}



\section{Achieving Distributional Objectives}
\label{sec:DistributionalObjectives}

When using direct mechanisms, our earlier discussion shows that only serial dictatorship (SD) achieves visible fairness. By eliciting less information about preferences—through carefully designed restricted message spaces—we expand the set of allocations that can be deemed visibly fair for a given problem. This relaxation provides the mechanism designer with additional flexibility, allowing for the implementation of a broader array of allocation rules that still adhere to this notion of fairness.

There are, in principle, many distinct distributional objectives that can be accommodated within this broader framework. In this section, we introduce one family of such objectives, which we denote \emph{Modular Upper-Bounds}, and give complete instructions on how to design mechanisms that are visibly fair, strategy-proof, and respect these bounds. Modular Upper-bounds model distributional objectives by imposing limits on the number of officers of certain types assigned to specific subsets of states. 

\subsection{Modular Upper-Bounds}

We extend our original model by saying that each officer has a type $t$ from a finite set of types $T$. The distributional goals of the designer are modeled through type-specific modular upper-bounds, where for each type $t \in T$, a collection of upper-bounds specifies limits on the allocation of type-$t$ officers to subsets of states.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:ModularUpperBounds}
Given a finite set of types $T$, for each type $t \in T$, a \textbf{modular upper-bound system for type $t$}, $H^t$, is a collection of pairs $h=(S_h^t, k_h^t)$, where:
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
    \item $S_h^t \subseteq S$ is a subset of states,
    \item $k_h^t \in \mathbb{N}$ is an upper-bound on the number of type-$t$ officers that can be assigned to states in $S_h^t$.
\end{enumerate}

We denote by $H=\left(H^t\right)_{t\in T}$ a \textbf{modular upper-bound system}.
\end{defi}


\begin{defi}
\label{def:respectsModularUpperBounds}
An allocation $a \in \mathcal{A}$ \textbf{respects the modular upper-bounds} $H=\left(\right)_{t\in T}$ if, for every type $t \in T$ and every $(S_h^t, k_h^t) \in H^t$, the number of type-$t$ officers assigned to states in $S_h^t$ does not exceed $k_h^t$, that is,
\[
    \left| \left\{ i \in I : a_i \in S_h^t \text{ and } t_i = t \right\} \right| \leq k_h^t.
\]   
\end{defi}


We say that modular upper-bound $(S_h^t, k_h^t)$ is \textbf{binding} at allocation $a \in \mathcal{A}$ if:

$$\left| \left\{ i \in I : a_i \in S_h^t\text{ and } t_i = t \right\} \right| = k_h^t$$

The literature has proposed several ways to formalise “quota–type” constraints.\footnote{An incomplete list includes \cite{echeniqueHowControlControlled2015}, \cite{kamadaEfficientMatchingDistributional2015}, \cite{gotoDesigningMatchingMechanisms2017}, \cite{kamadaStabilityStrategyproofnessMatching2018}, \cite{azizMatchingDiversityConstraints2020}, \cite{kojimaJobMatchingConstraints2020}, and \cite{kamadaFairMatchingConstraints2024}.} An example of a very \emph{permissive} notion is the hereditary family of \citet{gotoDesigningMatchingMechanisms2017}: write a matching as a vector that counts, for every state, how many officers of each type are assigned there; a subset of vectors is \emph{hereditary} when it is closed under coordinate-wise decreases. Any system of pure ceilings clearly has this property, so every modular upper-bound instance fits inside the hereditary domain. A much \emph{tighter} specification is the hierarchical (laminar) system analysed by \citep{kamadaEfficientMatchingDistributional2015,kamadaStabilityStrategyproofnessMatching2018}: here the subsets that carry quotas must form a tree—any two are either disjoint or one contains the other. Laminar caps are useful when the policy maker wants, say, regional ceilings that line up neatly with district ceilings, but they rule out overlapping constraints such as “no more than ten officers in the Northeastern states \emph{and} no more than eight in the coastal states.” All laminar systems are modular, yet the converse is false.

\subsection{Modular-induced Message Spaces}

To design mechanisms that respect modular upper-bounds, we define the \emph{Modular-induced Message Spaces}. These are zonal message spaces where states are partitioned into zones based on the upper-bounds, grouping together states involved in the same upper-bounds.

\begin{defi}
\label{def:stateUpperBoundSignature}
For each state $s \in S$ and type $t\in T$, define its \textbf{upper-bound signature} as the collection of type-specific upper-bounds that involve $s$ for type $t$:
\[
    H(s,t) = \left\{ h :\ (S_h^t, k_h^t) \in H^t,\ s \in S_h^t \right\}.
\]
\end{defi}

\begin{defi}
\label{def:modularInducedMessageSpaces}
For any type $t\in T$, define an equivalence relation $\sim_{t}$ on $S$ such that, for all $s, s' \in S$,
\[
    s \sim_{t} s' \quad \text{if and only if} \quad H(s,t) = H(s',t).
\]

The \textbf{Modular-induced Message Space} associated with the modular upper-bounds $H^t$ is the zonal message space $M_{t}$ with zones $Z = \{ Z_1, Z_2, \dots, Z_k \}$, where each zone $Z_j$ is an equivalence class under $\sim_{t}$, that is,
\[
    Z_j = \{ s \in S : s \sim_{t} s_j \},
\]
for some representative state $s_j \in S$.
\end{defi}

\noindent This construction ensures that:
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
    \item Zones are disjoint and partition the set of states: $\bigcup_{j} Z_j = S$ and $Z_j \cap Z_{j'} = \emptyset$ for $j \neq j'$.
    \item All states within the same zone are involved in exactly the same set of upper-bounds for the type $t$. Therefore, if some upper-bound is binding for some state in a zone, then it binds for all states in that zone.
\end{enumerate}




\begin{example}
Consider a problem with five states \(S=\{s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,s_5\}\), each having capacity \(q_s=1\). There are two officer types: \(t_1\) and \(t_2\).
    \begin{itemize}

        \item For type \(t_1\), the upper-bound system for $t_1$ is 
        \[
        H^{t_1}=\Bigl\{ \bigl(\{s_1,s_2,s_3\},\,2\bigr) \Bigr\},
        \]
        meaning that at most 2 type-\(t_1\) officers may be assigned to states in \(\{s_1,s_2,s_3\}\).
        \item For type \(t_2\), the upper-bound system is 
        \[
        H^{t_2}=\Bigl\{ \bigl(\{s_3,s_4,s_5\},\,1\bigr) \Bigr\},
        \]
        meaning that at most 1 type-\(t_2\) officer may be assigned to states in \(\{s_3,s_4,s_5\}\).
    \end{itemize}
\end{example}\medskip


\subsection{Modular Prioritized Allocation Mechanism}

Fix a finite set of agents \(I\) and a strict priority order \(\pi\) on \(I\). Let \(H=\bigl(H^t\bigr)_{t\in T}\) be a given modular upper-bound system for a finite set of types \(T\). Each type \(t \in T\) induces a \emph{modular-induced message space} \(M_{t}\), as in Definition~\ref{def:modularInducedMessageSpaces}, where for each type $t\in T$, \(S\) is partitioned into zones according to equivalence classes of states under the same upper-bound signature. 

Thus, for an officer \(i\) of type \(t_i\), the mechanism \emph{offers} the zonal space \(M_{t_i}\), with associated zones $z_1,\dots,z_K$, requiring her to submit a message \(m_i\in M_{t_i}\). That message ranks all states within each zone but cannot compare states across different zones.

\medskip

As part of the mechanism design, each officer \(i\) is also assigned an \emph{exogenous} ranking
\[
  z_1 \,\blacktriangleright_i\, z_2 \,\blacktriangleright_i\,\cdots\,\blacktriangleright_i\, z_K
\]
over the same zones, independent of the message \(m_i\). These exogenous rankings---which can encode policy priorities such as emphasizing certain zones first or last---do not depend on agents' reports. 

\begin{defi}\label{def:modularPriorityMechanism}
Given a strict priority ranking \(\pi\), a collection of modular upper-bounds \(H=\bigl(H^t\bigr)_{t \in T}\), an exogenous zone ranking \(\blacktriangleright_i\) for each officer \(i\), and a profile of messages \(m=(m_i)_{i\in I}\), each \(m_i\) in the modular-induced message space \(M_{t_i}\), the \textbf{Modular Priority Mechanism} \(\psi\) proceeds as follows:

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Initialization:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item For each \(s \in S\), set remaining capacity \(C_s = q_s\).
  \item For each zone \(z\) and type \(t\), set a flag \(B_z^t = \mathrm{False}\). 
  \item Set \(a_i=\varnothing\) for all \(i\in I\).
\end{itemize}

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Sequential Assignment:}
Process the officers in the order \((i_1,\dots,i_n)\) where \(\pi(i_1) < \cdots < \pi(i_n)\). For each \(k=1,\dots,n\):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Let \(t_{i_k}\) be the type of officer \(i_k\). Her message \(m_{i_k}\) partitions \(S\) into zones \(z_1,\dots,z_K\). 

  \item Starting from the top-ranked zone \(z_1\) under \(\blacktriangleright_{i_k}\), find the first zone \(z_\ell\) such that:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \(B_{z_\ell}^{t_{i_k}} = \mathrm{False}\). (No modular upper-bound for type \(t_{i_k}\) is yet fully binding in \(z_\ell\).)
    \item There is a state \(s \in z_\ell\) for which \(C_s>0\).
  \end{itemize}

  \item If no such zone is found, set \(a_{i_k}=\varnothing\), and move to \(i_{k+1}\). Otherwise:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Let \(s^*\) be the \emph{most-preferred} state of \(i_k\) \emph{within} \(z_\ell\) (according to \(m_{i_k}\)) that still has \(C_{s^*} > 0\). 
    \item Set \(a_{i_k} = s^*\) and reduce capacity \(C_{s^*} \leftarrow C_{s^*}-1\).
    \item For each modular upper-bound \(\bigl(S_h^{t_{i_k}}, k_h^{t_{i_k}}\bigr)\in H^{t_{i_k}}\):
    \begin{itemize}
      \item If \(s^* \in S_h^{t_{i_k}}\), then \emph{increase} the count of type-\(t_{i_k}\) officers in \(S_h^{t_{i_k}}\). 
      \item If this count has now \emph{reached} \(k_h^{t_{i_k}}\) (i.e.\ the upper-bound is \emph{binding}), then for all zones \(z'\in \{z_1,\dots,z_K\}\) with \(z'\cap S_h^{t_{i_k}}\neq\varnothing\), set \(B_{z'}^{t_{i_k}} = \mathrm{True}\). 
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Outcome:} 
After processing all agents \(i_1,\dots,i_n\), the mechanism outputs the allocation \(a=(a_i)_{i\in I}\). We denote 
\[
  \psi(m)\;=\;a.
\]
\end{defi}
\medskip

The Modular Priority Mechanism can be viewed as a partitioned priority mechanism in which each officer type is associated with a zonal message space of states that share the same upper-bound constraints. An officer’s final assignment is determined by two key factors: (1) a counter that tracks remaining capacity for the relevant states, and (2) a flag indicating whether any upper-bound restrictions in that zone have become binding. Because all states in a given zone are governed by the same set of constraints, a single triggered bound applies uniformly across the entire zone. Below, we present the main result and two examples that illustrate how this mechanism operates in practice.

\begin{thm}\label{modulartheorem}
    The Modular Priority Mechanism is visibly fair, strategy-proof and respects modular upper-bounds.
\end{thm}



\begin{example}[Distributing officers across two regions]\label{ex:example1}

Consider four states $S = \{s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4\}$, which are partitioned administratively into two regions: $R_1 = \{s_1,s_2\}\text{ and }R_2 = \{s_3,s_4\}$, and each \(s\in S\) having capacity \(q_s=2\). There are 8 officers \(I=\{i_1,\dots,i_8\}\), and an officer type is the region they are originally from: \(t_i\in\{R_1,R_2\}\). 

The policy objective is that at most 50\% of the jobs in a region are taken by local officers. For each \(r\in\{R_1,R_2\}\), the modular upper-bound is, therefore:
\[
H^r=\{(R_r,2)\},
\]
so that for an officer of type \(r\) the cap applies only to states in \(R_r\). For any given region $r$, denote by $\hat{r}$ the other region in $T$.

For an officer \(i\) of type \(r\), the upper-bound signature is
\[
H(s,r)=
\begin{cases}
\{(r,2)\}, & \text{if } s\in r,\\[1mm]
\varnothing, & \text{if } s\in \hat{r}.
\end{cases}
\]
Thus, the induced equivalence relation partitions \(S\) into two zones:
\[
z_1=\{s_1,s_2\},\quad
z_2=\{s_3,s_4\}.
\]

Officers therefore use the same zonal message space, where they submit complete rankings over states within each zone (without comparing states across \(z_1\) and \(z_2\)).

\emph{Exogenous zone ranking:} each officer of type~\(r\) has the fixed ordering



\[
\begin{array}{rl}
   z_1 \;\blacktriangleright_{i}\; z_2  & \text{if } t_i=R_1 \\
   z_2 \;\blacktriangleright_{i}\; z_1  & \text{if } t_i=R_2  
\end{array}
\]

so that the officer's own region’s zone is ranked above other states.

An allocation \(a\) is feasible if for every \(s\in S\), $|\{i: a_i=s\}|\le q_s$, and for each \(r\in\{R_1,R_2\}\), $|\{i: t_i=r \text{ and } a_i\in R_r\}|\le 2$.

A modular--priority mechanism enforcing these bounds proceeds as in Definition~\ref{def:modularPriorityMechanism}, using the zonal message spaces \(M_{R_1}\) and \(M_{R_2}\) and exogenous rankings over zones $\left(\blacktriangleright_{i}\right)_{i\in I}$.
\end{example}
\medskip

\begin{example}[Distributing doctors across regions and urban/rural divides]\label{ex:example2}
Consider nine hospitals $S = \{s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,s_5,s_6,s_7,s_8,s_9\}$ partitioned into regions 
\[
R_1 = \{s_1,s_2,s_3\},\quad R_2 = \{s_4,s_5,s_6\},\quad R_3 = \{s_7,s_8,s_9\},
\]
with each hospital having capacity \(q_s=4\).  In each \(R_i\), the first hospital is \emph{rural} (e.g.\ \(s_1,s_4,s_7\)) and the others are \emph{urban} (\(s_2,s_3,s_5,s_6,s_8,s_9\)).  There are 27 doctors, 9 of each type \(t\in\{1,2,3\}\), indicating their home region.  

Two types of modular upper‐bounds apply:
\[
H^{t} = \{(R_{t},6)\}
\quad\text{for each }t=1,2,3,
\quad\text{and}\quad
H^{\mathrm{U}} = \{(U,18)\}
\]
where 
\(
U=\{s_2,s_3,s_5,s_6,s_8,s_9\}
\)
is the set of all urban hospitals, and \((U,18)\) is understood to be a universal cap across all doctors.  

\medskip

A doctor of type~1, for instance, faces the following \emph{signatures}:
\[
H(s,1)=
\begin{cases}
\{(R_1,6)\}, & \text{if $s\in R_1$ is rural (here, $s_1$)},\\
\{(R_1,6),(U,18)\}, & \text{if $s\in R_1$ is urban (here, $s_2,s_3$)},\\
\{(U,18)\}, & \text{if $s$ is urban but not in $R_1$ (e.g.\ $s_5,s_6,s_8,s_9$)},\\
\varnothing, & \text{if $s$ is rural outside $R_1$ (e.g.\ $s_4,s_7$).}
\end{cases}
\]
Hence the modular‐induced partition for a type‐1 doctor is:
\[
Z_1=\{s_1\},\quad
Z_2=\{s_2,s_3\},\quad
Z_3=\{s_4,s_7\},\quad
Z_4=\{s_5,s_6,s_8,s_9\}.
\]
Within each zone, the doctor ranks hospitals \emph{fully}, yet makes no cross‐zone comparisons. Message spaces following the same principle are constructed for the remaining types.

\emph{Exogenous zone ranking:} each doctor of type~\(1\) has the fixed ordering 
\[
Z_2 \;\blacktriangleright_{i}\; Z_1\blacktriangleright_{i}\; Z_4\blacktriangleright_{i}\; Z_3,
\]
so that hospitals in her own region’s zone are ranked above other states, and urban hospitals are ranked above rural. Exogenous zone rankings for other types of doctors are defined analogously.

Any allocation must respect both hospital capacities and these upper‐bounds:
\[
\bigl|\{i:a_i=s\}\bigr|\le4,\quad
\bigl|\{i:t_i=t,\;a_i\in R_t\}\bigr|\le6,\quad
\bigl|\{i:a_i\in U\}\bigr|\le18.
\]

A modular‐priority mechanism enforces these caps by assigning each type‐\(t\) doctor to her top feasible hospital within the partition induced by
\((R_t,6)\) and \((U,18)\).

A modular--priority mechanism enforcing these caps proceeds as in Definition~\ref{def:modularPriorityMechanism}, using the zonal message spaces defined above and the exogenous rankings over zones $\left(\blacktriangleright_{i}\right)_{i\in I}$.
\end{example}\medskip


As noted previously, the specification of the exogenous rankings over zones \(\left(\blacktriangleright_{i}\right)_{i\in I}\) does not affect the theoretical properties established in our results. However, if we know that, for instance, most doctors typically prefer urban hospitals over rural ones, defining the rankings as in the examples above tends to improve efficiency of the final allocations, without harming the other objectives. Naturally, if the actual preferences of participants substantially diverge from such assumptions, employing this approach could lead to less desirable outcomes.

It is also worth noting that the mechanisms introduced above---and the overarching notion of visible fairness---are ill-suited to implementing \emph{within}-state affirmative action quotas. Such policies, which often appear in the matching literature on diversity (e.g.\ majority quotas or type-specific ceilings in each state), could be encoded as modular upper-bounds with one state in each upper-bound. However, a scenario in which, for example, each state can admit a maximum number of agents of a certain type, the resulting zonal message space would have one state per zone. This, of course, would completely eliminate role of preferences in the allocation. If we attempted to compensate this by using zonal message spaces with ranking over zones, we would easily conclude that there is no visibly fair mechanism with these characteristics that would respect upper bounds.


\section{Efficiency}

As the following example demonstrates, the Modular Prioritized Allocation Mechanism does not guarantee efficiency.

\begin{example}\label{ex:motivation_formal}
Consider two officers $I = \{i_1, i_2\}$ of the same type $t$, and two states $S = \{s_1, s_2\}$ with capacities $q_{s_1} = 2$ and $q_{s_2} = 1$. Let there be a modular upper-bound of one officer of type $t$ allowed in $s_1$. Thus, the modular-induced message space partitions states into two zones: $z_1 = \{s_1\}$ and $z_2 = \{s_2\}$. Let the exogenous ranking of zones be $z_1 \blacktriangleright_i z_2$ for both officers.

Suppose that officers' true preferences are as follows:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\succ_{i_1}: \quad & s_2 \succ s_1, \\
\succ_{i_2}: \quad & s_1 \succ s_2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Under the Modular Prioritized Allocation Mechanism, the resulting allocation is
$a = (a_{i_1}, a_{i_2}) = (s_1, s_2)$.
\end{example}\medskip

\medskip

Example~\ref{ex:motivation_formal} shows that it is possible to improve the efficiency of a mechanism that remains visibly fair and satisfies all modular upper-bounds. Specifically, because officer $i_1$'s assignment to either $s_1$ or $s_2$ does not violate any bounds, it is unnecessary to separate those two states into different zones in $i_1$'s message space. Allowing the officer to place both states in the same zone incorporates her actual preferences more fully, thus leading to a more efficient assignment.

We now introduce a ``second-best'' notion of efficiency tailored to settings with modular upper-bounds.


\begin{defi}\label{def:ConstrainedPE}
An allocation \(a\) is \textbf{constrained Pareto efficient} if:
\begin{enumerate}[i)]
    \item \(a\) is feasible and respects the upper--bounds, and
    \item there is no other allocation \(a'\) that is feasible and respects the modular upper--bounds such that for every officer \(i\) with \(a'_i \neq a_i\) we have $a'_i \succ_i a_i$. 
\end{enumerate}
 A mechanism is constrained Pareto efficient if, for any problem, when agents submit truthful messages the outcome is constrained Pareto efficient.
\end{defi}


In other words, an allocation is {constrained Pareto efficient} if there is no alternative allocation that respects both capacity and modular upper-bound constraints and strictly Pareto improves upon $a$ (with respect to the agents' true preferences). A mechanism is constrained Pareto efficient if, whenever agents submit truthful messages, the resulting allocation is constrained Pareto efficient \emph{under their full (true) preferences}. The following result demonstrates that, in general, no static mechanism can simultaneously achieve constrained Pareto efficiency and visible fairness.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:NoMechanismAllThree}
There might be no static mechanism that is \emph{visibly fair} and \emph{constrained Pareto efficient}.
\end{thm}


Theorem~\ref{thm:NoMechanismAllThree} underscores a key tension in designing visibly fair mechanisms that must also adhere to constraints such as modular upper-bounds. At its core, this tension arises from the message-space design: enforcing both fairness and quota requirements demands that the space be curated to preempt scenarios where certain preference reports would necessarily violate upper-bounds. Consequently, to avoid such violations, the mechanism must collect more restricted preference information than might otherwise be desirable. This reduction in elicited information, while upholding fairness and preserving the bounds, can lead to efficiency losses because the mechanism may lack the information needed to detect and implement mutually beneficial reallocations that remain compliant with all constraints.

Nonetheless, if one can condition each officer's reported preferences on the assignments of higher-ranked officers, it becomes possible to obtain second-best outcomes. We exploit this insight to propose a dynamic mechanism, described next.


\begin{defi}
\label{def:DynamicModularPriorityMech}
The \textbf{Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism} \( \psi \) operates as follows:

\vspace{1em}

\noindent \textbf{Initialization:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item For each officer \( i \in I \), set \( a^0_i = \emptyset \).
    \item For each type \( t \in T \), initialize the set of binding modular upper-bounds \( B^t_0 =\emptyset\).
\end{itemize}

\vspace{1em}

\noindent \textbf{Sequential Assignment:} Process the officers in the order \((i_1,\dots,i_n)\) where \(\pi(i_1) < \cdots < \pi(i_n)\). For each \(k=1,\dots,n\):

\begin{enumerate}

    \item Let $M_{i_k}$ be a zonal message space that partitions $S$ into two zones:

    $$z_1 \;=\; \left\{ s \in S : \left(\forall\, (S_h^{t_{i_k}}, k_h^{t_{i_k}}) \in B_k^{t_{i_k}},\; s \notin S_h^{t_{i_k}}\right)
\quad \text{and} \quad 
\left|\{ j < k : a_j = s \}\right| < q_s \right\}$$
    $$z_2=S\backslash z_1$$

    \item Elicit from officer $i_k$ a message $m_k\in M_{i_k}$.
    \item Let $a_{i_k}$ be officer \( i_k \)'s most-preferred state \( s^\ast \in z_1 \), according to \( m_{i_k} \). Notice that this can be $\emptyset$ if $z_1=\emptyset$.

    \item Update Binding Upper-Bounds:

        \begin{itemize}
            \item Let $B^t_{k+1}=B^t_{k}$
            \item For each upper-bound \( h=(S_h^{t_{i_k}}, k_h^{t_{i_k}}) \) involving type \( t_{i_k} \):

            \begin{itemize}
                \item If \( s^\ast \in S_h^{t_{i_k}} \):

                \begin{itemize}
                    \item Calculate:
                    \[
                        N_h = \left| \left\{ i \in I : a^{k}_i \in S_h^{t_{i_k}},\ t_i = t_{i_k} \right\} \right|.
                    \]

                    \item If \( N_h = k_h^{t_{i_k}} \):

                    \begin{itemize}
                        \item Add \( (S_h^{t_{i_k}}, k_h^{t_{i_k}}) \) to $B^t_{k+1}$
                    \end{itemize}
                \end{itemize}
            \end{itemize}
        \end{itemize}

        \item Proceed to the next officer \( i_{k+1} \).

    \end{enumerate}




\medskip

\noindent \textbf{Outcome:}  
After processing all officers $i_1,\ldots,i_n$, the assignment $a^n=(a^n_i)_{i\in I}$ is the mechanism’s final allocation:
\[
  \psi\bigl(m\bigr) \;=\; a^n.
\]
\end{defi}

The Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism, therefore, sequentially assigns officers, dynamically tailoring the menu of available states for each officer based on previous assignments. At each officer’s turn, the mechanism constructs a zone containing all states with remaining capacity that do not belong to any subset of states where a modular upper-bound for the officer’s type has already become binding. The officer is then matched to her most-preferred state among these feasible alternatives. By construction, this ensures visible fairness since each officer always obtains her top choice from that zone. Furthermore, by dynamically adjusting the zones and enabling officers to fully express their preferences within these constraints, the mechanism attains constrained efficiency: any allocation that improves an officer’s assignment without harming others would necessarily violate at least one modular upper-bound. The following proposition summarizes these properties, including incentives.\footnote{While the definitions of visible fairness and respecting modular upper bounds carry over directly without introducing additional notation, strategy-proofness warrants further clarification in our dynamic setting. Because the Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism allows officers’ strategies to depend on both past history and the structure of the message spaces (i.e., the composition of the zones), our original, static definition of strategy-proofness does not explicitly cover this complexity. Nevertheless, the informal argument used in the proof of Theorem~\ref{dynamicModPriorityMechVisiblyFairModularAndSP} presented in Appendix A remains sound: no matter how other players behave over time, an officer cannot secure a better outcome by misreporting, so truth-telling continues to be a dominant strategy.}

\begin{thm}
\label{dynamicModPriorityMechVisiblyFairModularAndSP}
    The Dynamic Modular Priority Mechanism is visibly fair, constrained Pareto efficient, strategy-proof, and respects modular upper-bounds. 

\end{thm}










\section{Conclusion}
We explored how designing mechanisms that restrict the preferences participants can report can help reconcile multiple policy objectives—particularly distributional constraints—with fairness principles grounded in strict priority orders. We introduced the concept of visible fairness, in which a mechanism never produces an allocation that appears to violate a participant’s priority based on the partially observed preferences. Our main theoretical result (Theorem 1) demonstrated that every visibly fair mechanism operates as a variant of serial dictatorship adapted to partial preference orders. We further showed that this framework can accommodate diverse distributional objectives by employing modular upper-bounds, which limit how many individuals of certain types can be placed in specified subsets of positions. Central to this approach are modular-induced message spaces, which prevent participants from specifying cross-group preference comparisons that would otherwise undermine these quotas.

We then characterized how these mechanisms can be made incentive-compatible by parsing out two critical conditions—expressiveness and (weak) availability—that together ensure no participant can profit by misreporting her partial preferences. While restricting the scope of preference reporting can, in principle, worsen efficiency, we introduced a dynamic modular-priority framework that sequentially elicits partial preferences and updates feasibility constraints in real time, thus recovering the best possible Pareto outcomes subject to capacity and distributional limits. Overall, this paper highlights how deliberately constraining participants’ message spaces provides mechanism designers with new levers for achieving desired policy goals while preserving fairness and incentive properties in priority-based allocation settings


