\subsection{Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) Mechanism }

\cite{sonmez2013bidding}'s model of cadet-branch matching problem consists of
\begin{enumerate}
    \item a finite set of cadets $I = \{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_n\}$,
    \item a finite set of branches $B = \{b_1, b_2, \dots, b_m\}$,
    \item a vector of branch capacities $q = (q_b)_{b \in B}$,
    \item a set of ``terms" $T = \{t_1, \dots, t_k\}$,
    \item a list of cadet preferences $P = (P_i)_{i \in I}$ over $(B \times T) \cup \{\emptyset\}$, and
    \item a list of base priority rankings $\pi = (\pi_b)_{b \in B}$.
\end{enumerate}

The ROTC mechanism is not direct. Instead, each cadet submits a ranking of branches $\succ_i'$, and he can sign a branch-of-choice contract for any of his top three choices under $\succ_i'$
