\subsection{Tensor models}
\label{sec:opentensor}

In section \ref{sec:subexpon}, we discussed a model where GUE or Haar 
unitary matrices act on disjoint factors of a tensor product.
A natural generalization 
of this model allows the random matrices to interact.
As the simplest example of such a model, given 
independent GUE matrices $X_1^N,\ldots,X_r^N$ of dimension $N^2$, 
define the random matrices of dimension $N^{r+1}$ as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tensortoy}
	\hat X_i^N = \id^{\otimes (i-1)}\otimes
	X_i^N \otimes \id^{\otimes (r-i)}.
\end{equation}
Thus $\hat X_i^N$ may be viewed as a GUE matrix that acts on the $i,i+1$ 
tensor factors of $(\mathbb{C}^N)^{\otimes (r+1)}$. More generally, 
a tensor model with index set $V$ consists of any collection of 
independent random matrices of the appropriate dimensions that act on 
arbitrary subsets $I\subseteq V$ of the tensor factors of 
$(\mathbb{C}^N)^{\otimes V}$. 

Tensor models arise in the study of quantum many-body systems 
\cite{ML19,CY24} and as random matrix models of right-angled Artin groups 
\cite{MT23}. Charlesworth and Collins \cite{CC21} showed that 
they converge weakly to a limiting model that is described by the notion 
of graph-independence \cite{SW16}. The strong convergence 
problem was recently settled for tensor GUE models by Chen, Garza-Vargas 
and the author \cite[\S 9.4]{CGV25} by an iterated application of the 
polynomial method; the analogous result for Haar unitaries can be deduced 
by the method of \cite{CM14}. However, 
strong convergence of tensor models based on random permutation matrices 
remains open.

Another question which is motivated by physical considerations \cite[\S 
5]{CY24} is what happens if one takes the random matrices that act on 
different tensor factors to be defined by the \emph{same} GUE matrix, 
rather than by independent GUE matrices. In this case, even weak 
convergence has not been investigated systematically.

