\subsubsection{Communication Infrastructure}

The thermal-fluid integration of the testbed relies on a robust communication infrastructure that bridges the physical facility with computational models and AI systems. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} illustrates the complete system topology, encompassing sensor networks, communication protocols, and computational components that enable real-time synchronization between physical and virtual domains.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Figure2.jpg}
    \caption{(a) Integrated thermal-fluid architecture showing the physical facility, OPC-UA communication infrastructure, SAM-based digital twin, and AI framework integration, and (b) schematic communication and control pathway within the testbed.}
    \label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

The communication backbone employs OPC-UA (Open Platform Communications Unified Architecture) protocol, selected for its deterministic performance, built-in security features, and widespread adoption in industrial automation. The OPC-UA server, implemented using the open62541 library, establishes a unified namespace encompassing all sensor readings, actuator states, and control variables. This server maintains a sampling rate of \SI{10}{\hertz} for critical parameters (temperatures, flow rates, heater power) and \SI{1}{\hertz} for auxiliary measurements, ensuring adequate temporal resolution while minimizing network overhead.

External system integration utilizes OPC-UA's service-oriented architecture, enabling seamless connection with Python-based control algorithms, machine learning models, and digital twin implementations. The Python control server, developed using the asyncua library, serves as the central intelligence hub, maintaining the \SI{100}{\milli\second} control loop timing required for stable operation while enabling integration of advanced algorithms. The PLC retains essential safety interlocks and low-level device control, with complex decision-making delegated to higher-level systems.

