\section{Linear Lambda Calculus}
\label{sec:case-study:linear}
Our primary case study builds entirely on a standard, intuitionistic substitution calculus.
To demonstrate Pyrosome's expressiveness, we also verify a CPS pass analogous to our simply typed one
from a separate, linear lambda calculus to a similarly linear continuation calculus.
The linear substitution calculus is similar to the intuitionistic one, including retaining all rules in \autoref{fig:subst},
but it restricts substitutions and variables to prohibit weakening.
We show the rules for the linear functions extension in \autoref{fig:linear}.
These rules are similar to those of STLC, but in the rules with more than one expression or value,
the context is broken up into multiple (disjoint) pieces.
This separation continues in the substitution rule for application, where the left-hand substitution must break into two parts, one for $e$ and one for $e'$.
Just like with the intuitionistic substitution calculus,
we programmatically generate the last two substitution rules for linear extensions.

The CPS pass itself is actually almost identical to the CPS pass for STLC,
with the only difference being the managing of explicit uses of the exchange rule
as the mechanization is de Bruijn indexed.
This case study currently has more manual proofs since our automation is not yet sufficient to
handle environment splitting without user input, but it still benefits from the metatheory
and principled subgoal management Pyrosome provides.

