\section*{Notations}
\noindent In this section we define some notations that we will be used throughout the article.
\begin{itemize}
\item \(\measspace^+(\mathcal{X})\) is the set of finite positive measures defined on \(\mathcal{X}\).
\item \(\inp{\cdot}{\cdot}\) is the inner product associated with the Euclidean norm \(\norm{\cdot}\) in \(\R^N\).
\item \(\preceq\) stands for a component-wise inequality.
\item \(\lesssim\) stands for \(\leq\) up to a multiplicative universal positive constant. 
\item \(\Delta_N\) is the \((N\!-\!1)\)-dimensional simplex of \(\R^N\) defined by
    \[\Delta_N \defeq \set*{z \in (\R_+)^{N}}{\sum_{i=1}^N z_i = 1}.\]
\item \(\sqcup\) stands for the disjoint union.
\end{itemize}

\

\section{Introduction}
Given two probability measures \(\mu\) and \(\nu\) on compact domains \(\mathcal X \subset \R^d\) and \(\mathcal Y\subset \R^d\) and a cost function \(c\!:\mathcal X\!\times\!\mathcal Y\to\R_+\), the Monge-Kantorovich optimal transport problem consists in solving
\begin{equation}
\label{intro:ot}
\inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu , \nu)} \int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(x,y)\dd\gamma(x,y) \end{equation}
where \(\Gamma(\mu,\nu)\) denotes the set of all probability measures on the product space \(\mathcal X\!\times\!\mathcal Y\) having \(\mu\) and \(\nu\) as marginals.
A popular way to solve numerically this problem consists in adding an entropy regularizing term, tuned via a nonnegative parameter \(t\), which leads to the following \emph{entropic optimal transport} problem
\begin{equation}
\label{intro:entropic}
\inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu , \nu)} t \left(\int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(x,y)\dd\gamma(x,y)\right) + \entropy(\gamma \lvert \mu\! \otimes\! \nu), \end{equation}
where \(\entropy\) is the relative entropy. In the literature, one often finds a parameter \(\varepsilon \geq 0\) placed in front of the entropy rather than in front of the cost function. In this case, the focus is on the limit as \(\varepsilon\) vanishes. These two formulations are equivalent as long as the parameters remain positive, corresponding in our case to \(t^{-1}=\varepsilon\) and letting \(t\to +\infty\).

Over the last decade this kind of approach has witnessed an impressive increasing interest since it has proved to be an efficient way to approximate OT problems. From the computational point of view, \labelcref{intro:entropic} can be solved by an iterative projections method, which turns out to correspond to the celebrated Sinkhorn's algorithm \cite{sinkhorn1967diagonal,sinkhornRelationshipArbitraryPositive1964}, successfully developed in the pioneering works \cite{cuturi2013sinkhorn,Galichon-Entropic,benamouIterativeBregmanProjections2015}
The simplicity of the implementation and the good convergence guarantees \cite{franklinScalingMultidimensionalMatrices1989, marinoOptimalTransportApproach2020} determined the success of the entropic optimal transport for a wide range of applications, see for instance \cite{peyre2019computational} and the references therein.
Notice now if we look at \labelcref{intro:entropic} as a perturbation of \labelcref{intro:ot}, then it is quite natural to study the behavior of both optimal values and minimizers, when the regularization parameter varies, and in particular when \(t\to+\infty\).
According to this let us mention some related works \cite{mikami1990variational,leonard2012schrodinger,leonardSurveySchrodingerProblem2014,carlierConvergenceEntropicSchemes2017}, which established \(\Gamma\)-convergence results of \labelcref{intro:entropic} to \labelcref{intro:ot}, \cite{pal2019difference,conforti2021formula,altschuler2022asymptotics,ecksteinConvergenceRatesRegularized2022,carlier2023convergence,malamut2023convergence,nenna2024convergence}, which provided asymptotic expansion for the cost. Here we want to focus on the convergence rate of the primal and dual variable in the spirit of \cite{cominetti1994asymptotic,weed2018explicit} in the discrete setting for a generalized OT problem: unbalanced OT.

By definition, classical optimal transport requires the two given measures to have the same total mass, which can be a limitation for certain practical applications, particularly in machine learning, or imaging. An extension of the classical framework, known as unbalanced optimal transport, addresses this limitation by relaxing the marginal constraints and incorporating a divergence term into the objective function to penalise the marginals. It was simultaneously introduced by \cite{chizat2018unbalanced,liero2018optimal,mons2016} and may be expressed in the following form, 
\begin{equation}\label{pb:uot}
 \boxed{\inf\limits_{\gamma \in \measspace^+(\mathcal{X}\times \mathcal{Y})} \int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(x,y)\dd\gamma(x,y) + \mathrm{D}_\phi(\gamma_1 \lvert \mu)+\mathrm{D}_\phi(\gamma_2 \lvert \nu)}, 
\end{equation}
where \(\gamma_i\) is the \(i\)-th marginal of the coupling \(\gamma\) and \(\mathrm{D}_\phi\) is a \(\phi\)-divergence, also known as Csiszàr divergence (see \Cref{Csiszar_divergence discrete}).

As in the usual optimal transport problem, one can introduce an entropy regularized version of the unbalanced optimal transport problem, as follows
\begin{equation}\label{pb:entro_uot}
 \boxed{\inf_{\gamma \in \measspace^+(\mathcal{X}\times \mathcal{Y})} t\left( \int_{\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}}c(x,y)\dd\gamma(x,y) + \mathrm{D}_\phi(\gamma_1 \lvert \mu)+ \mathrm{D}_\phi(\gamma_2 \lvert \nu)\right) +\entropy(\gamma|\mu\!\otimes\!\nu)},
\end{equation}

This paper focuses on this problem when both measures have finite support. The aim is to establish convergence rates of the regularized optimal variables, for the primal and dual problems, towards the original ones. The main contributions can be summarized in the following two theorems.
\begin{theoremA} (Simplified statement of \Cref{main theorem}) \\
For positive \(t\), let \(t\mapsto\gamma(t)\) be the curve of optimal solutions to the regularized unbalanced optimal transport problem \labelcref{pb:entro_uot}.
Under the assumption that \(\mu\) and \(\nu\) are atomic measures, and given certain conditions, the trajectory \(t\mapsto \gamma(t)\) converges towards an optimizer \(\gamma^*\) of \labelcref{pb:uot} at a rate of at least \(1/\sqrt{t}\), i.e.
        \[\lVert \gamma^* - \gamma(t) \rVert \lesssim \frac{1}{\sqrt{t}}.\]
\end{theoremA}
\begin{theoremB} (Simplified statement of \Cref{main theorem 2}) \\
For positive \(t\), let  \(t\mapsto\xi(t)\) be the curve of optimal solutions to the regularized dual problem to \labelcref{pb:entro_uot}.
Under the assumption that \(\mu\) and \(\nu\) are atomic measures, and given certain conditions, the dual trajectory \(t\mapsto \xi(t)\) converges towards the dual optimizer \(\xi^*\) at a rate of at least \(1/t\), i.e.
        \[\lVert \xi^* - \xi(t) \rVert \lesssim \frac{1}{t}.\]
\end{theoremB}

\paragraph{Outline of the paper.} 
The paper is organized as follows: in \cref{sec:properties} we introduce the discrete balanced and unbalanced optimal transport problems, their dual problems, and their regularized counterparts. In particular we focus on the dual unbalanced problem and give some preliminary results. \Cref{sec:convergence} is devoted to the convergence of both the primal and the dual trajectory of solutions to the regularized unbalanced OT. In \cref{sec:asymptotics} we provide an asymptotic analysis of the regularized problem when the regularization parameter tends to \(+\infty\). \Cref{sec:numerics} is devoted to numerical experiments and comparisons with the established asymptotics.

\section{Discrete unbalanced optimal transport }

\subsection{The setting}

Since we study the case of measures \(\mu,\nu\) with finite support, we assume from now on that \(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{Y}\) are finite sets. Notice that measures \(\mu \in \measspace^+(\mathcal X)\),  \(\nu \in \measspace^+(\mathcal Y)\) and \(\gamma \in \measspace^+(\mathcal X\times \mathcal Y)\) can now be written as
\[\mu=\sum_{x\in\mathcal X}\mu_x\delta_x, \quad \nu=\sum_{y\in\mathcal Y}\nu_y\delta_y,\quad\text{and}\quad \gamma =\! \sum_{(x,y) \in \mathcal X \!\times\! \mathcal Y} \! \gamma_{x,y} \delta_{(x,y)},\]
and can thus be identified with their respective weight vectors \((\mu_x)_x\in \R^{\mathcal X}\), \((\nu_y)_y \in \R^{\mathcal Y}\) and \((\gamma_{x,y})_{(x,y)} \in \R^{\mathcal X\times \mathcal Y}\). In the same way, the cost \(c\) can be identified with the vector \((c(x,y))_{(x,y)} \in \R^{\mathcal X\times \mathcal Y}\). By introducing the linear map \(A\!: \R^{\mathcal{X}\times \mathcal{Y}} \to \R^{\mathcal{X} } \! \oplus \mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{Y}} \) defined by
\[A\gamma  = \left( \left(\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}} \gamma_{x,y} \right)_{x\in\mathcal{X}} , \left(\sum_{x\in\mathcal{X}} \gamma_{x,y} \right)_{y\in\mathcal{Y}} \right),\]
one can re-formulated the constraint \(\gamma\in \Gamma(\mu , \nu) \) as \(A\gamma = (\mu,\nu) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=} q\) and the optimal transport problem and its regularized formulation can then be expressed as finite-dimensional problems as follows,
\begin{equation}
\label{DOT}
    \inf_{\gamma \in(\mathbb{R}_+)^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}}  \left\{ \langle c \vert \gamma \rangle \: \vert \: A\gamma = q \right\} \tag{\(\text{OT}\)},
\end{equation}
and for any non-negative $t$,
\begin{equation}
\label{DEOT_t}
    \inf_{\gamma \in(\mathbb{R}_+)^{\mathcal{X}}} \left\{ t\langle c | \gamma \rangle + \entropy(\gamma) \: \vert \: A\gamma = q \right\}, \tag{\(\text{EOT}_t\)}
\end{equation}
where \(\langle c | \gamma \rangle \defeq \sum_{(x,y)\in \mathcal X\!\times\!\mathcal Y}c(x,y)\gamma_{x,y}\) denotes the canonical inner product on \(\R^{\mathcal X\!\times\! \mathcal Y}\) and \(\entropy\!:\R^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}\!\to\!\R\) is the discrete entropy, i.e. the entropy relative with respect to the counting measure $\mathcal H^0$ defined by 
\[
\entropy(\gamma) \defeq \entropy(\gamma \vert \mathcal H^0 ) = \begin{dcases*}
\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}} \gamma_{x,y} (\log \gamma_{x,y} - 1)& if \(\gamma_{x,y} \geq 0\;\text{for every }(x,y)\)\\
 +\infty& otherwise,
\end{dcases*}
\]
with a slight abuse of notation.
\begin{remark}
      We take the convention that \(0 \log 0 = 0\) providing a continuous extension of \(t \mapsto t \log t\) on \(\R_+\).
\end{remark}
Notice that, in the case where \(\mu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X)}\) and \(\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y)}\), the entropy relative with respect to the product measure \(\mu\otimes\!\nu\) and the discrete entropy satisfy the following relation:
\[\inf \labelcref{DEOT_t} = \inf \labelcref{intro:entropic} - \entropy(\mu) - \entropy(\nu) - 2 .\]
\noindent 
One can also express problems \labelcref{DOT} and \labelcref{DEOT_t} respectively as follows,
\[
   \inf_{\gamma\in (\R_+)^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}}\left\{  \langle c | \gamma \rangle\;|\;A \gamma = q \right\}\quad\text{and}\quad
   \inf_{\gamma\in \R^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ t\langle c | \gamma \rangle + \entropy(\gamma)\;|\; A \gamma = q \right\}.
\]

\begin{remark}
Assuming that \(\mathcal{X}\) and \(\mathcal{Y}\) are finite subsets of \(\R^d\), say \(\mathcal{X}=\{x_1,\cdots,x_N\}\) and \(\mathcal{Y}=\{y_1,\cdots,y_M\}\), the linear map \(A\) can be associated with its matrix \(A_{mat}\) in the bases \( \mathcal{B}=(e_{x_1,y_1} , e_{x_1,y_2} , \cdots , e_{x_n,y_M}) \) and \( \mathcal{B}'=(e_{x_1} , \cdots , e_{x_n} , e_{y_1} , \cdots e_{y_M}) \) defined as
\[A_{mat} =
\begin{array}{cc} 
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbb{1}_M & 0_M & \cdots & 0_M &\quad  \textnormal{I}_M \quad \\
0_M & \mathbb{1}_M & \cdots & 0_M & \textnormal{I}_M \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots &\quad \vdots \quad \\
0_M & 0_M & \cdots & \mathbb{1}_M &\quad \textnormal{I}_M\quad  \\
\end{pmatrix}
\end{array}^T.\]
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}
\label{link EOT dual}
For any \(t>0\), \labelcref{DEOT_t} and its dual admit a unique solution \(\gamma_t\) and \(\xi_t\) (up to the kernel of $A^*$, which has dimension $1$, for $\xi_t$), respectively. Moreover,  the following relation holds
\[\gamma_t = e^{t(A^*\xi_t - c )}.\]
\end{proposition}
\noindent We will not detail the proof since it is well known in the literature (for instance it is a by-product of the result in \cite{peyre2019computational}).

\indent The unbalanced optimal transport problem is a natural extension of classical optimal transport problem where one can consider measures of different masses by relaxing the mass conservation constraint, for more details we refer the reader to the seminal works \cite{chizat2018unbalanced,chizat2018interpolating,liero2018optimal,mons2016}. This approach, in particular, allows the creation or destruction of mass. To formalize this problem, we introduce a function \(\F\!:\R^\mathcal{X}\!\oplus\R^\mathcal{Y}\!\to\!\R \cup\{+\infty\}\) which is used as a penalization on the marginals of the transport coupling. The unbalanced optimal transport problem then reads as:
\begin{equation}
    \label{UOT}
    \boxed{\inf_{\gamma\in(\R_+)^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ \langle c | \gamma \rangle + \F(A\gamma)  \right \}}. \tag{\(\text{UOT}\)}
\end{equation}


\noindent In the same way as before, we consider a regularized version of this optimization problem which is given, for any non-negative \(t\), by

\[\inf_{\gamma\in\R^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ t\langle c | \gamma \rangle + t \F(A\gamma) + \entropy(\gamma)   \right \}. 
\]
For practical reasons, it will often be more convenient to work with its re-scaled version, defined for any positive $t$ as
\begin{equation}
    \label{UOT_t rescaled}
    \boxed{\inf_{\gamma\in \R^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ \langle c | \gamma \rangle + \F(A\gamma) + \frac{1}{t}\entropy(\gamma)   \right \} }.\tag{\(\text{UOT}_t\)}
\end{equation}
For any positive \(t\), we denote by \(\mathscr{C}_t\) the functional of the regularized unbalanced optimal transport problem, i.e.,
\[\mathscr{C}_t \defeq \langle c \vert \gamma \rangle + \F(A\gamma) + \frac{1}{t}\entropy(\gamma). \]


Throughout all this paper we will use the following assumptions for \(\F\):
\begin{enumerate}[(H1)]
    \item \label{H1}\(\F\) is a proper convex and lower semi-continuous function;
    \item \label{H2} There exists \((\gamma_1,\gamma_2) \in (\R_+^*)^{\mathcal X}\! \oplus (\R_+^*)^{\mathcal Y}\) such that  \(\F((\gamma_1,\gamma_2)) < +\infty\).
\end{enumerate}

In the literature, for instance \cite{sejourne2022faster}, \(\F\) is often taken to be a Csiszàr \(\phi\)-divergence defined as follows.
\begin{definition}
\label{Csiszar_divergence discrete} (Discrete Csiszàr \(\phi\)-divergence) \\
Let \(p,\,q\in(\R_+)^N\) be two non-negative vectors and \(\phi\!:\R \to \R_+ \cup \{+\infty\}\) an entropy function meaning that it is a convex and lower semi-continuous function such that \(\phi(1)=0\). The discrete Csiszàr \(\phi\)-divergence is defined as
\begin{equation*}
    \mathrm{D}_\phi(p \: | \: q) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=} \sum_{i\,:\,q_i>0}q_i\phi\bigg(\frac{p_i}{q_i}\bigg)+\phi'_\infty\sum_{i\,:\,q_i=0}p_i,
\end{equation*}
where \(\phi_\infty'\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\lim_{t\to\infty}\phi(t)/t\) is the recession constant.
\end{definition}
\begin{remark}
    The choice of considering a general function \(\F\) instead of the Csiszàr divergence is motivated by the fact that one can also consider  variational problems given by the sum of an optimal transport term and a congestion on the marginal. For this reason, in the following, we are going to make assumptions only on the function \(\F\) that one can easily translate as assumptions on the entropy function \(\phi\) when \(\F(\cdot)=\mathrm{D}_\phi(\cdot \vert q)\).
\end{remark}


\begin{remark}
In the case where \( \F(\cdot) = \mathrm{D}_\phi(\cdot \vert q) \) with fixed \( q \), assuming that \( \phi \) is a proper, convex, and lower semi-continuous function is sufficient to ensure \cref{H1}. The existence of some \( \alpha > 0 \) such that \(\phi(\alpha)<+\infty\) guarantees \cref{H2}. Indeed, for any \( x \in \mathcal{X} \), define
\[
\gamma_1^x =
\begin{cases}
\mu_x \alpha & \text{if } \mu_x > 0, \\
\alpha & \text{if } \mu_x = 0,
\end{cases}
\]
and build \( \gamma_2 \) similarly. This ensures that \( (\gamma_1, \gamma_2) \in (\mathbb{R}_+^*)^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus (\mathbb{R}_+^*)^{\mathcal{Y}} \) and that \(
\F((\gamma_1, \gamma_2)) < +\infty\). Moreover, if \( \phi \) is also superlinear (see \cref{def: superlinear}), which implies that \( \phi'_\infty = +\infty \), then it is necessary that \(
q \in (\mathbb{R}_+^*)^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus (\mathbb{R}_+^*)^{\mathcal{Y}}\).
\end{remark}
\pap{Découper en 2 remarques pour les 2 jeux d'hypothèses : traduction des hypothèses sur $F$ en hypothèses sur $\phi$}


\subsection{Properties of the regularized unbalanced optimal transport}\label{sec:properties}
\pap{Changer titre ?}

Before studying in more details the properties of the solutions let us give some results concerning the primal and dual unbalanced problems. We introduce \(A^*:\R^{\mathcal{X}} \! \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}} \to \R^{\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}\) as the adjoint operator of \(A\) defined for any \(\xi=(\varphi , \psi ) \in \R^{\mathcal{X}}\!\oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}} \) as
\[A^*\xi = \varphi \oplus \psi = (\varphi_x + \psi_y)_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}.\]
We start our analysis by introducing the dual formulations of \labelcref{UOT} and \labelcref{UOT_t rescaled}.

\begin{proposition}
    Under assumptions \(\textnormal{\cref{H1}}\) and \(\textnormal{\cref{H2}}\), the dual of \labelcref{UOT} is given by       
    \begin{equation}
    \label{Dual UOT}
    \min_{\xi\in \R^{\mathcal{X}}\!\oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ \F^*\!\left(-\xi \right) \: | \: A^*\xi \preceq c \right\}. \tag{\(\text{Dual}\)}
    \end{equation}
Moreover, if \(\F\) is assumed to be coercive then strong duality holds, that is
\[\min_{\xi\in \R^{\mathcal{X}}\!\oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}}} \left\{ \F^*\!\left(-\xi \right) \: | \: A^*\xi \preceq c \right\} = - \min_{ \gamma\in \R^{\mathcal{X} \!\times\! \mathcal{Y}}} \langle c \vert \gamma \rangle + F(A\gamma).
\]
\end{proposition}
\pap{Étendre pour dire : dualité (+existence dual), existence au primal (si $F$ coercive).

Preuve de dualité par Rockafellar, preuve d'existence primal par compacité


Une remarque après sur l'unicité ? }

\begin{proof}
We begin by introducing the function \( G: \R^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \to \R \) defined by
\[
G(\gamma) = \langle c, \gamma \rangle + \iota_{(\R_+)^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}}}(\gamma),
\]
where \( \iota_{(\R_+)^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}}} \) denotes the indicator function of the non-negative orthant in \( \R^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \). With this definition, the unbalanced optimal transport \labelcref{UOT} problem can be written as
\[
\inf_{\gamma \in \R^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}}} G(\gamma) + \F(A\gamma).
\]
By the Fenchel–Rockafellar duality theorem, strong duality holds, and there exists a solution to the dual problem, which is given by
\[
\sup_{\xi \in \R^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}}} -G^*(A^* \xi) - \F^*(-\xi) = \max_{\xi \in \R^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}}} -G^*(A^* \xi) - \F^*(-\xi),
\]
where \( G^* \) and \( \F^* \) are the Legendre–Fenchel transforms of \( G \) and \( \F \), respectively.
Now, assume that \( \F \) is coercive. Consider a sequence \( (\gamma^n)_{n \in \N} \) in \( \R^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \) such that \( \|\gamma^n\| \to +\infty \) as \( n \to +\infty \). Since \( c \in (\R_+)^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \), we have
\[
\langle c, \gamma^n \rangle + \F(A\gamma^n) \geq \F(A\gamma^n).
\]
Moreover, because \( A \) is injective (i.e., \( \ker A = \{0\} \)), it follows that \( \|A\gamma^n\| \to +\infty \), and hence the coercivity of \( \F \) implies
\[
\F(A\gamma^n) \to +\infty.
\]
Therefore, the objective functional is coercive, and it follows that the primal problem admits a solution.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
\label{I_0 and slack variable}
    Take $\varphi$ and $\psi$ solution to \labelcref{Dual UOT} then we denote by \(I_0\)  the set of saturated constraint,
    \[I_0 \stackrel{\text{def}}{=} \{ (x,y) \!\in\! \mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y} \:|\:  \varphi_x^* + \psi_y^* = c(x,y)\}.\]
The slack variable \(\kappa\) is the gap between \(A^*\xi^*\) and \(c\). For any \((x,y) \!\in\! \mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}\) we define it as, \[\kappa_{x,y} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=} c(x,y) - \varphi_x^* - \psi_y^*.\]
\end{definition}


\begin{proposition}
\label{prop: existence for regularized problems}
For any positive \(t\), and under assumptions \textnormal{\cref{H1}} and \textnormal{\cref{H2}}, the problem \labelcref{UOT_t rescaled} admits a unique solution. Moreover, strong duality holds, and the corresponding dual problem is given by
\begin{equation}
\label{Dual UOT_t}
\min_{\xi \in \R^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}}} \, \F^*(-\xi) + \sum_{(x,y) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \frac{1}{t} \exp\left( t \langle A^* \xi - c \vert e_{x,y} \rangle \right), \tag{\(\text{Dual}_t\)}
\end{equation}
where \( (e_{x,y})_{(x,y) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \) denotes the canonical basis of \( \mathbb{R}^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \). The dual problem admits a solution, and if \( \F^* \) is strictly convex, then it is unique.
\end{proposition}
\pap{Fusionner Prop 2.4, 2.5,2.7 pour dire : existence primal, dualité (+existence dual), unicité au primal. Unicité au dual (avec stricte convexité de $F^*$).

Preuve de l'existence primale par méthode directe (superlinéarité de l'entropie pour coercivité).

Preuve de dualité par conditions d'optimalité

Preuve d'unicité (sous condition pour $F^*$)

}

\begin{proof}
Let \( t > 0 \). Since \( \F \) is convex, there exist \( a, b \in \R^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}} \) such that for any \( \xi \in \R^{\mathcal{X}} \oplus \R^{\mathcal{Y}} \), we have
\[
\F(\xi) \geq \langle a, \xi \rangle + b.
\]
This implies that
\[
\mathscr{C}_t(\gamma) = \langle c, \gamma \rangle + \F(A\gamma) + \frac{1}{t} \, \entropy(\gamma) \geq \langle c + A^*a, \gamma \rangle + \frac{1}{t} \, \entropy(\gamma) + b.
\]
Using the superlinearity of the entropy functional \( \entropy \), we deduce that \( \mathscr{C}_t \) is coercive. Therefore, the rescaled regularized unbalanced optimal transport problem \labelcref{UOT_t rescaled} admits at least one solution. Moreover, since \( \entropy \) is strictly convex, it follows that \( \mathscr{C}_t \) is strictly convex as well, implying that the solution is unique. 
Now, let \( \gamma_t \) be the unique minimizer of \labelcref{UOT_t rescaled}. Then, by first-order optimality conditions,
\[
0 \in \partial \mathscr{C}_t(\gamma_t).
\]
Define the functional \( G_t : \R^{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}} \to \R \) by
\[
G_t(\gamma) \defeq \langle c, \gamma \rangle + \frac{1}{t} \, \entropy(\gamma).
\]
Then the optimality condition becomes
\[
0 \in A^* \, \partial \F(A\gamma_t) + \partial G_t(\gamma_t),
\]
where we used assumption \textnormal{\cref{H2}} (see \cite{rockafellar1970convex}[Theorem 23.8]). Therefore, there exists \( u \in \partial \F(A\gamma_t) \) and \( v \in \partial G_t(\gamma_t) \) such that
\[
0 = A^* u + v.
\]
By the Fenchel–Young equality, we have
\[
\F(A\gamma_t) + \F^*(u) = \langle u, A\gamma_t \rangle,
\quad \text{and} \quad
G_t(\gamma_t) + G_t^*(v) = \langle \gamma_t, v \rangle = \langle \gamma_t, -A^*u \rangle = -\langle u, A\gamma_t \rangle.
\]
Summing the two equalities yields:
\[
\F(A\gamma_t) + G_t(\gamma_t) + \F^*(u) + G_t^*(-A^*u) = 0,
\]
from which we get 
\[
\F(A\gamma_t) + G_t(\gamma_t) = -\F^*(u) - G_t^*(-A^*u),
\]
and this concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Although the Fenchel–Rockafellar theorem could also be used to prove \cref{prop: existence for regularized problems}, our proof provides a more direct approach without requiring additional assumptions.
\end{remark}


\begin{remark}
Uniqueness of the solution for \labelcref{Dual UOT_t} holds modulo \(\ker A^*\), even in the absence of strict convexity of 
\(\F^*\).
\end{remark}

We denote by \(\mathscr{K}_t\) the functional of the dual problem, that is 
\[ \mathscr{K}_t(\xi) = \F^*\!\left(-\xi\right) + \!\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y}}\frac{1}{t}e^{t(\varphi_x +\psi_y-c(x,y))}.\]


