    To vertically align the sample, an SRS-$z$ is performed. If the sample is tipped to an angle such that no specular reflections will observably occur, as seen in \cref{fig:srs-z2}a, then the the spatial information simply shows the rising shadow from the substrate blocking the incident beam. In this case, the data can be reduced to the same $z$-scan discussed in \cref{sec:scan} by adding all the counts from each column (a sum over each of the entire images of the scan) (\cref{fig:srs-z2}b).

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    %\includegraphics{figures/srs-z-fits.pdf}
    \includegraphics{11-1.pdf}
    \includegraphics{11-2.pdf}
