%    The first part of the extra assumption, $s_i < 1$, is apparently natural since the case of a single producer covering the whole demand of the market is quite rare. And the second part, $\low{b}_i+1<\high{b}_i$, is also mild since $\low{b}_i+1\geq \high{b}_i$ only happens when agent $i$ can sell off its supply as a pivotal agent, which requires a strict equality between the total supply and the demand. And there is a more intuitive explanation why the upper bound is smaller than $\high{b}_i$: The unit price is $\high{b}_i$ if and only if every agent providing power to the market bids $\high{b}_i$ deterministically. And it cannot be a Nash equilibirum, since there must be an agent who only sells partially and can improve the utility by bidding $\high{b}_i-1$ to sell off its supply in this case due to $\low{b}_j<\low{b}_i<\low{b}_i+1<\high{b}_i$.
