\item \ul{There exists an infinite subsequence $\mathcal{S}' \subseteq 
\mathcal{S}$ such that for any $\sigma_k \in \mathcal{S}'$,  $\mathrm{support}(\sigma^k_A) \neq \{0\}$ and $\mathrm{support}(\sigma^k_B) \neq \{0\}$}.
\smallskip

Let $\sigma^\star = (\sigma^\star_A, \sigma^\star_B)$ be the limiting point of $\mathcal{S}'$. If there exist $i \geq 1$ and $j \geq 1$ such that $\sigma^\star_A(i) > 0$ and $\sigma^\star_B(j) > 0$ then the expected payment of $\sigma^\star$ is at least \[\sigma^\star_A(i) \cdot \sigma^\star_B(j) \cdot \left(x_A(i,j)\cdot p_A(i,j) + x_B(i,j)\cdot p_B(i,j)\right)  > 0\]
where the first inequality follows by individual rationality. Since $\lim_{k \rightarrow \infty} \epsilon_k = 0$ we know that $\sigma^\star$ is mixed NE for the original game (marginal costs $0$) and admits positive payment.
\smallskip
\smallskip


