%	\item Step 3- Dropout: a regularization technique to prevent overfitting. Randomly disables a percentage of neurons during training, forcing the network to learn more robust representations.
%	\item Step 4- Max-pooling (size: 2): reduces the dimensionality of the data, taking the maximum value in a 2x2 window. Reduces calculation complexity and increases the model's tolerance to small position variations.
%	\item Step 5- Flatten: transforms the data into a one-dimensional vector, necessary to connect it to the next fully connected layer.
%	\item Step 6- Dense (Units: 100, Activation: relu): a fully connected layer with 100 neurons. Every neuron in this layer is connected to every neuron in the previous layer. The ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) activation function introduces non-linearity, allowing the model to learn complex relationships.
%	\item Step 7- Dense (Units: 1, Activation: relu): another fully connected layer with a single neuron, probably used for regression, where the output is a continuous value.
%	\item Step 8- Class (Transition or Normal): output layer, providing the final prediction. In this case, it appears to classify the data into two categories:  ``Transitional" or ``Normal".
