The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec:model} introduces the finite-agent and MFG problems of the optimal tracking portfolio. Section \ref{sec:auxiliary} proposes an auxiliary state process with reflection and derive the associated HJB equation with Neumann boundary condition for the best response control problem, in which the best response portfolio process of the representative agent can be obtained in analytical form in terms of a dual reflected diffusion process. Section \ref{sec:MFG}  verifies the consistency condition and establishes the existence of the MFE with the aid of an auxiliary reflected dual process. Section \ref{sec:numerical} presents some quantitative properties and numerical examples of the MFE. The proofs of the main results in previous sections are collected in Section \ref{sec:proofs}.

\section{Finite-Agent and Mean Field Game Problems}\label{sec:model}

Let $(\Omega,\F,\Fx,\Px)$ be a filtered probability space with the filtration $\Fx=(\F_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ satisfying the usual conditions. We consider a financial market consisting of $n$ agents, in which the $i$-th agent invest her wealth in the $i$-th risky asset whose price dynamics is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:stock}
  \frac{d S_t^i}{S_{t}^i} = \mu dt +\sigma dW_t^i,\quad t\in [0,T],
\end{equation}
where the return rate $\mu>0$ and the volatility  $\sigma>0$. Here, $(W^1,\ldots,W^n)=(W_t^1,\ldots,W_t^n)^{\top}_{t\in[0,T]}$ is an $n$-dimensional Brownian motion, specified to each individual risky asset, standing for the idiosyncratic noise. It is assumed that the riskless interest rate $r=0$, which amounts to the change of num\'{e}raire. From this point onwards, all processes including the wealth process and the benchmark process are defined after the change of num\'{e}raire. At time $t\geq0$, let $\theta_t^i$ be the amount of wealth that the agent $i$ allocates in the stock $S^i$. The self-financing wealth process of agent $i$ satisfies the following controlled SDE:
 \begin{align}\label{eq:Vt}
dV_t^i=\theta^i_t \mu dt+\theta^i_t\sigma dW_t^i,\quad t\in [0,T].
\end{align}

We consider the relaxed benchmark tracking using the capital injection for each agent in the market.  At any time $t\geq0$, it is
 assumed that agent $i$ can strategically inject capital at such that the total wealth $V_t^i+C_t^i$
satisfies the relaxed constraint w.r.t. the linear combination of the average wealth process $\overline{V}_t^n=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n V^i_t$  and the market index process $Z_t^i$ that agent $i$ is concerned. The interaction of $n$ agents occurs through the average wealth process $\overline{V}^n_t$ in the relaxed constraint, which depicts the relative performance concern by the agent $i$ with respect to other peers. That is, after the injection of capital $C^i=(C_t^i)_{t\in[0,T]}$, each agent requires the total fund account $V_t^i+C_t^i$ to stay above the benchmark process $\lambda \overline{V}_t^n+(1-\lambda)Z_t^i$ for $\lambda \in(0,1)$ at all times $t\in[0,T]$. The market index process  $Z^i=(Z_t^i)_{t \in [0,T]}$, for $i=1,...,n$, is described as the following geometric Brownian motion (GBM) that
\begin{align}\label{eq:Zt-n}
d Z_t^i=\mu_Z Z_t^i d t+\sigma_Z Z_t^i d W_t^{i}, \quad Z_0^i=z\geq0,
\end{align}
where the return rate $\mu_Z>0$ and the volatility $\sigma_Z>0$ which satisfies $\frac{\mu_Z}{\sigma_Z}>\frac{\mu}{\sigma}$. Mathematically speaking, agent $i$ now aims to minimize the cumulative capital injected at the terminal time $T$, for $i=1,\ldots,n$, 
\begin{align}\label{eq:n-player}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle {\rm w}^i(\mathrm{v},z):=\text{$\inf_{(\theta, C)\in\mathbb{U}} \Ex\left[ C_0^i+\int_0^{T} e^{-\rho t}dC^i_t\Big|V^i_0=\mathrm{v},Z_0^i=z\right]$}\\[1em]
\displaystyle \text{subjecting to\ \  $\lambda \overline{V}_t^n+(1-\lambda)Z_t^i\le V^i_t+C^i_t$ for all $t\in [0,T]$,}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\lambda \in(0,1)$  represents the competition weight of agent $i$ towards her relative performance concern. The admissible control set $\mathbb{U}$ is defined as:
\begin{align*}
&\mathbb{U}:=\big\{\text{$(\theta,C)=(\theta_t,C_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$;~ $\theta$ is $\Fx$-adapted processes taking values on $\R$, $C$ is a non-negative,}\\
&\qquad\quad\text{ non-decreasing process with r.c.l.l. paths}\big\}.
\end{align*}
Here, for technical convenience, we assume that the initial wealth of each agent and the initial level of market index process are the same constants $(\mathrm{v},z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty)$. 

With continuum of agents, we can consider the mean field game (MFG) problem as $n\rightarrow\infty$. Let us denote $\overline{V}=(\overline{V}_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ by the average wealth of the mean field population. When $n\rightarrow\infty$, we expect that $[0,T]\ni t\mapsto\overline{V}_t$ is a deterministic differentiable function with the form $d\overline{V}_t=f(t)dt$ for some positive $f\in\C([0,T])$ and $\overline{V}_0=\mathrm{v}>0$. Here, $\C([0,T])$ denotes the set of real-valued continuous functions on $[0,T]$. Then, our MFG problem is to find $(\theta^*,C^*)=(\theta_t^*,C_t^*)_{t\in[0,T]}$ such that the best response satisfies the so-called consistency condition. 


For a given function $t\mapsto f(t)$ (or $t\mapsto\overline{V}_t$), we first consider the stochastic control problem for a representative agent that  
\begin{align}\label{eq:mfg}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle {\rm w}(\mathrm{v},z):=\text{$\inf_{(\theta,C)\in \mathbb{U}} \Ex\left[ C_0+ \int_0^{T} e^{-\rho t}dC_t\Big|V_0^{\theta}=\mathrm{v},Z_0=z \right]$}\\[1em]
\displaystyle \text{subjecting to\ \  $\lambda \overline{V}_t +(1-\lambda )Z_t\le V_t^{\theta}+C_t$ for all $t\in [0,T]$,}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where the wealth process $V$ of the representative agent satisfies that
\begin{align}\label{eq:Vt-MFG}
dV_t^{\theta}=\theta_t \mu dt+\theta_t\sigma dW_t,\quad t\in (0,T],\quad V_0^{\theta}=\mathrm{v}>0,
\end{align}
and the market index process $Z=(Z_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ of the representative agent is given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:Zt-MFG}
d Z_t=\mu_Z Z_t d t+\sigma_Z Z_t d W_t,\quad Z_0=z\geq0.
\end{align}

We next give the definition of the mean field equilibrium for our MFG problem:
\begin{definition}[Mean Field Equilibrium]\label{def:MFG}
For a given function $f\in\C([0,T])$, let $(\theta^{*,f},C^{*,f})=(\theta_t^{*,f},C_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}\in \mathbb{U}$ be the best response solution to the stochastic control problem \eqref{eq:mfg} for the representative agent. The strategy $(\theta^{*,f},C^{*,f})$ is called a mean field equilibrium (MFE) if it is the best response to itself such that $\Ex[V_t^{*,f}]=\mathrm{v}_0+\int_0^t f(s)ds$ for all $t\in[0,T]$, where $V^{*,f}=(V_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$ is the wealth process \eqref{eq:Vt-MFG} under the portfolio strategy $\theta^{*,f}$. 
\end{definition}
Based on the above definition, finding an MFE is to solve the following two-step problem:
\begin{itemize} 
\item {\bf Step 1.}~ For a given deterministic positive function $f\in\C([0,T])$, we solve the stochastic control problem \eqref{eq:mfg} for a representative agent against the fixed environment.
The best response strategy for the representative agent is denoted by $(\theta^{*,f},C^{*,f})\in \mathbb{U}$ and $V^{*,f}=(V_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$ stands for the wealth process in \eqref{eq:Vt-MFG} under the strategy $\theta^{*,f}=(\theta_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$.
\item{\bf Step 2.}~ We derive an MFE by using the consistency condition, which is equivalent to finding a fixed point $f^*\in\C([0,T])$ taking values on $\R_+$ to the fixed point problem $\Ex[V_t^{*,f}]=\mathrm{v}_0+\int_0^t f(s)ds$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Then, the expected MFE is given by $(\theta^{*,f^*},C^{*,f^*})=(\theta_t^{*,f^*},C_t^{*,f^*})_{t\in[0,T]}$.
\end{itemize}



\section{Equivalent Problem Formulation for Best Response Control}\label{sec:auxiliary}

To tackle the best response control of the representative agent in problem \eqref{eq:mfg} with the floor constraint, we first reformulate the problem based on the observation that, for a fixed control $\theta$, the optimal $C$ is always the smallest adapted right-continuous and non-decreasing process that dominates $\lambda \overline{V}_t +(1-\lambda )Z_t-V_t^{\theta}$. It follows from Lemma 2.4 in \cite{BoLiaoYu21} that,  for a fixed regular control $\theta=(\theta_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$, the optimal singular control satisfies $C^{\theta,*}_t =0\vee \sup_{s\leq t}\left(\lambda \overline{V}_s +(1-\lambda )Z_s-V_{s}^{\theta}\right)$, $\forall t\in [0,T]$. Thus, the control problem \eqref{eq:mfg} admits the equivalent formulation as an unconstrained control problem with a running maximum cost that
\begin{align}\label{eq_orig_pb-mfg}
\hat{{\rm w}}(\mathrm{v},z)&=(1-\lambda)(z-\mathrm{v})^+\nonumber\\
&\quad+\inf_{\theta\in\mathbb{U}^{\rm r}}\ \Ex\left[ \int_0^{T} e^{-\rho t} d\left(0\vee \sup_{s\leq t}\left(\lambda \overline{V}_s +(1-\lambda )Z_s-V_{s}^{\theta}\right)\right)\right],
\end{align}
where $\mathbb{U}^{\rm r}$ denotes the admissible control set of the regular strategies $\theta=(\theta_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ that will be specified later.

To formulate the auxiliary stochastic control problem, we will introduce a new controlled state
process to replace the process. Let $K_t:=\lambda \bar{V}_t+(1-\lambda )Z_t-V_t$ and define $L_t=0\vee \sup_{s\leq t}K_s$ for $t\in[0,T]$. Then, we define a new state process with reflection that $X_t:=L_t-K_t$ and 
\begin{align}\label{state-X}
dX_t=(\theta_t \mu -\lambda f(t)-(1-\lambda)\mu_Z Z_t)dt+(\theta_t\sigma-(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z Z_t )dW_t+dL_t,\quad t\in (0,T]
\end{align}
with $X_0=x=((1-\lambda)(\mathrm{v}-z))_+\in\R_+$. In particular, the process $L=(L_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ which is referred to as the local time of $X$, it increases at time $t$ if and only if $X_t=0$, i.e., $L_t=D_t$. We will change the notation from $L_t$ to $L_t^X$ from this point on wards to emphasize its dependence on the new state process $X$ given in \eqref{state-X}.

We then need to solve an equivalent stochastic control problem, for $(t,x,z)\in[0,T]\times\R_+^2$, 
\begin{align}\label{eq_mfg-2}
&u(t,x,z):=\text{$\sup_{\theta\in \mathbb{U}_t^r} \Ex\left[  -\int_t^{T} e^{-\rho (s-t)}dL_s^X\Big|X_t=x,Z_t=z \right]$},
\end{align}
where the admissible control set $\mathbb{U}_t^{\rm r}$ is specified as the set of $\Fx$-adapted control processes $\theta=(\theta_s)_{s\in[t,T]}$ such that the reflected SDE \eqref{state-X} has a unique strong solution. The admissible control set $\mathbb{U}^{\rm r}$ given in \eqref{eq_orig_pb-mfg} satisfies $\mathbb{U}^{\rm r}=\mathbb{U}^{\rm r}_0$. 
It is straightforward to derive the next result:
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:propoertyu}
Let $(t,z)\in[0,T]\times \R_+$ be fixed. Then, the value function $x\mapsto u(t,x,z)$ given by \eqref{eq_mfg-2} is non-decreasing. Moreover, for all $(t,x_1,x_2,z)\in[0,T]\times \R_+^3$, we have
\begin{align}\label{eq:lipvx}
\left|u(t,x_1,z)-u(t,x_2,z)\right|\leq  |x_1-x_2|.
\end{align}
\end{lemma}


Denote by $\mathcal{D}_T:=[0,T)\times \R_+^2$ and $\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T:=[0,T]\times \R_+^2$. In what follows, let us first assume that the value function $u$ satisfies ${\C}^{1,2}(\mathcal{D}_T)\cap {\C}\left(\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T\right)$ and $u_{xx} < 0 $ on $\mathcal{D}_T$, which will be discussed and verified later. By heuristic dynamic programming argument, the HJB equation can be derived by
\begin{align}\label{HJB}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle u_t+\sup_{\theta\in\R}\left[u_x\mu\theta+\frac{1}{2}u_{xx}(\sigma\theta-(1-\lambda)\sigma_Zz)^2+u_{xz}(\sigma\theta-(1-\lambda)\sigma_Zz)\sigma_Z z \right] \\[0.6em]
\displaystyle \qquad\qquad-((1-\lambda)\mu_Zz+\lambda f(t)) u_x+\mu_Z zu_z+\frac{1}{2}\sigma_Z^2z^2u_{zz}=\rho u,\quad \forall(t,x,z)\in\mathcal{D}_T,\\[0.6em]
\displaystyle u_x(t,0,z)=1,\quad \forall(t,z)\in[0,T)\times \R_+,\\[0.6em]
\displaystyle u(T,x,z)=0,\quad \forall(x,z)\in\R_+^2.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
The Neumann boundary condition $u_x(t,0,z)=1$ stems from the fact that $L_t^X$ increases if and only if $X_t=0$. 
 By taking the first order condition over the control,  we get
\begin{align}\label{HJB-nonlinear}
 &u_t-\frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}\frac{u_x^2}{u_{xx}}-\frac{\mu\sigma_Z z}{\sigma}\frac{u_xu_{xz}}{u_{xx}}+\frac{1}{2}\sigma_Z^2z^2\left(u_{zz}-\frac{u_{xz}^2}{u_{xx}}\right)+\mu_Z zu_z\\
 &\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad-\left(\frac{(1-\lambda)(\mu_Z\sigma-\mu\sigma_Z)}{\sigma}z+\lambda f(t)\right)u_x =\rho u,\quad \forall(t,x,z)\in\mathcal{D}_T.\nonumber
\end{align}
Note that PDE \eqref{HJB-nonlinear} is fully nonlinear.  We first apply the dual transform to linearize the original HJB equation \eqref{HJB-nonlinear}. As a direct result of Lemma~\ref{lem:propoertyu},  $|u_x(t,x,z)|= u_x(t,x,z)\leq 1$ for all $(t,x,z)\in\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$. Then, we may apply Legendre-Fenchel transform of the solution $u$ only w.r.t. $x$ that, for all $(t,y,z)\in[0,T]\times (0,1]\times\R_+$,
\begin{align}\label{eq:LFT-u}
\hat{u}(t,y,z):=\sup_{x>0}\{u(t,x,z)-xy\}. 
\end{align}
Consequently $u(t,x,z)=\inf_{y\in(0,1]}\{\hat{u}(t,y,z)+xy\}$ for $(t,x,z)\in\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$. Define that $x^*(t,y,z)=u_x(t,\cdot,z)^{-1}(y)$ with $y\mapsto u_x(t,\cdot,z)^{-1}(y)$ being the inverse function of $x\mapsto u_x(t,x,z)$. Thus, $x^*=x^*(t,y,z)$ satisfies the equation $u_x(t,x^*,z)=y$ for all $(t,z)\in[0,T]\times \R_+$. 
%Using the relationship between $y$ and $u_x(t,x^*,y)$, we have
%\begin{align*}
%&\hat{u}(t,y,z)=u(t,x^*,z)-x^*y,\quad \hat{u}_y(t,y,z)=-x^*,\quad\hat{u}_{t}(t,y,z)=u_t(t,x^*,z),\\
%&\hat{u}_z(t,y,z)=u_z(t,x^*,z),\quad\hat{u}_{yy}(t,y,z)=-\frac{1}{u_{xx}(t,x^*,z)},
%\quad \hat{u}_{yz}(t,y,z)=\frac{u_{xz}(t,x^*,z)}{u_{xx}(t,x^*,z)},\nonumber\\
%&\hat{u}_{zz}(t,y,z)=u_{zz}(t,x^*,z)-\frac{u_{xz}(t,x^*,z)^2}{u_{xx}(t,x^*,z)}.
%\end{align*}
In view of the dual relation, we get the following dual PDE of Eq.~\eqref{HJB-nonlinear} that
\begin{align}\label{eq:dual-u}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \hat{u}_t+\frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}y^2\hat{u}_{yy}-\frac{\mu\sigma_Z }{\sigma}zy\hat{u}_{yz}+\frac{1}{2}\sigma_Z^2z^2\hat{u}_{zz}+\rho y\hat{u}_y+\mu_Zz\hat{u}_z\\[0.9em]
\displaystyle \quad-\left(\frac{(1-\lambda)(\mu_Z\sigma-\mu\sigma_Z)}{\sigma}z+\lambda f(t)\right)y=\rho \hat{u},\quad \forall(t,y,z)\in[0,T)\times (0,1)\times\R_+,\\[0.8em]
\displaystyle \hat{u}_y(t,1,z)=0,\quad \forall(t,z)\in[0,T)\times \R_+,\\[0.6em]
\displaystyle \hat{u}(T,y,z)=0,\quad \forall(y,z)\in(0,1]\times\R_+.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
For convenience, we further consider the transform  
 $v(t,r,z)=e^{-\rho t}\hat{u}\left(t,e^{-r},z\right)$,  $\forall (t,r,z)\in\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, and we arrive at the equation of  $v(t,r,z)$ give by
\begin{align}\label{eq:HJB-v}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle v_t+\frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}v_{rr}+\frac{\mu\sigma_Z }{\sigma}zv_{rz}+\frac{1}{2}\sigma_Z^2z^2v_{zz}+\left(\frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}-\rho\right) v_r+\mu_Z zu_z\\[0.8em]
\displaystyle\qquad\qquad-\left((1-\lambda)\eta z+\lambda f(t)\right)e^{-\rho t-r}=0,\quad \forall(t,r,z)\in[0,T)\times (0,+\infty)\times\R_+,\\[0.8em]
\displaystyle v_r(t,0,z)=0,\quad \forall(t,z)\in[0,T)\times\R_+,\\[0.6em]
\displaystyle v(T,r,z)=0,\quad \forall(r,z)\in\R_+^2,
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\eta:=\frac{\mu_Z\sigma-\mu\sigma_Z}{\sigma}>0$.

We study the existence and uniqueness of the classical solution to the problem \eqref{eq:HJB-v} using the probabilistic representation. To this end, for $(t, r,z) \in \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, let us define the function by
\begin{align}\label{eq:v}
v(t,r,z)&:=-\lambda\Ex\left[ \int_t^Te^{-\rho s-R^{t,r}_s}f(s) ds\right]-(1-\lambda)\eta\Ex\left[ \int_t^Te^{-\rho s-R^{t,r}_s}Z_s^{t,z} ds\right],
\end{align}
where, for $(t,r,z)\in  [0,T]\times\R_+^2$, the process $(Z_s^{t,z})_{s\in [t,T]}$ is given by \eqref{eq:Zt-MFG} with $Z_t^{t,z}=z$, and the process $(R_s^{t,r})_{s\in [t,T]}$ is a reflected Brownian motion defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:R}
&R_s^{t,r}:=r+\int_t^s \left(\frac{\mu^2}{2\sigma^2}-\rho\right)du+\frac{\mu}{\sigma}\int_t^s dW_u+\int_t^s dL_u^{t,r}\geq 0,\quad \forall s\in[t,T].
\end{align}
Here, $(L_s^{t, r})_{s\in [t,T]}$ is a continuous and non-decreasing process that increases only on $\{s \in[t, T];~R_s^{t, r}=0\}$ with $L_t^{t, r}=0$. 
%Thus, we can rewrite the function $v$ in \eqref{eq:v} by
%\begin{align}\label{eq:v2}
%v(t,r,z)&=-(1-\lambda)\eta z\Ex\left[ \int_t^T\exp\left(-\rho s-R^{t,r}_s+\left(\mu_Z-\frac{\sigma_Z^2}{2}\right)(s-t)+\sigma_Z(W_s-W_t)\right) ds\right]\nonumber\\
%&\qquad-\lambda\Ex\left[ \int_t^Te^{-\rho s-R^{t,r}_s}f(s) ds\right].
%\end{align}







The well-posedness of the problem \eqref{eq:dual-u} is then provided in the next result.
\begin{proposition}\label{thm:dual-u}
Let $f\in {\C}([0,T])$ satisfy $f(t)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Then, the function  $\hat{u}(t,y,z)$ defined  by $ \hat{u}\left(t,y,z\right)=e^{\rho t}v(t,-\ln y,z)$, for $(t,y,z)\in[0,T]\times (0,1]\times \R_+$,
is the unique classical solution of the dual equation \eqref{eq:dual-u} satisfying $|\hat{u}(t,y,z)|\leq C(1+z)$ for some positive constant $C$. Moreover, $\hat{u}_y \in\C^{1,2,2}([0,T]\times (0,1]\times\R_+)$, and  for each $(t,z) \in[0, T)\times\R_+ $, the solution $(0,1] \ni y \mapsto \hat{u}(t,y,z)$ is strictly convex.
\end{proposition}



We next present the verification theorem on the best response control of the representative agent. 
\begin{theorem}[Verification Theorem]\label{thm:verification}
Let $f\in {\C}([0,T])$ satisfy $f(t)> 0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Let us introduce the set ${\cal O}_T$ defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:O}
{\cal O}_T:=\left\{(t,x,z)\in\overline{{\cal D}}_T;~x<x_0(t,z)\right\},
\end{align}
where the function $x_0(t,z)$ for $(t,z)\in[0,T]\times \R_+$ is defined by
 \begin{align}\label{eq:x0}
 x_0(t,z)&:=\lambda \int_t^{T} f(s)ds+(1-\lambda)\left(e^{\eta (T-t)}-1\right)z.
 \end{align}
 Then, we have 
\begin{itemize}
\item[{\rm(i)}] Consider the function $u(t,x,z)$ given  by
\begin{align}\label{eq:sol-u}
u(t,x,z):=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \inf_{y\in(0,1]}\{\hat{u}(t,y,z)+yx\},&\text{if}~ (t,x,z)\in\mathcal{O}_T,\\[0.8em]
\displaystyle \qquad\qquad0,&\text{if}~ (t,x,z)\in \mathcal{O}_T \cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
Consequently, the function $u\in \C^{1,2,2}(\mathcal{D}_T)\cap \C(\overline{\mathcal{D}}_T)$ is well-defined and is a classical solution of the primal HJB equation \eqref{HJB}.

\item[{\rm(ii)}] Introduce the feedback control function as follows:
\begin{align}\label{eq:optimal-theta}
&\theta^*(t,x,z)\nonumber\\
&:=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle -\frac{\mu}{\sigma^2}\frac{u_x(t,x,z)}{u_{xx}(t,x,z)}-\frac{\sigma_Z}{\sigma}\frac{zu_{xz}(t,x,z)}{u_{xx}(t,x,z)}+\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}z,\quad\text{if}~ (t,x,z)\in\mathcal{O}_T,\\[1.5em]
\displaystyle \frac{\mu}{\sigma^2}\lim_{y\downarrow 0} y\hat{u}_{yy}(t,y,z)-\frac{\sigma_Z}{\sigma} \lim_{y\downarrow 0}z \hat{u}_{yz}(t,y,z)+\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}z,\quad \text{if}~ (t,x,z)\in \mathcal{O}_T^c \cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
For $(t,x)\in[0,T]\times \R_+$, consider the controlled state process $X^*=(X^*_s)_{s\in[t,T]}$ that obeys the following reflected SDE:
\begin{align}\label{eq:optimal-SDE}
 X^*_s=x+\int_t^s\mu \theta^*(\ell,X_{\ell}^*,Z_{\ell}) d\ell+\int_t^s \sigma \theta^*(\ell,X_{\ell}^*,Z_{\ell}) dW_\ell -\int_t^s \lambda f(\ell)d\ell+L_s^{X^*}.
\end{align}
 Define  $\theta^*_s=\theta^*(s,X^*_s,Z_s)$ for all $s\in[t,T]$. Then, the strategy  $\theta^*=(\theta^*_s)_{s\in [t,T]}\in\mathbb{U}^r_t$ is a best response control of the representatitve agent. 
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

Moreover, we have from Theorem~\ref{thm:verification} the following corollary on the semi-explicit form of the optimal portfolio (feedback) function given by
\begin{corollary}\label{coro:optimal-portfolio}
The optimal feedback function $\theta^*(t,x,z)$ given by \eqref{eq:optimal-theta} is always non-negative and  admits the following semi-explicit expression: on $\mathcal{O}_T$,
\begin{align}\label{optimal-portfolio-O}
\theta^*(t,x,z)&=\frac{\lambda \mu}{\sigma^2} \int_{t}^{T}\int_{-\infty}^{r} e^{-\rho (s-t)+\ell}\phi(s-t,\ell,r)f(s)d\ell ds\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta \mu}{\sigma^2} z\int_{t}^{T}\int_{-\infty}^{r} \exp\left(-(\rho-\kappa)(s-t)+\left(1-\frac{\sigma\sigma_Z}{\mu}\right)\ell\right) \phi(s-t,\ell,r)d\ell ds\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta\sigma_Z}{\sigma}  \Ex\left[ \int_t^{\tau_{r}^t \wedge T}e^{-\rho (s-t)-R^{t,r}_s+r}Z_s^{t,z} ds\right]+\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}z,
\end{align} 
while on $\mathcal{O}_T^c \cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$,
\begin{align}\label{optimal-portfolio-Oc}
\theta^*(t,x,z)&=\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)}z,
\end{align}
where $r=r(t,x,z):=-\ln y(t,x,z)$ and $x\to y(t,x,z)$ is the inverse function of $y\to-\hat{u}_y(t,y,z)$ for all $(t,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T$. The function $\phi(s,x,y)$ is defined by 
\begin{align}\label{eq:phi}
\phi(s,x,y)&:= \frac{2(2 y-x)}{\tilde{\sigma}^2\sqrt{2\tilde{\sigma}^2 \pi s^3}}\exp \left(\frac{\tilde{\mu}}{\tilde{\sigma}} x-\frac{1}{2} \tilde{\mu}^2 s-\frac{(2 y-x)^2}{2\tilde{\sigma}^2 s}\right)
\end{align}
with parameters $\tilde{\mu}:=\frac{\mu}{2\sigma}-\frac{\sigma}{\mu}\rho$ and $\tilde{\sigma}:=-\frac{\mu}{\sigma}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Mean Field Equilibrium}\label{sec:MFG}

In this section,  we verify the consistency condition provided in Definition \ref{def:MFG} for the deterministic function $t\mapsto f(t)$ that
\begin{align}\label{eq:consistency1}
\mathrm{v}+\int_0^t f(s)ds=\mathbb{E}\left[V_t^{\theta^{*,f}}\right], \quad \forall t\in [0,T],
\end{align}
and establish the existence of the positive mapping $f^*\in\C([0,T])$ determined by \eqref{eq:consistency1}. As a result, the mean field equilibrium is given by $(\theta^{*,f^*},C^{*,f^*})=(\theta_t^{*,f^*},C_t^{*,f^*})_{t\in[0,T]}$ characterized by Definition~\ref{def:MFG}. On the other hand, it follows from \eqref{eq:Vt-MFG} that $V_t^{\theta^{*,f}}=\mathrm{v}+\int_0^t \theta_s^{*,f} \mu ds+\int_0^t \theta_s^{*,f}\sigma dW_s$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Taking the expectation on both sides of the equation, we have
\begin{align}\label{eq:consistency2}
\Ex\left[V_t^{\theta^{*,f}}\right]=\mathrm{v}+\int_0^t \Ex[\mu\theta_s^{*,f}]  ds.
\end{align}
Thus, in view of \eqref{eq:consistency1} and \eqref{eq:consistency2}, to verify the consistency condition, it suffices to find a deterministic positive mapping $f\in\C([0,T])$ such that
\begin{align}\label{eq:consistency-f}
f(t)= \mu\Ex\left[\theta_t^{*,f}\right],\quad\forall t\in[0,T],
\end{align}
where $\theta^{*,f}=(\theta_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$ is the optimal feedback strategy provided in Theorem \ref{thm:verification} under the mapping $f\in\C([0,T])$. Toward this end, we proceed with the following three steps.
\begin{itemize} 
\item[]{\bf Step 1.}\quad Given a positive function $f\in\C([0,T])$, we introduce the reflected dual process and represent optimal feedback strategy $\theta^{*,f}$ in view of the reflected dual process.
\item[]{\bf Step 2.}\quad We prove that, start from the dual variable, there exists some positive function $f\in\C([0,T])$ satisfying the consistency condition \eqref{eq:consistency-f}.
\item[]{\bf Step 3.}\quad We next show the continuous map from the dual variable to the primal state variable, which help us find the (positive) fixed point $f^*\in\C([0,T])$ satisfying the consistency condition \eqref{eq:consistency-f} for primal state variables $(x,z)\in\R_+\times (0,\infty)$. 
\end{itemize}


Given a positive function $f\in\C([0,T])$, we will show that the partial derivative $u_x\in \C^{1,2,2}({\cal O}_T)$ and introduce the dual reflected diffusion process. We first have
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:u_x}
The partial derivative of value function $u$ w.r.t. $x$ given by \eqref{eq:sol-u} satisfies that $u_x\in \C^{1,2,2}({\cal O}_T)$.
\end{lemma}
Consequently, it holds that
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dual-process}
Introduce the process $Y=(Y_t)_{t\in [0,T]}$ by $Y_t=u_x(t,X_t^*,Z_t)$ for $t\in[0,T)$ and $Y_T:=\lim_{t\uparrow T}Y_t$ with $(X_0^*,Z_0)=(x,z)\in\R_+^2$. Define the stopping time $\tau_0:=\inf\{t\in[0,T];~X_t^*\geq x_0(t,Z_t)\}\wedge T$ ($\inf \emptyset=+\infty$). Here, the process $X^*=(X_t^*)_{t\in[0,T]}$ is given by \eqref{eq:optimal-SDE}. Then 
\begin{itemize}
\item[{\rm(i)}] if $(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T$, then $\tau_0=T$, $\Px$-a.s.. If $(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T^c\cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, then $\tau_0=0$, $\Px$-a.s. and for any $t\in[0,T]$, $(t,X_t^*,Z_t)\in  {\cal O}_T^c\cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, $\Px$-a.s.;
\item[{\rm(ii)}] for $(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T$, the process $Y$ taking values on $(0,1]$ is a reflected process that satisfies the SDE:
\begin{align}\label{eq:Yt}
dY_t= \rho Y_tdt-\frac{\mu}{\sigma}Y_tdW_t-dL_t^Y,\quad t\in(0,T],
\end{align}
where the process $L=(L_t^Y)_{t\in[0,T]}$ is a continuous and non-decreasing process (with $L_t^{Y}=0$) that increases on the set $\{t\geq 0;~Y_t=1\}$ only.
\end{itemize}
\end{lemma}


\begin{remark}\label{rem:injection}
Lemma \ref{lem:dual-process} shows that the capital injection region ${\cal O}$ and non capital injection region ${\cal O}_T^c\cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$ are not interconnected. In other words, if the initial state $(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T$, $(t,X_t^*,Z_t)\in{\cal O}_T$, $\Px$-a.s., for any $t\in[0,T]$; if $(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T^c\cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, then for any $t\in[0,T]$, $(t,X_t^*,Z_t)\in  {\cal O}_T^c\cap \overline{\mathcal{D}}_T$, $\Px$-a.s.. Whether the capital injection is needed completely depends on the initial wealth level of the fund manager and  initial level of the benchmark process.
\end{remark}

In view of Theorem \ref{thm:verification}, Corollary \ref{coro:optimal-portfolio} and the reflected dual process, the optimal portfolio strategy $\theta^{*,f}=(\theta_t^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$  admits the following expression.
\begin{corollary}\label{coro:optimal-portfolio-dual}
The optimal portfolio strategy $\theta_t^{*,f}=(\theta^{*,f})_{t\in[0,T]}$ defined in Theorem \ref{thm:verification} admits the following expression,  on $\mathcal{O}_T$,
\begin{align}\label{optimal-portfolio-daul-O}
\theta^{*,f}_t&=\frac{\lambda \mu}{\sigma^2} \int_{t}^{T}\int_{-\infty}^{R_t^{0,r}} e^{-\rho (s-t)+x}\phi(s-t,x,R_t^{0,r})f(s) ds\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta \mu}{\sigma^2} Z_t^{0,z}\int_{t}^{T}\int_{-\infty}^{R_t^{0,r}} \exp\left(-(\rho-\kappa)(s-t)+\left(1-\frac{\sigma\sigma_Z}{\mu}\right)x\right) \phi(s-t,x,R_t^{0,r})dx ds\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta\sigma_Z}{\sigma}  \varphi(t,R_t^{0,r},Z_t^{0,z})+\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}Z_t^{0,z},
\end{align} 
on $\mathcal{O}_T^c \cap \overline{\mathcal{D}_T}$,
\begin{align}\label{optimal-portfolio-dual-Oc}
\theta^{*,f}_t&=\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)}Z_t^{0,z},
\end{align}
where the functon $\varphi:[0,T]\times \R_+^2\to \R$ is defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:varphi}
\varphi(t,r,z):=\Ex\left[ \int_t^{\tau_{\hat{r}}^t \wedge T}e^{-\rho (s-t)-R^{t,r}_s+r}Z_s^{t,z} ds\right].
\end{align}
Recall that the reflected process $(R_t^{0,r})_{t\in [0,T]}$ is given by \eqref{eq:R} with $r=-\ln(u_x(0,x,z))$ and the function $\phi$ is given by \eqref{eq:phi}.
\end{corollary}

Based on Corollary \ref{coro:optimal-portfolio-dual}, for any $(x,z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty)$, let us consider the mapping ${\cal I}:\C([0,T])\to \C([0,T])$ given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:Phi}
{\cal I} f(t):=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \int_t^T G(r,s,t)f(s)ds+H(r,z,t), & \text{if}~(0,x,z)\in {\cal O}_T^f,\\[1.2em]
\displaystyle \frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)+\mu_Z t}z, &\text{if}~(0,x,z)\in (\mathcal{O}_T^f)^c \cap \overline{\mathcal{D}_T},
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $r=r^f(0,x,z):=-\ln(u_x^f(0,x,z))$, the function $G(r,s,t)$ and $H(r,z,t)$ are given by, for $(r,z)\in\R_+^2$ and $0\leq t\leq s\leq T$,
\begin{align}
&G(r,s,t):=\frac{\lambda \mu^2}{\sigma^2} \Ex\left[\int_{-\infty}^{R_t^{0,r}} e^{-\rho (s-t)+x}\phi(s-t,x,R_t^{0,r})dx \right],\label{eq:G}\\
&H(r,z,t)\label{eq:H}\nonumber\\
&:=\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta \mu^2}{\sigma^2} \Ex\left[Z_t\int_{t}^{T}\int_{-\infty}^{R_t^{0,r}} \exp\left(-(\rho-\kappa)(s-t)+\left(1-\frac{\sigma\sigma_Z}{\mu}\right)x\right) \phi(s-t,x,R_t^{0,r})dx ds\right]\nonumber\\
&\quad+\frac{(1-\lambda)\eta\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}  \Ex\left[\varphi(t,R_t^{0,r},Z_t^{0,z})\right]+\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\mu_Z t}z.
\end{align}
As a result, to verify the consistency condition \eqref{eq:consistency-f}, it is sufficient to show that the  mapping ${\cal I}$ has a fixed point $f^*$ satisfying $f(t)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. To this end, we need the next two results.


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fixpoint-r}
Let $(r,z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty)$. The mapping ${\cal J}:\C([0,T])\to \C([0,T])$ defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:map-J}
{\cal J}f(t):=\int_t^T G(r,s,t) f(s) ds+H(r,z,t),\quad \text{for}~t\in[0,T]
\end{align}
has a unique deterministic fixed point $f\in \C([0,T])$ satisfying $f(t)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$.
\end{lemma}


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:continuity-f}
Let $z>0$ be fixed. For $(r,t)\in\R_+\times[0,T]$, denote by $f(t;r)$ the unique deterministic fixed point given by Lemma \ref{lem:fixpoint-r}. Then, the function $(t,r)\mapsto f(t;r)$ is continuous.
\end{lemma}



Lemma \ref{lem:fixpoint-r} establishes the existence and uniqueness of the fixed point to the mapping \eqref{eq:map-J} while Lemma \ref{lem:continuity-f} shows the continuity of the function $f$ given by Lemma \ref{lem:fixpoint-r} with respect to $(t,r)$. Using these auxiliary results, we have the existence of the mean field equilibrium in the next main result of this paper.
\begin{theorem}[Mean Field Equilibrium]\label{thm:MF-equilibrium}
Let $(x,z)\in\R_+\times (0,\infty)$. There exists a  deterministic fixed point $f^*\in\C([0,T])$ to Eq. \eqref{eq:Phi} satisfying $f^*(t)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Then, $(\theta^{*,f^*},C^{*,f^*})=(\theta_t^{*,f^*},C_t^{*,f^*})_{t\in[0,T]}$ is a mean field equilibrium, where $\theta^{*,f^*}$ is defined in  Theorem \ref{thm:verification} for the given function $f^*$ and
\begin{align*}
C_t^{*,f^*}=0\vee \sup_{s\leq t}\left(\lambda \int_0^s f^*(u)du +(1-\lambda )Z_s-V_{s}^{\theta^{*,f^*}}\right),\quad\forall t\in[0,T].    
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
 For $z>0$, let us consider $\hat{x}_0(z)$ defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:hat-x}
\hat{x}_0(z):=(1-\lambda)\left(\lambda\left(e^{\mu_Z T}-e^{\eta T}\right)+e^{\eta T}-1\right)z>0.
\end{align} 
For $(x,z)\in\{(x,z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty):~x\geq \hat{x}_0(z)\}$, it is not hard to check that
\begin{align}
f(t)=\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)}\Ex\left[Z_t^{0,z}\right]=\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)+\mu_Z t}z
\end{align}
satisfies the consistency condition \eqref{eq:Phi}. 

Next, we consider the case with $(x,z)\in\{(x,z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty);~x< \hat{x}_0(z)\}$. It follows from Lemma \ref{lem:fixpoint-r} that for $(r,z)\in \R_+\times (0,\infty)$, the mapping \eqref{eq:map-J} has a unique fixed point $f(\cdot;r)\in C([0,T])$ satisfying $f(t;r)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. In view of the dual relationship, we have 
\begin{align}\label{eq:x-r}
x(r)&=-\hat{u}_y(0,y,z)=e^{r}v_r(0,r,z)\\
&=\lambda e^{r}\mathbb{E}\left[\int_0^{\tau_r^0 \wedge T} e^{-\rho s-R_s^{0, r}} f(s;r)ds\right]+(1-\lambda)\eta  e^{r}\Ex\left[ \int_0^{\tau_r^0 \wedge T}e^{-\rho s-R^{0,r}_s}Z_s^{0,z} ds\right].
\end{align}
 This, together with Lemma \ref{lem:continuity-f}, yields that $r\to x(r)$ is continuous and 
\begin{align}\label{eq:lim-xr}
\lim_{r\downarrow 0}x(r)=0,\quad \lim_{r\uparrow +\infty}x(r)=\hat{x}_0(z).
\end{align}
Consequently, it follows from the continuity of $r\to x(r)$ and \eqref{eq:lim-xr} that for any $\tilde{x}\in [0,\hat{x}_0(z))$, there exists some $r_{\tilde{x}}\in \R_+$ such that $x(r_{\tilde{x}})=\tilde{x}$ and also a unique fixed point $f(\cdot;r_{\tilde{x}})\in C([0,T])$ of the mapping \eqref{eq:map-J} satisfying $f(t)>0$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. For $(x,z)\in\R_+\times (0,\infty)$, let us define
\begin{align}\label{eq:equilibrium-f}
f^*(t)=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle f(t;r_x),&\text{if}~x\in[0,\hat{x}_0(z)),\\[1em]
\displaystyle \frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)+\mu_Z t}z,&\text{if}~x \in[\hat{x}_0(z),+\infty).
\end{cases}
\end{align}
Then, we can easily verify that the function $t\mapsto f^*(t)$ given by \eqref{eq:equilibrium-f} is a fixed point to the mapping ${\cal I}$ given by \eqref{eq:Phi}. Thus,  the function $t\to f^*(t)$ satisfies the consistency condition \eqref{eq:consistency1}, and hence $(\theta^{*,f^*},C^{*,f^*})$ is a mean field equilibrium.
\end{proof}



\section{Discussions on the Mean Field Equilibrium}\label{sec:numerical}
We present in this section some quantitative properties and numerical results of the MFE obtained in Theorem \ref{thm:MF-equilibrium}. We recall that, for $(z,T)\in(0,+\infty)^2$, the capital injection threshold of initial wealth is given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:threshold}
\hat{x}_0(z)=(1-\lambda)\left\{\lambda\left(e^{\mu_Z T}-e^{\eta T}\right)+e^{\eta T}-1\right\}z.
\end{align} 
If the representative agent's initial wealth level $x\geq \hat{x}_0(z)$, then the portfolio feedback function $\theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)$ admits the following explicit expression, for $(t,x,z)\in[0,T]\times [\hat{x}_0(z),+\infty)\times (0,+\infty)$,
\begin{align}\label{MFG-portfolio-Oc}
\theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)&=\frac{(1-\lambda)\sigma_Z\mu}{\sigma}e^{\eta (T-t)}z,
\end{align}
and the capital injection strategy $C^{*,f^*}_t=0$ a.s. for $t\in[0,T]$. In this case, the representative agent does not need to inject any capital.

We first comment on two extreme cases.  As the competition weight parameter $\gamma$ tends towards $1$, the constraint condition in \eqref{eq:n-player} becomes  $\overline{V}_t^n \le V_t^{i}+C_t^{i}$. This indicates that the agent is primarily concerned with their relative performance compared to other peers, resulting in the threshold  $\hat{x}_0(z)\equiv 0$ and optimal portfolio $\theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)\equiv 0$. Without the pressure from the market index performance, the agent refrains from making any investments. Conversely, as the competition weight parameter $\gamma$ tends towards $0$, the constraint condition in \eqref{eq:n-player} becomes  $Z_t^i \le V_t^{i}+C_t^{i}$.  In this scenario, the agent's goal is to outperform the market index benchmark $Z_t^i$, disregarding the wealth performance of other agents. Consequently, the mean field game problem reduces to the stochastic control problem discussed in Section 6 of  \cite{BoLiaoYu21} with $\hat{x}_0(z)=(e^{\eta T}-1)z$ and $\theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)=\sigma_Z\mu/\sigma e^{\eta (T-t)}z$.

We next consider the sensitivity analysis on the return rate parameter $\mu_Z$ of the market index process. From \eqref{eq:threshold}, \eqref{MFG-portfolio-Oc}, and the relation $\eta=\mu_Z-\mu\sigma_Z/\sigma$, it follows that $\partial \hat{x}_0/\partial \mu_Z>0$,  and  $\partial \theta^{*,f^*}/\partial \mu_Z>0$, which implies that both the threshold  and the portfolio feedback function increase with respect to $\mu_Z$. When the market index benchmark process has a higher return, the agent will require a higher level of initial wealth and will invest more in the risky asset to outperform the targeted benchmark and avoid capital injection.


If the initial wealth level $x< \hat{x}_0(z)$, capital injection is necessary. In this case, we can numerically calculate the MFE and value function by following the next steps:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] Set the parameters $(\mu,\sigma,\mu_Z,\sigma_Z,\lambda,\rho)$, the time horizon $T$ and the initial level of wealth and benchmark process $(x_0,z_0)$.
\item [(ii)] Calculate the capital injection threshold of initial wealth $\hat{x}_0(z)$ given by \eqref{eq:threshold}, if $x_0\geq \hat{x}_0(z)$, then the portfolio feedback function $\theta^{*,f^*}$ can be computed directly by \eqref{MFG-portfolio-Oc}, the injection strategy $C^{*,f^*}\equiv 0$ and the value function $v(t,x,z)=0$. Otherwise, proceed as following steps.
\item[(iii)]For some $r>0$, compute the corresponding fixed point function $f(r;\cdot)$ of \eqref{eq:map-J} in Lemma \ref{lem:fixpoint-r} through iteration and the value of $x(r)$ given by \eqref{eq:x-r}.
\item [(iv)]Repeat step (iii) with different choice of $r$,  plot the function $r\mapsto x(r)$ and find $r_0$ such that $x(r_0)=x_0$. 
\item[(v)] Finally, compute the fixed point function $f(\cdot;r_0)$ as step (iii). Then we can get the MFG and value function with $f(\cdot;r_0)$ by Theorem \ref{thm:verification}.
\end{itemize}
Let us set the parameters $(\mu,\sigma,\mu_Z,\sigma_Z,\lambda,\rho)=(0.1,0.1,$ $0.2,$ $0.1,0.2,1)$, the time horizon $T=1$ and the initial level of  benchmark process  $z_0=20$. Figure \ref{fig:f}-(a) plots the function $r\mapsto x(r)$, which shows that there exists a unique $r_{\tilde{x}}$ such that $x(r_{\tilde{x}})=\tilde{x}$, i.e., the MFE is unique under the given model parameters. Figure \ref{fig:f}-(b) plots the  fixed point  function $t\mapsto f^*(t)$ under different initial level of wealth process. Then we plot the portfolio feedback function $x\mapsto \theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)$ in Figure \ref{fig:theta}-(a) and value function (i.e., the expectation of the total optimal discounted capital injection) $x\mapsto v(t,x,z)$ in Figure \ref{fig:theta}-(b). In particular, it is interesting to see that $\theta^{*,f^*}(t,x,z)$ is a decreasing function of $x$ for the fixed $t$ and $z$, which seems counter-intuitive. However, we note that the large value of $x$ indicates that the current wealth level of all agents or the population is also high, i.e., the $f(t)$ is very large as well. As a result, in the equilibrium state, it has a larger chance for the agent to inject capital more frequently due to the tracking constraint. To reduce the cost of capital injection, the representative agent in the equilibrium will strategically reduce the allocation in the risky asset when $x$ becomes large to pull down the $f(t)$ to lower the growth rate of the benchmark process. From Figure \ref{fig:f}-(b), it can be observed that under different initial values of the state process $X$, although the function $f^*(t)$ is always non-negative, its monotonicity in terms of $t$ may vary significantly under the impact of initial wealth. In particular, in the situation when $f(t)$ climbs to a very large level at some time $t$, we can see from Figure \ref{fig:f}-(b) that the functions $f(t)$ will start to decrease afterwards, which echos from the observation in \ref{fig:theta}-(a) that the portfolio strategy under MFE is decreasing in $x$ such that the resulting $f(t)$ under MFE will decline when it hits a high level, that is, the representative agent will choose the portfolio strategy to maintain the growth rate of the wealth around a reasonable level.   
