\begin{abstract} % 250/250
Objective: Latent diffusion models (LDMs) could mitigate data scarcity challenges affecting machine learning development for medical image interpretation. The recent CCELLA LDM improved prostate cancer detection performance using synthetic MRI for classifier training but was limited to the axial T2-weighted (AxT2) sequence, did not investigate inter-institutional domain shift, and prioritized radiology over histopathology outcomes. We propose CCELLA++ to address these limitations and improve clinical utility. Methods: CCELLA++ expands CCELLA for simultaneous biparametric prostate MRI (bpMRI) generation, including the AxT2, high b-value diffusion series (HighB) and apparent diffusion coefficient map (ADC). Domain adaptation was investigated by pretraining classifiers on real or LDM-generated synthetic data from an internal institution, followed with fine-tuning on progressively smaller fractions of an out-of-distribution, external dataset. Results: CCELLA++ improved 3D FID for HighB and ADC but not AxT2 (0.013, 0.012, 0.063 respectively) sequences compared to CCELLA (0.060). Classifier pretraining with CCELLA++ bpMRI outperformed real bpMRI in AP and AUC for all domain adaptation scenarios. CCELLA++ pretraining achieved highest classifier performance below 50\% (n=665) external dataset volume. Conclusion: Synthetic bpMRI generated by our method can improve downstream classifier generalization and performance beyond real bpMRI or CCELLA-generated AxT2-only images. Future work should seek to quantify medical image sample quality, balance multi-sequence LDM training, and condition the LDM with additional information. Significance: The proposed CCELLA++ LDM can generate synthetic bpMRI that outperforms real data for domain adaptation with a limited target institution dataset, advancing machine learning development for medical imaging. Our code is available at \url{https://github.com/grabkeem/CCELLA-plus-plus}
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Generative AI \and Latent Diffusion Models \and Medical Image Generation \and Prostate MRI \and Domain Adaptation}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Labeled data scarcity is a common challenge impeding machine learning (ML) advancements within the field of medical image interpretation. Causes for labeled data scarcity include limited institutional cohort size, data sharing challenges surrounding patient privacy and data governance, and the time and financial costs required for expert radiologist annotation~\cite{cheplyginaNotsosupervisedSurveySemisupervised2019,litjensSurveyDeepLearning2017,changDistributedDeepLearning2018}. This in-turn limits the volume of available training data, which can negatively impact ML performance. Two notable strategies for mitigating data scarcity within medical imaging are domain adaptation--adapting a pretrained model to a new domain with limited data volume for the same task~\cite{kimTransferLearningMedical2022}--and generative ML--creating synthetic data to artificially increase ML training data volume~\cite{guoMAISIMedicalAI2024}.

Prostate cancer detection from biparametric MRI (bpMRI) is a medical pathology and imaging method that benefits well from ML-based data scarcity research. Prostate cancer is the second most common cancer type in men globally, with MRI significantly reducing biopsy rates when included during the prostate cancer diagnostic workup~\cite{ProstateCancerStatistics,schootsMagneticResonanceImaging2025}. However, literature reports experience-based inter-reader variability during prostate MRI interpretation~\cite{stabileFactorsInfluencingVariability2020}. Additionally, prostate MRI has relatively low publicly available domain-specific data availability, with the largest public dataset consisting of 1500 samples~\cite{sahaArtificialIntelligenceRadiologists2024}. This low public data availability is reflected within latent diffusion model (LDM) for medical MRI synthesis literature, where prostate MRI generation is investigated much less than brain MRI which has a plethora of publicly available data~\cite{fanSurveyEmergingApplications2024}. LDM studies for prostate MRI synthesis additionally tend to be unimodal~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025,wildeMedicalDiffusionBudget2024}, are limited to 2D images~\cite{wildeMedicalDiffusionBudget2024,saeedBiparametricProstateMR2023}, and restricted to use of curated keywords or short phrases that remove much of the rich textual information present in full-text clinical radiology reports~\cite{saeedBiparametricProstateMR2023}. Each of these limitations hinder the application of LDMs for clinically-motivated ML development within the domain of prostate cancer detection from MRI.

A recent study proposed the ``CCELLA-centric LDM framework'' for generating labeled, synthetic 3D prostate axial T2-weighted MRI (AxT2) using the full radiology report text generated during clinical routine~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025}. Beyond surpassing the performance of a recent LDM foundation model~\cite{guoMAISIMedicalAI2024}, the authors demonstrated that adding synthetic images generated with their framework to a real multi-center dataset improved the performance of a classifier for prostate cancer detection from MRI beyond training on real data alone. However, this study presented a few key limitations. Firstly, it only used the AxT2 sequence, whereas clinically-relevant prostate bpMRI additionally consists of a high b-value diffusion-weighted image (HighB) and the apparent diffusion coefficient map (ADC)~\cite{pesapaneComparisonSensitivitySpecificity2021}. Secondly, their classifier dataset spanned multiple institutions but investigated neither the domain shift between these institutions, nor the training utility of institution-specific synthetic data in an inter-institutional data sharing scenario. Finally, the study prioritized radiology classification over the more clinically relevant histopathology outcomes for both LDM conditioning and classifier prediction.

This study extended the CCELLA-centric LDM framework from~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025} toward improved downstream performance and medical domain relevance while addressing the above limitations. Specifically, we: 1) developed CCELLA++ which expands CCELLA to simultaneously generate all three bpMRI sequences, 2) conducted a series of domain adaptation experiments to investigate the usability of pretraining on synthetic data for fine-tuning on a data-limited, out-of-distribution dataset for the purpose of predicting prostate cancer histopathology results from MRI. It was hypothesized that synthetic bpMRI should improve classifier performance beyond synthetic AxT2 alone, as the added HighB and ADC series are integral to prostate MRI interpretation~\cite{americancollegeofradiologyPIRADSProstateImaging2019}.

We performed domain adaptation experiments that analyzed the usability of LDM-generated synthetic images when training classifiers for prostate cancer prediction from MRI in a hypothetical inter-institutional data sharing scenario. Specifically, we assessed domain adaptation across two disjointed, artificial institutions: one larger silo sourced from one private institution (Institution 1), and one smaller silo sourced from two public, multi-center datasets (Institution 2). We investigated the performance of classifiers trained on progressively smaller real data volumes from Institution 2, and compared this to classifiers first pretrained with real or LDM-generated synthetic data from Institution 1 and then fine-tuned with Institution 2 data. To assess our CCELLA++ method, we performed these domain adaptation experiments for both AxT2-only with synthetic data generated by CCELLA and bpMRI with synthetic data from CCELLA++. It was hypothesized that out-of-distribution pretraining would outperform training from scratch at smaller Institution 2 data volumes, with synthetic data performing comparably to real data for pretraining. It was also hypothesized that CCELLA++ pretraining would outperform CCELLA pretraining.

In summary, this paper makes the following contributions:
\begin{itemize}
	\item CCELLA++, an expansion of the CCELLA LDM framework to enable simultaneous bpMRI generation.
	\item An investigation on classifier performance when pretrained on out-of-distribution synthetic data prior to domain adaptation.
	\item An investigation on the effects of using CCELLA++ bpMRI versus CCELLA AxT2 images within a domain adaptation scenario.
\end{itemize}


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:relatedwork}

\subsection{Latent Diffusion Models for Medical Imaging}
Latent diffusion models (LDM) are a recent generative ML model architecture composed of a volume-compressing vector-quantized autoencoder (VQ-VAE) and a U-Net for latent image denoising~\cite{rombachHighResolutionImageSynthesis2021}. The VQ-VAE encoder first encodes an image to a fixed-distribution latent space. Gaussian noise is added to the latent image repeatedly over many (e.g. 1000) time steps. The U-Net is then trained to predict the noise added in each time step, thereby enabling a removal of the added Gaussian noise (i.e. denoising). On inference, randomly generated Gaussian noise is repeatedly denoised by the U-Net from time steps 1000 to 0. This denoised latent is then decoded by the VQ-VAE decoder head to produce the resultant synthetic image.

Medical imaging literature has seen a recent surge in the number of LDM studies. Reported motivations for medical LDM use include augmenting datasets for rare pathologies and alleviating data annotation costs~\cite{kazerouniDiffusionModelsMedical2023}. Applications of LDMs within this space include image generation, image reconstruction, and segmentation~\cite{fanSurveyEmergingApplications2024}. While medical LDMs for MRI generation are typically uni-modal, recent literature has explored generating multiple MRI sequences for the same exam~\cite{kazerouniDiffusionModelsMedical2023}. For example, CoLa-Diff conditioned the denoising U-Net with multiple brain MRI sequences and anatomical segmentations to generate a different MRI modality~\cite{jiangCoLaDiffConditionalLatent2023}. Cross-conditioned Diffusion Model used the distributions of desired target modalities to generate images in those modalities given source images~\cite{xingCrossconditionedDiffusionModel2024}. Lüpke et al. developed a physics-informed diffusion method that first generates physical tissue property maps for each MRI sequence which are then mapped into the individual sequence images by a physical signal model~\cite{lupkePhysicsInformedLatentDiffusion2024}.

Grabke et al.~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025} is described above and is the most similar LDM work to our study, though with a few critical differences. Firstly, our study expanded the CCELLA-centric LDM framework to accommodate simultaneous generation of all bpMRI series for a single exam. We also investigated the effects of inter-institutional domain adaptation when pretraining a classifier on real and/or synthetic data. Finally, our work prioritized histopathology classification over radiology classification, which is more clinically relevant for prostate MRI~\cite{sahaArtificialIntelligenceRadiologists2024}.


\subsection{Domain Shift and Adaptation}
Transfer learning occurs when learned knowledge from a pretrained ML model is used to improve the performance of a new model on a new task~\cite{panSurveyTransferLearning2010}. A primary motivation for transfer learning is to mitigate data scarcity, enabling improved performance on reduced training data. Domain shift is a common challenge to transfer learning, occurring when the target dataset is of different distribution than the pretrained source dataset~\cite{moreno-torresUnifyingViewDataset2012}. Domain shift sources include noise, label imbalance, and incomplete samples. Within medical imaging, domain shift can occur from differences in patient population, imaging machine properties and image acquisition protocols~\cite{guanDomainAdaptationMedical2022,mataArtificialIntelligenceAssisted2021}. Domain adaptation is a subset of transfer learning that seeks to mitigate domain shift.

One commonly successful method for domain adaptation is fine-tuning, where a model is first pretrained on a large dataset followed by fine-tuning on the smaller target dataset~\cite{chenCloserLookFewshot2020,dhillonBaselineFewShotImage2020}. Fine-tuning models, either partially or entirely at a lower learning rate, has yielded performance increases within recent medical imaging ML literature~\cite{motamedTransferLearningApproach2019,candemirTrainingStrategiesRadiology2021}.

Brugnara et al. is similar in concept to our study~\cite{brugnaraAddressingGeneralizabilityAI2024}. The authors of this study proposed the use of synthetic brain MRI from a generative adversarial network (GAN) to mitigate inter-institutional domain shift when training classifier models. The authors demonstrated that training classifier models on a combination of real and synthetic data improved classifier performance on test data from an external, unseen institution while maintaining test set performance on the local institution compared to training on real data alone~\cite{brugnaraAddressingGeneralizabilityAI2024}.

Our work differs in multiple ways to~\cite{brugnaraAddressingGeneralizabilityAI2024}. Our study performed a more comprehensive domain adaptation experiment by training on multiple data subsets from the external institution, in addition to the zero-shot (i.e. no external training) generalization assessment present in~\cite{brugnaraAddressingGeneralizabilityAI2024}. We additionally assessed the performances of models trained independently on real or synthetic data rather than only assessing their combination. Our study also used and improved upon an LDM framework instead of using a GAN for synthetic image generation. Finally, our study focused on prostate instead of brain imaging.

%% METHODS
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}
\subsection{Dataset}
This retrospective study used biparametric MRI (bpMRI) of men at high-risk for prostate cancer. This study was approved by an institutional research ethics board with the need for informed consent waived. Two non-overlapping datasets were sourced for this study, forming the two simulated institutional datasets (Institutions). The first dataset (Institution 1) consisted of 4152 images sourced from one healthcare institution. The second dataset (Institution 2) consisted of 1658 images sourced from two publicly available datasets (1500 from the PI-CAI Grand Challenge~\cite{sahaArtificialIntelligenceRadiologists2024}, 158 from the Prostate158 dataset~\cite{adamsProstate158Expertannotated3T2022}). Institution 1 inclusion criteria were referral for prostate MRI, being treatment naïve, and MRI date between April 2009 and August 2021. Exclusion criteria were non-diagnostic image quality or incomplete exam. Institution 2 inclusion and exclusion criteria have been published~\cite{sahaArtificialIntelligenceRadiologists2024,adamsProstate158Expertannotated3T2022}.

Every bpMRI comprised three sequences: 1) the axial T2-weighted (AxT2) sequence, 2) diffusion weighted imaging at b$\ge$1000 s/mm\textsuperscript{2} (HighB), 3) bespoke apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) maps. All HighB sequence b-values for Institution 1 were calculated at b=1600 s/mm\textsuperscript{2} whereas the Institution 2 HighB b-values varied. Institution 1 studies also included full radiology report texts as generated during clinical routine. All bpMRI images and reports were anonymized prior to this study. Images and radiology classifications from Institution 1 were adjusted as necessary to abide by the latest prostate MRI reporting guidelines (PI-RADS)~\cite{americancollegeofradiologyPIRADSProstateImaging2019} prior to this study under the supervision of a trained radiologist with over 20 years specific experience in prostate MRI interpretation. All exams had either a PI-RADS score or histopathology results from within 6 months of the MRI. In keeping with the PI-CAI Grand Challenge reporting standards, exams were assigned their class label (i.e. positive or negative) based on their histopathology results, using PI-RADS score only if histopathology information was missing~\cite{sahaArtificialIntelligenceRadiologists2024}. Images were preprocessed for LDM and classifier use per the methods described in~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025}.

Whole gland (WG) segmentations for each exam were used for data preprocessing. WG segmentations for Institution 1 MRI exams were produced by a pretrained nnU-Net, trained prior to this study on data outside of both Institutional test sets. A model was used for WG segmentation in lieu of human experts to keep with the reduced manual annotation motivation from the CCELLA study~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025}. Institution 2 WG segmentations were already present in their source public datasets and used as-is.

Each institutional dataset was randomly split into train, validation, and test sets targeting a ratio of 80:5:15. Splits were proportional by class within the respective Institution and occurred on a per-patient level to ensure all images from one patient were contained to a single split. Final train, validation, and test split sizes were 3324, 208, 620 for Institution 1 and 1329, 82, 247 for Institution 2 (respectively). Test sets were held out, unseen by all models until the end of the experiment when models were run once against these images to evaluate performance metrics. Figure \ref{figdataflow} depicts the data flow and partitions used in all parts of this study.

