%% DISCUSSION
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
The objectives of this study were 1) to extend the CCELLA-centric LDM framework from~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025} for simultaneous bpMRI generation, and 2) to conduct a series of domain adaptation studies assessing the value of synthetic data pretraining for fine-tuning on out-of-distribution data compared to the real data pretraining and no pretraining scenarios.

When comparing LDM FIDs, CCELLA++ achieved better HighB and ADC but slightly worse AxT2 3D FID than the CCELLA AxT2. Within CCELLA++, the ADC sequence achieved best sequence performance in all FID metrics. A likely explanation for the HighB 2D FID being much worse than the ADC despite comparable 3D FIDs is the normalization method used for each FID calculation. Because the 2D and 3D FID models required different normalization techniques (linear rescaling versus z-score normalization respectively), the comparatively narrow signal intensity range of the HighB sequence seen in Table~\ref{table2} could have caused outliers to be over-emphasized during linear rescaling. This reaffirms the intent of this study to consider 3D FID as the primary LDM performance metric, with the 2D FID metrics calculated for consistency with the relevant baseline LDM studies~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025,guoMAISIMedicalAI2024}. Compared to the 0.025 3D FID in~\cite{grabkePromptGuidedLatentDiffusion2025}, 3D AxT2 FID performances in this study were worse but HighB and ADC 3D FID were better. The likely explanation for the poorer AxT2 FID performances is the smaller training dataset size used for this study (3532 versus 5071). Future work should seek to explore the effect of training volume on LDM performance, with intent to find a saturation point beyond which performance returns diminish or plateau.

This study performed domain adaptation experiments for both CCELLA and CCELLA++. Overall, the CCELLA experiment demonstrated that Institution 1 pretraining resulted in improved performance compared to training from scratch. Real versus synthetic Institution 1 pretraining did not demonstrate a clear performance difference. The trend reversal, where no pretraining outperformed pretraining, at only the 25\% partition is likely due to the quality of training selected samples at that partition. Training every classifier in triplicate was intended to mitigate the effects of individual sample quality on model training, however this would not have guaranteed similar average sample quality across all (especially the smaller) data partitions.

In contrast, the CCELLA++ domain adaptation experiment depicts a clear trend. Synthetic data pretraining provided a clear performance improvement until the 50\% partition, after which training from scratch performed better. Notably, synthetic outperformed real data pretraining in all scenarios. Training from scratch outperformed real data pretraining for all except the smallest partition. These results align with the trend described in~\cite{brugnaraAddressingGeneralizabilityAI2024}, where using synthetic data resulted in higher generalizability than real data alone.

When training from scratch within Institution 2, classifier training with bpMRI outperformed AxT2-only for prostate cancer detection. This is consistent with clinical prostate reporting guidelines placing emphasis on the HighB and ADC sequences~\cite{americancollegeofradiologyPIRADSProstateImaging2019}. Pretraining on Institution 1 synthetic data maintained the trend of bpMRI consistently outperforming AxT2-only for AP but not AUC. Synthetic pretraining AUC and both real pretraining metrics only held this trend at 25\% and above. This is likely due to the differences in mean ADC voxel intensity between Institution 1 and Institution 2. The 41-48\% increase in mean ADC intensity despite only a 9\% upper bound increase shown in Table~\ref{table2} would have shifted the normalized intensity of this sequence between Institutions. Thus, fine-tuning on the Institution 2 ADC sequence after Institution 1 pretraining likely required more training on more data to overcome this domain shift, hence the bpMRI performance increases only becoming apparent at higher Institution 2 partition sizes.

This study presented a few limitations and opportunities for future work. Firstly, this study did not directly investigate individual data sample quality. Sample quality variance likely contributed to the variances in classifier performance at smaller data partitions despite efforts to mitigate this through metric averaging. It would be worthwhile to investigate methods for quantifying individual real and synthetic medical image quality. Secondly, this study explored latent concatenation for generating multiple bpMRI sequences in order to reuse the pretrained VQ-VAE. Future work should seek to compare the results from this study to different multi-sequence generation methods. Finally, CCELLA++ exhibited slightly worse 3D AxT2 FID than CCELLA. This presents an opportunity for future work to improve CCELLA++ performance, either by improving the quality of each sequence or by incorporating additional LDM conditioning information such as biopsy data or anatomical segmentations.

%% CONCLUSION
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In summary, this study presents CCELLA++ for synthetic multi-sequence prostate MRI generation as well as a domain adaptation experiment investigating the utility of synthetic data for pretraining before fine-tuning on out-of-distribution data. This study successfully demonstrated that classifier performance when predicting prostate cancer from MRI improves when trained on synthetic bpMRI generated by our method versus synthetic AxT2-alone generated by its predecessor. This study also demonstrated that our method's synthetic bpMRI data outperforms real bpMRI data when pretraining a classifier for domain adaptation to a second institution regardless of external institution data volume. During domain adaptation, this study also demonstrated that synthetic bpMRI data pretraining outperforms training from scratch at smaller training dataset sizes. 

These results reaffirm the findings from existing literature that models trained on synthetic data can be more generalizable to external, out-of-distribution institutions than training only on real data. These results also suggest that pretraining on synthetic data can result in improved performance compared to training from scratch on small out-of-distribution datasets. Future work should explore methods for quantifying medical image sample quality, balancing multi-sequence LDM training, and conditioning the LDM with additional information.

