\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
\begin{figure}[th]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.475\textwidth]{figure1.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Motivation}: ID features are effective but have limited benefits in MCFRec. (a) Comparison of ID-based and ID-free MCFRec method. (b) ID-Text distances are much closer than ID-Image and Text-Image distances in three public datasets, indicating that ID features can be replaced by text features. (c) Replacing ID features with text features in MCFRec (FREEDOM~\cite{zhou2023tale}) leads to a smaller Recall@20 drop than in CFRec (LightGCN~\cite{he2020lightgcn}), indicating that text features have the potential to completely replace ID features. }
  \label{fig:motivation}
\end{figure}

Multimodal collaborative filtering recommendation (MCFRec) methods achieve more accurate recommendations than traditional collaborative filtering recommendation (CFRec) methods by introducing multimodal information such as text, images, and audio. MCFRec has shown significant application prospects in e-commerce, social media, video platforms, and other fields. Most existing MCFRec methods are ID-based, using ID features as key information and multimodal features as auxiliary information to achieve effective recommendations~\cite{zhou2023tale, tao2022self, zhou2023selfcf, su2024soil, zhou2023enhancing}. However, ID features inherently have less semantic information than multimodal features and may lead to generalization problems~\cite{zhang2024ninerec, yuan2023where}. Therefore, whether MCFRec methods should be ID-based or ID-free (as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:motivation}(a)) in the multimodal era has sparked discussions~\cite{yuan2023where, lin2025unified}.

Since ID-based MCFRec methods have achieved the best recommendation results, it has sparked our exploration of the effectiveness of ID features. Firstly, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:motivation}(a), we calculate the average Euclidean distance between the ID features and text features (ID-Text), the ID features and image features (ID-Image), and the text features and image features (Text-Image) on three public datasets. Specifically, the ID features are taken as initial 64-dimensional embeddings, while the text and image features are reduced to 64 dimensions using the PCA algorithm. On three datasets, the distance of ID-Text is much smaller (about $70 \times$) than the distance ID-Image and the distance of Text-Image. This indicates that ID features may be replaced by text features. Furthermore, we consider replacing the item ID features with text features in existing ID-based CFRec method (LightGCN~\cite{he2020lightgcn}) and ID-based MCFRec method (FREEDOM~\cite{zhou2023tale}) to verify whether ID features can be replaced. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:motivation}(b), the experimental results demonstrate that on three datasets, the FREEDOM (Text) only decreases by an average of 7.87\% in Recall@20, while the LightGCN (Text) decreases by an average of 27.38\%, which indicates that text features have the potential to completely replace ID features.

Among the above experiments, we believe that \textbf{\textit{ID features are effective but have limited benefits in MCFRec}}. This further inspires us to analyze the advantages and disadvantages of ID features in existing MCFRec methods, aiming to retain the advantages and eliminate the disadvantages to achieve true ID-free in MCFRec. The pros and cons of ID features in MCFRec are as follows:

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=lightblue,
  colframe=blue!40,
  width=.475\textwidth,
  arc=2mm, auto outer arc,]
  \ding{182} ID features provide initial trainable embeddings for users and items, \textbf{\textit{but lack semantic richness.}}\\
  \ding{183} ID features provide a unique identifier for each user and item, \textbf{\textit{but hinder generalization to untrained data.}}\\
  \ding{184} ID features assist in aligning and fusing multimodal features, \textbf{\textit{but may lead to representation shift.}}
\end{tcolorbox}

To verify the correctness of our viewpoint and achieve ID-free MCFRec, we propose an \textbf{ID}-free multimodal collaborative \textbf{F}iltering \textbf{RE}comm\textbf{E}ndation baseline, called \textbf{IDFREE}. For ID-free embedding, we use the item multimodal features as the initial embedding of items, further combine the user-item interaction relationship to obtain the initial embedding of users. Then, all users and items essentially form two different ordered sequences. We construct unique position embeddings based on the number of users and items to achieve unique identifiers. ID-free embeddings are obtained by combining multimodal initial embeddings and positional embeddings. For ID-free multimodal collaborative filtering, we focus on the semantic information of the modalities themselves. We propose an adaptive similarity graph module to construct dynamic user-user and item-item graphs based on multimodal features. Furthermore, we propose an augmented user-item graph encoder to improve the users and items encoding. For ID-free loss, we use contrastive learning between modalities to achieve semantic alignment between modalities. Additionally, we use Softmax loss as recommendation loss. Considering recommendation loss and multimodal alignment loss together, ID-free multimodal collaborative filtering recommendation is realized. The contributions of our work are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
  \item We summarize the main pros and cons of ID features in multimodal collaborative filtering: as initial embeddings but lack semantic richness, provide unique identifiers but bring generalization problem, and assist in multimodal alignment and fusion but may lead to representation shift.
  \item Based on the above insights, we propose \textbf{IDFREE}, an \textbf{ID}-free multimodal collaborative \textbf{F}iltering \textbf{RE}comm\textbf{E}ndation baseline. We introduce multimodal features and positional encodings to achieve generic ID-free embedding for users and items. Furthermore, we propose an adaptive similarity graph module to compute dynamic user-user and item-item graphs based on multimodal features.
  \item We validate the effectiveness of the IDFREE on three public datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that IDFREE achieves an average performance improvement of 72.24\% on all metrics (Recall@5, 10, 20, 50 and NDCG@5, 10, 20, 50) across all datasets. Exploratory and extended experiments further validate our findings on the limitations of ID features in MCFRec.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}
\subsection{ID-based Multimodal Collaborative Filtering Recommendation}
ID-based multimodal collaborative filtering recommendation (MCFRec) methods combine ID-based collaborative filtering recommendation (CFRec) with multimodal content representation to improve recommendation effectiveness~\cite{xu2025survey}. The BPR method~\cite{rendle2012bpr} in recommendations and LightGCN~\cite{he2020lightgcn} based on graph learning are recognized as effective methods in traditional collaborative filtering. Existing MCFRec methods mostly follow the framework of CFRec and mainly improve from the perspective of multimodal fusion and alignment. For example, VBPR~\cite{he2016vbpr} first extracts visual features and simply concatenates them with ID embeddings for fusion. MMGCN~\cite{wei2019mmgcn} further adopts the LightGCN architecture, constructs a user-item graph of conscious modalities, and achieves multimodal feature fusion. DualGNN~\cite{wang2021dualgnn}, LATTICE~\cite{zhang2021mining}, FREEDOM~\cite{zhou2023tale}, and other methods~\cite{zhou2023enhancing, yu2023multi} further conduct research on the construction of user-user and item-item graphs to achieve more effective multimodal fusion. Meanwhile, FETTLE~\cite{li2024align}, SOIL~\cite{su2024soil}, MENTOR~\cite{xu2024mentor}, and other methods~\cite{zhou2023bm3} improve from the perspective of contrastive learning to achieve semantic alignment of multimodal features. In general, ID-based MCFRec methods have made significant progress in multimodal fusion and alignment, but further exploration is needed on the effectiveness of ID features. The conclusion in~\cite{yuan2023where} is that MCFRec is more advantageous when it includes ID features. We believe that their research conclusion is one-sided and limited because existing ID-based MCFRec methods have not been designed for the ID-free case.

\subsection{ID-free Multimodal Recommendation}
ID-free multimodal recommendation is still in its infancy and mainly focuses on sequential recommendation (SRec), with few explorations in MCFRec. The study~\cite{yuan2023where} explores the effects of ID-based and ID-free methods in multimodal SRec, finding that relying solely on multimodal information can achieve better recommendation results. Subsequently, there have been recommendation methods in multimodal SRec that decouple ID and multimodal features or even do not rely on ID information. Studies~\cite{zhang2024disentangling, deng2024end, lin2025unified} focus on aligning and fusing multimodal semantic information, treating ID as equal or auxiliary information to achieve effective recommendations. MMSR~\cite{lin2025unified} proposes a general framework for multimodal sequential recommendation that ignores ID features and demonstrates improvements in cold start and transfer learning. NineRec~\cite{zhang2024ninerec} points out the drawbacks of ID features in transfer learning and proposes datasets and benchmarks for transfer learning in SRec. Additionally, some scholars have explored the replacement of ID features through semantic IDs~\cite{zhu2025unimodal, zheng2025enhancing}. These methods construct multimodal embeddings and then use codebook techniques to convert the embeddings into semantic IDs for recommendation. However, there are only few works based on large language models (LLMs)~\cite{li2023exploring, sheng2024language} in MCFRec that explore ID-free methods by using LLMs to process textual information of items and replacing item ID features with output text features. However, these works do not consider the fusion and alignment of multimodal features such as text and images, nor do they achieve true ID-free since users still use ID features.

