\section{Methodology}\label{sec:methodology}
\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{subsec:preliminaries}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Problem Definition}}
Given user set $U=\{1, 2,\dots, u, \dots,|U|\}$ and item set $I=\{1, 2,\dots, i, \dots, |I|\}$, the user-item interaction matrix $\mathbf{R} \in \mathbb{R}^{|U| \times |I|}$, where $\mathbf{R}_{u,i}$ indicates the interaction behavior between user $u$ and item $i$, with $\mathbf{R}_{u,i} = 1$ or $0$ indicating whether the user and item have interacted. The input of multimodal recommendation includes multiple modalities such as text $t$, image $v$, etc., and the multimodal set is defined as $M=\{t, v\}$. The multimodal feature representation of item $i$ is denoted as $X_i^M=\{x_i^t, x_i^v\}$, where $x_i^t$ and $x_i^v$ represent the text and image features of item $i$, respectively. The goal of MCFRec is to predict the rating of user $u$ on item $i$ based on the multimodal features of all items $X_I^M$ and the user-item interaction matrix $\mathbf{R}$.

\subsubsection{\textbf{User Multimodal Embedding Calculation}}
In the absence of user ID features, users lack initial embeddings. We construct the initial embeddings of users based on $X_I^M$ and  $\mathbf{R}$. For the text modality, the textual embedding of user $u$ is calculated as Eq.~(\ref{eq:user_text_embedding}):
\begin{equation}
  x_u^t = \frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^{|I|} \mathbf{R}_{u,i}}\sum_{i=1}^{|I|} (\mathbf{R}_{u,i} \cdot x_i^t), \label{eq:user_text_embedding}
\end{equation}
which is the average of the textual features of all items that user $u$ has interacted with. Similarly, the image embedding of the user $x_u^v$ can also be calculated. Finally, the multimodal embedding of the user is $X_u^M = \{x_u^t, x_u^v\}$.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Multimodal User-User and Item-Item Graph Construction}}\label{subsubsec:m_graph_construction}
We construct the basic user-user and item-item graphs based on the initial multimodal embeddings of users and items, which are used for the adaptive similarity graph module. As shown in Eq.~(\ref{eq:user_user_graph}):
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{S}_{u,u^\prime}^t = 
\begin{cases} 
\frac{x_u^t \cdot x_{u^\prime}^t}{\|x_u^t\|_2 \cdot \|x_{u^\prime}^t\|_2} & \text{if } \mathbf{S}_{u,{u^\prime}}^t \text{ is among the top } k \text{ for user } u \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\label{eq:user_user_graph}
\end{equation}
For the user-user graph, we firstly calculate the similarity value $\mathbf{S}_{u,{u^\prime}}^t$, that represents the text modality relationship between user $u$ and user $u^\prime$. Then, we can obtain the similarity graph $\mathbf{S}_U^t$ under the text modality. Similarly, we can obtain the user-user similarity graph $\mathbf{S}_U^v$ through the image modality and item-item similarity graph $\mathbf{S}_I^t$ and $\mathbf{S}_I^v$. The multimodal user-user graph $\mathbf{R}_U$ and item-item graph $\mathbf{R}_I$ are calculated based on Eq.~(\ref{eq:multi_user_item_graph}):
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{R}_U = \mathbf{S}_U^t + \mathbf{S}_U^v, \quad \mathbf{R}_I = \mathbf{S}_I^t + \mathbf{S}_I^v. \label{eq:multi_user_item_graph}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Overview of ID-free Baseline: IDFREE} \label{subsec:overview}
The architecture of the proposed ID-free MCFRec baseline IDFREE is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}. \textbf{In ID-free embedding}, to achieve the effect of initial embedding and unique identifiers provided by ID features, we use multimodal features as the initial embeddings of users and items and combine them with Positional Encoding (PE) module to construct ID-free embeddings. \textbf{In ID-free multimodal collaborative filtering}, to better utilize multimodal features in ID-free situation, we propose an Adaptive Similarity Graph (ASG) module to compute dynamic user-user and item-item graphs based on multimodal features. Furthermore, we propose an Augmented user-item Graph Encoder (AGE) that uses the dynamically augmented user-item interaction graph for effective encoding of users and items. \textbf{In ID-free loss}, we propose a holistic optimization objective that comprehensively considers the multimodal alignment loss and recommendation loss under ID-free condition to achieve ID-free MCFRec.

\subsection{ID-free Embedding}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Initial Embedding}}\label{subsubsec:initial_embedding}
For user $u$ and item $i$, the initial embedding needs to align the dimensions of text and image features obtained from different pre-trained models. Specifically, we use linear transformation to map multimodal features to the same dimension and perform the operation in Eq.~(\ref{eq:initial_embedding}):
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  h_{i(u)}^t &= \text{LN}(\sigma(x_{i(u)}^t \cdot \mathbf{W}^t + b^t)), \\
  h_{i(u)}^v &= \text{LN}(\sigma(x_{i(u)}^v \cdot \mathbf{W}^v + b^v)), 
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:initial_embedding}
\end{equation}
where $h_{i(u)}^t$ and $h_{i(u)}^v$ represent the text and image initial embeddings of item $i$ or user $u$. $\mathbf{W}^t$, $\mathbf{W}^v$, $b^t$, and $b^v$ are trainable parameters. $\sigma(\cdot)$ represents the Tanh activation function. $\text{LN}(\cdot)$ represents the LayerNorm operation. Finally, the multimodal features of users and items are uniformly mapped to $d$ dimensions, where $h_i^t, h_i^v, h_u^t, h_u^v \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Positional Encoding}} \label{subsubsec:positional_encoding}
Positional encoding is mainly used to achieve unique identifiers for users and items. The user set $U$ and item set $I$ can be viewed as user sequences and item sequences arranged in ID order, respectively. Inspired by the positional encoding method in Transformer~\cite{vaswani2017attention}, we adopt similar method to map the position of users and items to unique embeddings. The formula for positional encoding $\text{PE}(\cdot)$ is as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \text{PE}(pos, 2j) = \sin\left(\frac{pos}{10000^{\frac{2j}{d}}}\right), \quad \text{PE}(pos, 2j+1) = \cos\left(\frac{pos}{10000^{\frac{2j}{d}}}\right), \label{eq:positional_encoding}
\end{equation}
where $pos$ represents the position of the user or item in the sequence, and $j$ represents the $j$-th position of the embedding dimension $d$. Finally, the positional encoding of user $u$ and item $i$ are denoted as $p_u, p_i \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$. Afterward, as shown in Eq.~(\ref{eq:positional_encoding_embedding}), the ID-free embeddings of user $u$ and item $i$ are obtained.
\begin{equation}
  \widetilde{h}_{i(u)}^t = h_{i(u)}^t + p_{i(u)}, \quad \widetilde{h}_{i(u)}^v = h_{i(u)}^v + p_{i(u)}.
\label{eq:positional_encoding_embedding}
\end{equation}

\subsection{ID-free Multimodal Collaborative Filtering}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Adaptive Similarity Graph}} \label{subsubsec:adaptive_similarity_graph}
Existing methods~\cite{yu2023multi, zhang2021mining, zhou2023tale} mostly construct item-item graph through inter-item similarity computation in the preprocessing stage. To better utilize multimodal features in the ID-free situation, we consider dynamically calculating user-user and item-item graphs based on multimodal features during the training process. However, directly calculating feature similarity between each user and each item during training has a time complexity of $O(|U|^2d+|I|^2d)$. Inspired by the graph generation in~\cite{yang2024adaptive}, we perform adaptive similarity graph construction on the pre-computed $\mathbf{R}_U$ and $\mathbf{R}_I$ in \S~\ref{subsubsec:m_graph_construction}.

For the user-user adaptive similarity graph, the user-user graph $\mathbf{R}_U$ can be represented from the perspective of the user node set $U$ and the edge set $E$ as $(U, E)$. For the $c$-th edge $e_c$, $e_c \in E$, the two user nodes are $u_{c}$ and $u_{c}^\prime$. We use $\widetilde{h}_{u_{c}}^t$ and $\widetilde{h}_{u_{c}^\prime}^v$ to calculate the adaptive weight $w_c$ of the edge $e_c$, as shown in Eq.~(\ref{eq:adaptive_weight}).
\begin{equation}
  w_c = \text{sigmoid}(\left[\delta(\widetilde{h}_{u_{c}}^t \cdot \mathbf{W}_0 + b_0) \odot \delta(\widetilde{h}_{u_{c}^\prime}^v \cdot \mathbf{W}_1 + b_1)\right] \cdot \mathbf{1}), 
  \label{eq:adaptive_weight}
\end{equation}
where $\delta(\cdot)$ represents the ReLU activation function, and $\mathbf{W}_0, \mathbf{W}_1, b_0, b_1$ are trainable parameters with $\mathbf{W}_0, \mathbf{W}_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$. $\mathbf{1} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times 1}$ has all values as 1. The time complexity of the above operation is only $O(|E|d)=O(k|U|d)<<O(|U|^2d)$. Furthermore, we multiply the calculated adaptive weight $w_c$ with the corresponding edge value to obtain the adaptive similarity graph $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_U$. Similarly, we can obtain $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_I$. During inference, we dynamically calculate $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_U$ and $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_I$ by Eq.~(\ref{eq:user_user_graph}) based on the multimodal fused user and item embeddings.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Augmented user-item Graph Encoder}} \label{subsubsec:augmented_user_item_graph_encoder}
The augmented user-item graph encoder module mainly achieves the fusion and encoding of multimodal features between users and items. First, we fuse the multimodal features of user $u$ and item $i$ to obtain the user embedding $\widehat{h}_u$ and the item embedding $\widehat{h}_i$, as shown in Eq.~(\ref{eq:init_encoding_embedding}). Here, $\alpha$ represents the fusion weight.
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{h}_u = \alpha \cdot \widetilde{h}_u^t + (1 - \alpha) \cdot \widetilde{h}_u^v, \quad \widehat{h}_i = \alpha \cdot \widetilde{h}_i^t + (1 - \alpha) \cdot \widetilde{h}_i^v.
  \label{eq:init_encoding_embedding}
\end{equation}

Then, based on $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_U$ and $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_I$, we enhance the user-item interaction graph $\mathbf{R}$ to obtain the augmented user-item graph $\mathbf{A}$. Additionally, we use the hyperparameter $\rho$ to denoise $\mathbf{A}$ during training and directly use $\mathbf{A}$ during inference similar to~\cite{zhou2023tale}. To regularize $\mathbf{A}$, we calculate the Laplacian regularization matrix $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}$. The formula is as Eq.~(\ref{eq:augmented_graph}):
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{A} = \left| \begin{matrix}
    \overline{\mathbf{R}}_U&		\mathbf{R}\\
    \mathbf{R}^T&		\overline{\mathbf{R}}_I\\
    \end{matrix} \right|, \quad 
  \widehat{\mathbf{A}} = \mathbf{D}^{-\frac{1}{2}} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{D}^{-\frac{1}{2}}.
  \label{eq:augmented_graph}
\end{equation}

Furthermore, based on $\widehat{\mathbf{A}}$, we use light graph convolutional neural network (LightGCN~\cite{he2020lightgcn}) to build collaborative filtering encodings of users and items. 
The input of the 0-th layer $\widehat{\mathbf{H}}_{UI}^0=\widehat{\mathbf{H}}_U \oplus \widehat{\mathbf{H}}_I$, $\widehat{\mathbf{H}}_U[u, :] = \widehat{h}_u$, $\widehat{\mathbf{H}}_I[i, :]=\widehat{h}_i$, $\widehat{\mathbf{H}}_{UI}^0 \in \mathbb{R}^{(|U|+|I|) \times d}$, $\oplus$ represents the concatenation operation. Finally, the average of the output of $ L $ layers of graph convolution is taken as the final encoding of users and items $\mathbf{E}_U, \mathbf{E}_I$. The formula is as Eq.~(\ref{eq:final_encoding}):
\begin{equation}
  [\mathbf{E}_U, \mathbf{E}_I] = \frac{1}{L} \sum_{l=1}^{L} (\widehat{\mathbf{A}} \cdot \widehat{\mathbf{H}}_{UI}^{l-1}).
  \label{eq:final_encoding}
\end{equation}

\subsection{ID-free Loss}\label{subsec:loss}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Multimodal Alignment Loss}} \label{subsubsec:multimodal_alignment}
In the ID-free case, we perform inter-modal alignment based on the multimodal features. We use InfoNCE~\cite{oord2018representation} loss to achieve semantic alignment between modalities. For the multimodal alignment loss $\mathcal{L}_\text{u}$ of users, we align the text modality to the image modality by Eq.~(\ref{eq:multimodal_alignment_u}), which treats the image modality of the same user as a positive sample and the image modality of other users as negative samples.
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{u} = -\frac{1}{B} \sum_{u\in U_B} \log \frac{\exp(\text{sim}(\widetilde{h}_u^t, \widetilde{h}_u^v) / \tau)}{\sum_{u^\prime \in U_B} \exp(\text{sim}(\widetilde{h}_u^t, \widetilde{h}_{u^\prime}^v) / \tau)}, 
  \label{eq:multimodal_alignment_u}
\end{equation}
where $B$ represents the batch size, $U_B$ represents the user set of the batch, $U_B \subseteq U$ and $|U_B|=B$. $\text{sim}(\cdot)$ represents the cosine similarity, and $\tau$ represents the temperature parameter. Similarly, we can obtain the multimodal alignment loss $\mathcal{L}_\text{i}$ of items. Additionally, the multimodal alignment loss $\mathcal{L}_\text{align}$ is obtained by Eq.~(\ref{eq:multimodal_alignment}):
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{align} = \mathcal{L}_\text{u} + \mathcal{L}_\text{i}.
  \label{eq:multimodal_alignment}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Recommendation Loss}}
Existing ID-based MCFRec methods that mostly use Bayesian personalized ranking (BPR) ~\cite{rendle2012bpr} as recommendation loss. However, existing research~\cite{yang2024psl} has found that the Softmax Loss (SL)~\cite{wu2024effectiveness} has better performance than the BPR in practice. Therefore, we use the SL as recommendation loss. The formula of the $\mathcal{L}_\text{rec}$ is as follows:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{rec} = -\frac{1}{B} \sum_{(u,i^+,i^-) \in D_B} \log \frac{\exp(sim(e_u, e_{i^+}) / \tau)}{\exp(sim(e_u, e_{i^-}) / \tau)},
  \label{eq:softmax_loss}
\end{equation}
where $(u,i^+,i^-)$ represents a triplet in a batch training dataset $D_B$. The interacted item $i^+$ is taken as a positive sample, where $\mathbf{R}_{u, i^+}=1$. Conversely, $i^-$ denotes a negative sample and $\mathbf{R}_{u,i^-}=0$. 

To the end, we jointly optimize $\mathcal{L}_\text{rec}$ and $\mathcal{L}_\text{align}$ by Eq.~(\ref{eq:final_loss}) to obtain the ID-free loss $\mathcal{L}$:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{rec} + \mathcal{L}_\text{align}.
  \label{eq:final_loss}
\end{equation}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}
To verify the effectiveness of the proposed ID-free MCFRec baseline IDFREE and further prove our analysis and understanding of ID features, we conduct basic, exploratory, and extended experiments on three public datasets.

\subsection{Datasets}
We conduct experiments on the Amazon review dataset~\cite{he2016ups}. Consistent with~\cite{zhou2023tale, su2024soil}, \textit{Baby}, \textit{Sports and Outdoors}, and \textit{Clothing, Shoes and Jewelry} are selected for experiments, referred to as \textbf{Baby}, \textbf{Sports}, \textbf{Clothing}. The key parameters of the datasets are shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:dataset}. Consistent with existing methods~\cite{yu2023multi, zhou2023tale, su2024soil, xu2024mentor}, we use 4096-dimensional visual pre-extracted features and 384-dimensional textual pre-extracted features.
