\subsection{Basic Experiments on IDFREE}
\subsubsection{\textbf{Analysis of IDFREE Recommendation Performance}}
To verify the recommendation performance of the ID-free MCFRec baseline IDFREE, we conduct a comparative analysis with existing ID-based CFRec methods and ID-based MCFRec methods. Tab.~\ref{tab:performances} shows the performance comparison of different methods on the three datasets.

Overall, IDFREE outperforms all methods on all metrics. Specifically, compared with the current ID-based MCFRec state-of-the-art (SOTA) method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil}, IDFREE achieves an average performance improvement of 82.30\% on the Baby dataset, 60.53\% on the Sports dataset, and 73.89\% on the Clothing dataset. This indicates that ID-free MCFRec baseline IDFREE has significant performance advantages in multimodal recommendation task.

Thoroughly, from the Recall (R@K) and NDCG (N@K) metrics, the performance improvement of IDFREE on the NDCG metric is more significant than that on the Recall metric. This indicates that IDFREE has achieved significant improvements in both recommendation coverage and recommendation quality, but IDFREE can better capture user interests and improve recommendation quality. From K=5 to K=50, IDFREE achieves the best performance on all metrics, indicating that IDFREE has significant performance advantages at different recommendation lengths. However, when the recommendation length is longer, the performance improvement of IDFREE is relatively small. This indicates that there is still room for further improvement in recommendation coverage when recommending large number of items.

Additionally, compared with LightGCN~\cite{he2020lightgcn}, SL~\cite{wu2024effectiveness} brings an average performance improvement of 9.46\%, 11.53\%, and 21.94\% on all metrics of the Baby, Sports, and Clothing datasets, respectively. This indicates that the Softmax loss has more performance advantages than the BPR loss in recommendation task.

In summary, IDFREE has significant performance advantages in multimodal recommendation task and achieves significant improvements in both recommendation coverage and recommendation quality. \textbf{\textit{The ID-free MCFRec baseline IDFREE is extremely effective in multimodal recommendation and has more advantages than the ID-based MCFRec methods.}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Impact of Modules in IDFREE}}
To verify the effectiveness of each module designed for ID-free situation in IDFREE, we conduct module ablation experiment. The results of the ablation experiment are shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:modules}.

In general, the modules in IDFREE all have a significant impact on performance. The average improvements of PE, ASG, AGE, and $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $ on all metrics of all datasets are 53.65\%, 44.58\%, 38.38\%, and 43.25\%, respectively. The PE module in IDFREE has the most significant impact on performance, followed by the ASG, $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $, and AGE modules.

In detail, from different datasets, the PE module has the most significant performance improvement on all datasets. This indicates that the PE module provides effective static features to distinguish different users and items. The roles of the ASG, AGE, and $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $ modules vary across different datasets. On the Baby dataset, the average effective ranking of the modules is ASG, AGE, $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $, while on the Sports and Clothing datasets, the average effective ranking of the modules is $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $, ASG, AGE. Due to the higher density of the Baby dataset than the other two datasets, the performance improvement of the ASG module on the Baby dataset is more significant. For datasets with more users and items and sparser interaction graphs, such as Sports and Clothing, the $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $ can achieve better multimodal alignment. From different values of K, the performance improvement of all modules is most significant when K=5, indicating that the performance improvement of the modules is more significant when the recommendation length is shorter.

Additionally, from the comparison between the third row (using the $\mathbf{R}_U$ and $\mathbf{R}_I$ for user-item graph augmentation) and the fourth row (not using any user-item graph augmentation) in Tab.~\ref{tab:modules}, the augmentation effect of the static $\mathbf{R}_U$ and $\mathbf{R}_I$ is not significant. This further proves the effectiveness of the proposed ASG module. By dynamically generating $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_U$ and $\overline{\mathbf{R}}_I$ based on multimodal features, IDFREE can better capture user interests.

In summary, the PE, ASG, AGE, and $ \mathcal{L}_\text{align} $ modules in IDFREE all significantly improve performance. This indicates that the modules designed for ID-free situation are effective. \textbf{\textit{The ID-free MCFRec baseline can achieve better performance than ID-based MCFRec methods by replacing ID features and effectively modeling multimodal features.}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Impact of Hyperparameters in IDFREE}}\label{sec:hyperparameters}
To verify the impact of the hyperparameters set in this paper on the model performance (with R@20 and N@20 as evaluation metrics), we conducted experiments on different values of the hyperparameters $k$, the number of graph convolution layers $L$, the denoising ratio $\rho$, and the temperature parameter $\tau$ on three datasets.

