  \label{fig:hyperparameters_k_L}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:hyperparameters_k_L} shows the impact of the hyperparameters $k$ and $L$. On the Baby dataset, the performance first increases and then decreases with the increase of $k$, achieving the best result at $k=10$. While on the Sports and Clothing datasets, the best result is achieved at $k=5$. We believe that $k$ needs to be selected according to the specific situation of the dataset. From the impact of the number of graph convolution layers $L$, different from existing studies~\cite{su2024soil,zhou2023tale,wei2019mmgcn,zhou2023enhancing} mostly achieving the best result in 1 or 2 layers. IDFREE achieved the best number of graph convolution layers for R@20 on the Baby, Sports, and Clothing datasets are 15, 4, and 3, respectively. We believe that in the case of fully using multimodal features, the model can better learn rich semantic information in multimodal by more layers of graph convolution. For the Baby dataset, due to the small number of users and items, more graph convolution layers are needed to better learn the relationship between users and items.

\begin{figure}[ht]
