Fig.~\ref{fig:hyperparameters_tau_rho} shows the impact of the hyperparameters $\rho$ and $\tau$. Overall, IDFREE is quite sensitive to $\rho$ and $\tau$. On all datasets, the performance increases with the increase of $\rho$, achieving the best result at $\rho=0.8$. This indicates that a higher denoising ratio is needed in large graphs composed of multiple nodes, consistent with the results in~\cite{zhou2023tale}. With the increase of $\tau$, the performance first increases and then decreases, achieving the best result at $\tau=0.2$. Overall, this temperature parameter is in the low-temperature range, which can better amplify the similarity differences, enabling the model to better complete multimodal alignment and capture user interests in different items.

