  \label{fig:initial_embedding}
\end{figure}

From Fig.~\ref{fig:initial_embedding}, it can be seen that in the three datasets, the distribution area of ID embeddings is generally only about one-fourth of that of textual and visual embeddings, which fully proves that ID-free embeddings are more semantically rich. Meanwhile, from the regularity of the distribution, the ID embedding distribution resembles concentric circles and is very regular, while textual and visual embeddings generally have twisted and uneven distributions. This indicates that ID-free embeddings are more distinctive and can provide different features in personalized recommendation. Furthermore, in the three datasets, the distribution ranges of textual and visual embeddings are basically similar, indicating that the multimodal information processed by IDFREE has a consistent implicit space distribution, which can be better used for subsequent alignment. More specifically, in the Baby and Sports datasets, the distribution of visual embeddings is slightly larger than that of textual embeddings. This aligns with the common knowledge that visual modality information is richer than textual modality information, further indicating that IDFREE retains the differences between the two modalities after processing.

In summary, the ID-free embedding based on multimodal features is more semantically rich than the ID embeddings in ID-based MCFRec methods. \textbf{\textit{IDFREE using ID-free embedding based on multimodal features naturally has better semantic richness than ID-based MCFRec methods.}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Comparison of Multimodal Alignment Effect between IDFREE and ID-based SOTA Method}}
To verify the effectiveness of IDFREE in multimodal alignment, we randomly select 1000 items from each dataset in IDFREE and the ID-based SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil} and visualize the multimodal embeddings using t-SNE.
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{figure6.pdf}
  \caption{Visualization of item textual and visual embeddings in IDFREE and ID-based SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil}.}
  \label{fig:alignment}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:alignment}, the alignment effect in IDFREE is much better than that in SOIL, showing a one-to-one correspondence. Meanwhile, the textual and visual embeddings in IDFREE are distributed extremely uniformly. Although SOIL has some alignment effect, the modality alignment is not precise enough and the distribution is uneven. This indicates that IDFREE has significant performance advantages in multimodal alignment. Overall, \textbf{\textit{IDFREE focuses on the alignment between multimodal features, which is better than ID-based MCFRec methods.}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Comparison of Recommendation Effect of IDFREE and ID-based SOTA Method}}
To further qualitatively verify the effectiveness of IDFREE in recommendation effect, we randomly select 100 users and their interacted items from the three datasets and visualize the user and item embeddings output by IDFREE and the current ID-based MCFRec SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil} using t-SNE.
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{figure7.pdf}
  \caption{Visualization of user and item embeddings in IDFREE and ID-based MCFRec SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil}.}
  \label{fig:fusion}
\end{figure}

In Fig.~\ref{fig:fusion}, the distributions of IDFREE and SOIL on the three datasets are relatively uniform. However, we randomly select a user and connect it with interacted items (shown as red dashed lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:fusion}). The user and item embeddings in IDFREE are closer together on all three datasets. In contrast, the user and item embeddings in SOIL are relatively far apart, with some items causing the user embedding to be biased on the Clothing dataset. This experiment visually demonstrates that the user and item embeddings output by IDFREE are closer together, indicating that IDFREE outperforms SOIL in recommendation effect. In summary, \textbf{\textit{IDFREE achieves better user and item encoding in collaborative filtering recommendation compared to ID-based MCFRec methods by better utilizing multimodal features.}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Comparison of Cost Time and Parameters of IDFREE and ID-based SOTA Method.}}

To verify the advantages of IDFREE in terms of time and parameters, we evaluate the training time, inference time, and parameter count of the current ID-based MCFRec SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil} and IDFREE on the Sports dataset. The results are shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:cost}. The time is the average result per epoch, measured in seconds.

\begin{table}[ht]
  \centering
  \caption{Comparison of time and parameters of IDFREE and ID-based SOTA method SOIL~\cite{su2024soil}.}
  \rowcolors{1}{white}{lightgray}
  \begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
    \hline
    \rowcolor{lightblue}
    Method & Training Time (s) & Inference Time (s) & \#Params \\
    \hline
    SOIL & 10.935 & 6.623 & 85991872 \\
    IDFREE & 13.031 & 7.118 & 82543104 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \label{tab:cost}
\end{table}

From Tab.~\ref{tab:cost}, it can be seen that IDFREE has a smaller parameter count, but the training time and inference time are longer than those of SOIL. From the perspective of parameter count, the longer time is somewhat difficult to explain. Therefore, we further analyze the detailed time consumption of the PE, ASG, and AGE modules in IDFREE during training and inference phases. The results are shown in the following table, with the unit of data in milliseconds.

\begin{table}[ht]
  \centering
  \caption{Detailed time consumption of PE, ASG, and AGE modules in IDFREE.}
  \rowcolors{1}{white}{lightgray}
  \begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
    \hline
    \rowcolor{lightblue}
    Module & Training Time (ms) & Inference Time (ms)  \\
    \hline
    PE & 0.963 & 0.594  \\
    ASG & 2.513 & 48.013  \\
    AGE & 3.105 & 2.827  \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \label{tab:module_cost}
\end{table}

From Tab.~\ref{tab:module_cost}, it can be seen that during the training phase, the AGE module is more time-consuming. We believe this is because the number of layers in LightGCN in IDFREE is higher than the two or one layers in SOIL, which can explain why IDFREE has a longer training time than SOIL. During the inference phase, the ASG module is the most time-consuming. This is because the ASG module is designed to update the user-user graph and item-item graph based on dynamic multimodal embeddings using MLP during the training phase, while during the inference phase, it calculates the true relationships in real-time based on dynamic multimodal embeddings using Eq.~(\ref{eq:user_user_graph}), achieving acceleration during training and better performance during inference.

