\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
This work provides an in-depth analysis of the effectiveness of ID features in multimodal collaborative filtering recommendation. We propose the viewpoint that ID features are effective but have limited benefits in multimodal collaborative filtering recommendation. Furthermore, we argue that ID features suffer from insufficient semantic richness and lead to generalization and representation shift issues. Based on above insights, we propose IDFREE, an ID-free multimodal collaborative recommendation baseline. First, ID-free embedding based on multimodal features and positional encoding to replace ID embeddings. Furthermore, IDFREE achieves modality alignment based on multimodal features and generates dynamic user-user and item-item graphs using adaptive similarity graph module, ultimately enabling effective encoding of users and items under augmented user-item graph. Experimental results demonstrate that IDFREE achieves significant performance advantages on three public datasets. Exploratory and extended experiments further validate the correctness of our viewpoint.

