\begin{proof}
The forward direction is obvious using proof by contradiction. Now we prove the backward direction. Given the uniqueness property, the solution to $\gamma'({S}) = f({S}) \gamma({S})$ and $\gamma(0)=\overline{\gamma}$ takes the form $\gamma({S})=\overline{\gamma}\exp(\int_0^{{S}}fd\omega)$. If $f$ never vanishes, we have either $f>0$ or $f<0$. Thus, $\gamma({S})$ either monotonically increases (given  $f>0$) or monotonically decreases (given $f<0$).

